      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>IIcDOU&amp;&amp;UA'S PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOU II MARES THR BUST PASThY.</head>
        <p>IIcDOU&amp;&amp;UA'S PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOU II MARES THR BUST PASThY.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAI. The tour of the Duke and Duchess of Cornwall and York through Canada. which will be the subjoct of more than one little souvenir book, will help to call at- tention to that country, as a field for the emigration of wcmen. Miss E Lewthwaite, who went out to Wes- tern Canada some years ago, gives an account Of life on a farm in that part of the world, in the October number of the " Fortnightly Review." The want of women friends, and the absence of social and intellectual amusements, seem to be the greatest drawback, but to gentlewomen who are not afraid of work, who can do plain cooking, rough dressmaking, and make themselves generally useful, the life is not without certain attractions. Women are few, and of servants there are scarcely any, bat for those who are possessed of knowledge, adaptive powers, initiative, and general capability, there is ample scope for all who have the ccurage to seek an active life in Western Canada. - The Duke and Duchess of Cornwall and York will leave Halifax for home on the 21st inst. By that time their Royal Hignesses will have spent nearly a month travelling by train, with brief stcppages here and there, in different parts of the Dominion, some of the places visited being already under the winter snow. Every- where. the Duke and Duchess have been received with the greatest enthusiasm, the people collecting from miles around the townships where stoppages have been made, and giving the Royal vieitors a most hearty welcome. These remarkable manifestatiens of loyalty must be sonic compensation to the Duke and Duchess for the tedium at the journey. as, thcugh they are travelling on the meet luxuriously appointed train in the world, it must become not a tittle monotonous when continued week after week. Thee. however, is only one instance of the thoroughness with which their Reyel Hignesses have carried out their Celonial tour, and Lome must be doubly welcome after such a prolonged round ad travel and sighteering. Lady Hellen Stewart, whose engagement to Lord estavordale, has lately been announced, is a very beautiful and accorcplisbe d girl, and an immense favcurite in the very large circle in which she moves. Shine leaving the schoolroom Lady Helen has beF n Lady Londonderry's ccretant companien, accompanying her everywhere. The Marchioness of Londonderry is invari- ably held up as a shining light, when an example of a devoted wile and mother is desired, and deservedly so. Lady Locdonderry is one of those thoroughly good hearted women, who are the very salt of the earth, in whatever station they may be placed. In the Marcbisuess's case, beauty, high rank, and the adora- tion of her mm' date circle have left her unspoiled,and she throws herself with unfailing energy, into good works. Lady Helen Stewart promises to follow very closely in her mother's footsteps, " Lucas Malet," in her new bock, "The History of Sir Riehard Ca lmat? " (Methuen), has drawn for us one of the most beaotiful pictures of a moths.ses love and de- votion to be found in modern fiction, and such an achievement as this is indeed one of which any novelist may be proud. From start to finish one car, but be deeply impressed with " Lucas Malet's " latest work, which tide fair to run very close in public esteem, her well-known book "The wages of sin " published ten years ago. Mrs Harrison, as she is known in private life, ever urges her sisters in art, to keep their standard high in writing,to let money considerations come second only in whatever they writs, and to work on with the Pleasure, and the love of their work as their first object. Unquestionably Lucas Malet carries out,and lives op to, all that she here advises. As it has been a white summer, so it promises to be a white winter tram a modistic point ef view—white serge, zebelline—a smooth-faced cloth with a layer of loneish hairs on the surface, and rough frieze, trimmed with bands cf white or brown fur, are being utilised by the leading modistes for in and out-deor teilettes, while for evening, white satin brccade, chiffon and crime will , he extensively patrcnised by society women. Next in favour after white, will come black, with much glitter about it. To the impecunious, and there are many such, black proves a blessing alvvaye. There is no ques- tion of "matching," and if one has to make one's frocka last out a considerable time such a state of affairs is lees noticeable than when colours are employed. Creamy white Persian lamb, seen- s likely to enjoy a wide vogue this coming winter. It appeats already on many a white cloth coat, and needless to say the effect leaves nothing to be desired, even by the most fastid- ious. The majority of the new fichu-rues, built of ci ape or site, are being edged with a narrow band of white fur, or swansdown. the latter perhaps being Pre- ferable, on account of its extreme lightness. Very smart these fichus look and most useful will they prove, to throw over a coat that has perchance seen its best I days. There is but one item to seriously consider, in connection with I hese fur edged ticbus, and that is the probebility that later the masses will appreciate them all too well. Those among us who propoee investing in a new winter 'coat should wait awhile ere purchasing the same. The loose-backed variety is to go, about this there is no possible doubt. The newest medals lye quite tightly-fitting, and at the back the hasque 'is bag, while in front it slopes away, like a man's morn- log coat. Pockets appear on the hips, tut they are for ornament, not for use. Every thing this winter is to match in co'cur. Gown, ccat, blouse, gloves, bat, all must accord, or at any rate correspond in shade. This item should net be overlooked when sefecling a winter coat. No ore needs to be told that there are people who wash too little, but the " Lancet " making a slight variation from ite many warnings against the dangers of life, says that there are people who wash too much. With the best of soaps the application of water three or fcur times a day tends to roughen [the skin, and except once in the morning, and afternoon, the " Lancet" recommends that only a soft dry towel should be used for rubbing the face. Many women never use soap for their face, but the water, as well as the soap is squally cordemced when used too frequently Most of the old English custcms associated with Mict telmas are gradually dying out. incloding that of eating goose on Mict wlenas Day. In olden times this custom was almost as universal as feasting at Christ- mas, but like the history of many other ancient prac- tices, its origin is highly obscure. Some say that Queen El'zabeth was dining rff goose wben she heard of the defeat of the Spanish Asmada, and afterwsrds con- tinued this practice on MietWmas Day, in grateful remembrance of the British victory. Other accounts associate the payment of a " Michael- , mas Goose " with old material custems of land tenure, and this is no doubt nearer the truth. But in all pro- bability the fact that geese are at their best at this time of the year has a good deal to do witOt.lreland supplies a large proporticn of our home-grown geese, but it is well known that there are none equal to those that come from Norfolk. EIIAIVRCC[ 11. PUDDING.—Make a custard with one , pint of milk and two eggs (sugar to taste), and stiffer) it with half an ounce of white leaf gelatine. Pour it while het, on to three ounces of stale sponge cake crumbs and leave until cold. Then, half fill with the mixture a china mould and press a small jam pot into the middle. When set, turn out, and Bel with stewed (cold) fruit. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER:</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER:</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>For the autumn rather large hats with very low crowns in soft felts, sometimes smooth, sometimes hairy, are in vogue. Black felts, trimmed with chenille ruching and big nhoux of bright colour, are smart as well as useful, as are those of tricorn shape with a large bcw of glace ribbon and a waved quill in front. Feather toques seem likely to be a good deal worn, and some of the daintiest have crowns of greeny-blue plumage and brims of swathed chiffon. Scarves of spotted foulard are still popular trimmings, but large and full ostrich fa".thers sweeping round the brim are undoubtedly considered the most desirable adornment at the moment. The littie bolero is more worn than any (-tiler jacket during this early autumn season, but, judging by appearances, the close-fitting three- quarter-length coat will be the modish garment when colder weather comes. This type of jacket is very smart, indeed, when, as is often the case, it has broad revers, pocket-flaps and cuffs of em- broidered silk or satin, edged with fur. A coat of this shape looks particularly well in tan box-cloth with collar, pocket-flaps, and gauntlet cuffs of white satin ornamented with gold embroidery and edged wih sable. A full lace cravat provides an essential finish to the coat.</p>
        <p>Ii</p>
        <p>On a dark blue serge there is a flat, round collar in pale blue satin, with string-coloured braid sewn clesely in lines. The belt is made of satin and braid to match, and the plain sleeves ace turned back in small cuffs, also in satin and braid. Tweeds in shades of purple, mauve, or amethyst are made with white cloth collars, revers, and cuffs, much strapped with black silk, satin, or moire. Black or grey cloth skirts are much tucked. Three flounces are occasionally seen, and almost always looks smart. The dressmaker has to hit the golden mean between meagreness and over-fulness in these flounces. They suit only tall, slim figures. A grey cloth dress made in this way has all three flounces bordered with deep cream coloured guipure, and the top of the uppermost flounce has a hue of similar guipure.</p>
        <p>4</p>
        <p>• ••,,</p>
        <p>though the midday sun is, it is quite time think of autumn frocks and other reasonable garments, and it is wise to look to the seams of our umbrellas, and find out if they are weather-proof. After the record summer we have had we are likely to have a rainy autumn, and in the list of things to be bought, the wise will include stout-soled boots and shoes. Many a chill is caught through wear- ing thin shoes in damp weatner, aud "chill" is nowadays a fearsome word. It is a mistake, in choosing autumn gowns, to select very warm matetials, for the results of becoming over-heated in the walk are often suite as serious as any that could arise from being too thinly clad. Very few people have the resolution to keep on their out- door things for awhile for fear of growing cool too suickly. It is pleasant to throw them off, and moat of us do what is pleasant rather than what is wise.</p>
        <p>—sr that sealskin is to return to fashion again this winter, especially in conjunction with other furs. It will be used for bands and trimmings to cloth costumes, and also on hats. It is merely another instance of the fickleness of fashion that sealskin has had no great following of late, for nothing looks more comfortable. In Russian blouse form, with vest and belt of old brocade or embroidery, sealskin looks perhaps better than any other fur, unless it be sable. A pretty dress in brown cloth had the skirt trimmed with rounded bands of sable, caught up like garlands at intervals with the head of a small sable, and the bolero bodice trimmed in the same manner, a deep vieux rose Empre bolt showing at the waist. However, the fashion for whole animals, heads and paws in- cluded, as necklets and boas, seems to be dying out, and though fox will be fashionable for revers and vests we are not likely to see much of it, and its imitations, as complete boas, on well. dressed people.</p>
        <p>A very noticeable point is being made of threaded effects upon the silken blouses and cloth dresses of earliest autumn, and much more stress is being</p>
        <p>laid than hitherto has been the case upon ;the highly decorative aspect of the eyelet holes through which the velvet or riLbon is passed. The said holes are rendered conspicuous by an overlay of gold tissue elaborately embroidered, or some other form of decoration as striking. Girls who snake their own shirts should exploit the service- able merits of a good black silk model covered with tiny tucks, and beneath the black lace and satin chemisette should adorn with gold the eyelet holes through which gold tissue braid or black satin ribbon might be passed. A gold tissue belt .4, would fitly complete the scherae. Year loving _ Jinurns THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
