      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>SUPPOSED RAILWAY SUICIDE.</head>
        <p>SUPPOSED RAILWAY SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>Widnes man found shot in a Train. On the arrival on Saturday night of a train from Greenfield at Clegg-street Station, Oldham, the bcdy of a man was fcund in a first-class compartment, with the muzzle of a six-chambered revolver against the roof of his mouth and his arm resting on a ledge. The body was warm, and the revolver, one of the chambers of which had been discharged, was clenched in his hand. His clothes was covered with hired.</p>
        <p>The police had the body removed to the Rhodes Bsnk Mortuary. In a pocket was fsund a first-class ticket from Greenfield to Stalybridge. On Sundar night a brother of the deceased, Robert Bcnshall, of Knutsford-road, Warrington. identified the body as that of George Henshali, marine store dealer, lodging at the house of Mrs. Leach, 56, Oakland-street, Widnes. lhe deceased was a single man, age 32, and is supposed to have suffered from depre,sion of mind Be had been away from Widnes since Thursday morn' ing last, and his landlady stares that she believed him to be in lids usual state of bean and that be had gone away on a business journey. The deceased was the manager of tbe Widnes Marine Stores Company's shop in Mersey-road. The Inquest. An inquest was held on Monday at Crdham on the booty ot Life deeps.. d. Robert Henshall provision dea'er, Knutsford-road, Warrington, said deceased was his brother, and he last saw him alive about a month ago. He had been a sufferer from melancholia, presumably through business failures. Thomas Smith,guard 52, Fatr-qtreet,Stockport.aaid be had charge of the 8.25 train from Wudderstield ta Oldham Lpon arrival there he saw deceased with his face down- wards to the floor, with the barrel of a revolver in his mouth. There was no semblance of a struggle having taken place, and in his opinion thought he was only asleep.</p>
        <p>Two other witteeses were called, including Police Constable Franks, wrn ;aid he found in the man'a roe- eereicn a railway ticket and 9itd in money and other articles.</p>
        <p>A verdict of Suicide whilstetcrnperarily insane</p>
