      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Town Council was held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall, on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Alderman Roberts) presided and there wcre also present :Aldermen Heaton,Sutton. Shaw Green,Monks, Hutchinson, Bolton, Councillors Ellison, John Evans, James Evans, W Brough. F R Roberts, William Roberts, Shillcock. Baird, Ildalkin, DlVin°, Pea— cock, Wilkinson; Smethurst, Dancan, A Jolleg, Tinnion, Burton, John Roherts, Bennett, A J Hulse, with Messrs J Lyon Whittle (town elerl), S Wilson (deputy town clerk), T Longdin (borough surveyor), J Fair- hurst (borough treasurer), Dr J Guest Gornall (Medical Officer of Health), J Dees (water engineer), A M Kr, (deputy borough survoyor),W SHadd,,clt (gasengineer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>LITE EMPRESS FREDERICK.</head>
        <p>THE LITE EMPRESS FREDERICK.</p>
        <p>- The Mayor read the followinv letter rEfOnle Office, Wnitehall, 10th September, 1901. Sir,—l have bad the honour to lay before the Bing the local and dutiful resolution ef the rown Council of the borough of Warrington .xoressing condolence with his Mijesty and the Royal FAmily on the death of her Imperial Majesty the Dowager Emnress Frederick of Germany, and I have to inform you that his Majosty wag pleased to receive the same wry Erniouglc.—l am, sir, your obedient aervant, CHARLES T. RITCHIE. The Town Clerk of Warrington.</p>
        <p>_ WATCH COMMITTEE. TIM NEW POLICE BUILDINGS. At a Meeting of the above committee it was resolved that at the suggestion of the architect the amount of the retention ironer under the contract for the build- in vof the new Police Premises be reduced to 10 per cent. Tenders were opened for fly* furnishing of the new police premises, and the tenders nf the fnllowing firms accepted.where lowest,for the several articles required: Messrs Garnett and Sons, Messrs J M Greenhalgh and Co., Mr Ralph Johnson. The MAYOR, in moving the adoption of the minutes, referring to the reduction of the retention money for the building of the new police premises, explained that in accordance with the terms of the contract a certain proportion of the amount due be the builder during the pr,gress of the contract had been retained. This now amounted to a very large sum indeed and the architects considered that it was far in excess of what was in any way necessary to secure the conditions of the con•ract. Having had the fioires before them, the committee agreed to their suggestion to T.clune the mcnoy to tee pe, cent. There would still he left in hand a sum considerably beyond four figures and very much in excess of what was necessary to ensure the comple- tion of the contract. Alderman BEATON, seconded, and the minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>Tin LYME-STREET CORNER. INOTEIER EFFORT TO GE r IT IMPROVED . The Finance and Estate Commitree held % meeting. on the 2 id September, when thecornmittee having considered the petition of ratepayers in flornernarket-atreet and •ha resolution of the Street Improvement and Lighting Committee with reference to the pulling down of the premises at the corner of LYme-street and Florsemarket- srreet, it was resolved that the committee are of opinion that before anything is done he S-reet Improvement and Lighting Committee be requested to obtain a report upon the probable cost of widening Gyrne-ttreetthromth- nit, and also to state how anon they can see their way clear to carry out the scheme, anti RS an alternative scheme the cutting off the projection and s-tting the trellises hack lineable with Shelton's and the adjoining property in Florsemarkerearreet The Tewn Clerk, at meeting of the same committee. on the 16 h S tratemher, tail before tie members the following letter from th Local Government Board and the sanctions referred to therein am directed by the Local Govern- ment Board to state that they have under consideration the report made by their Inapec'or, Dr Mantic. after the inquiry made by him with refer.pce to 'he application of the Town Council nf We.rio,tnn fr sanction to •he hot•rowing of the Fulls of £9 979*(inw increased to 2 0 1291 for the provision of a simallneur hospital, and 23 702 for the extensian of the fever hospital. The oard have decided to coennly with the +optic ition,and their forrntl sanction and approval are forwarded to yon herewith —Ymr ehNiient ce•wan`, J9IIN saristant secretary. The Towo Clerk. Alderman Sllkllr GREEN moved the confirmation of the minutes. and Alderman MONIES seconder!. C Mtlllllol. SI\IRTIII7R2T alind A to 'he notkion w.th yeoarri to the rrillioe• down ef the nremites at the corner tof Lvme-,treet and Florsernark,t-,treet. and asked why it had not been procented to tho Council. The petition, he believed. wee addresited to the Mayer. aldermer and nonncillori of the berough, and not to corn. rnittee. It came from something like 70 or 80 rate- payers in the Town Uttl W ird, including the leading people of the district, He dil not treelerstand why the petition had net been presented to the Council as inten_ anl before the commi, tee's reso'utinn was passed 'he wou'd salt for the nrodriction of the petition. The VlAtnn said he did riot RIITSMOSP there would he any ohisetion. but he did not fie. how they could deal With it after they had received it. Toe petition was 'there and could bn put on the table it it were wiahel. In any case aft,. th, itincil had etinailered it ha nre- cMtned theV would refer it to the S',,Pf‘i* Improvement; Committee for a report as had been done. The corn. Mittee had rceor.ed in favrur of the prayer of the petition as he rlnderet^o4 I. Couocillor SnETIMRST Fialo that anyone reading 1/"N'n the linoq would under itand q lite well what was meant by the resolgq^n• A Mcmriv.n : Will shelve it. Councillor Wltuaai ROEERTi said he thought it was 'Posse/ that this particular rower should be mailed down some twelve montha a7r, by the Cflaine,il• He did not know why after a rr,ointion like that it should be in the power of any commi•tee to alter it. Anyone conversant with the main atreata knew nerfectiv well that the hideous looking hlnelt at the corner of LVme- strcet was a source of very great danger to the com- munity. The MAYOR said they need not on into the general -question The queetion befere the Council was that a petition had been resented to the Cconcil in favour of the taking down Of the propertv. and in ad lition to that he found that in April. 1899. n resolution was flossed by the Council requesting the Finance aed E tate Committee to pull the property down. It s.emed to him that the Council was quite entitled to an esplans- don from the Finance Com hittee as to why they hal not ntrried out that request. Such a question would cr,m, within the scope of the minutes if it were desired to raise it. Tnn general q aestion of improvement or otherwise was past discussion bnause it had been settled. C mnellor JAMES EvANS said that the Street Improve- ment Committee hal more than once lIRRePd A Teßniti- lion that this property should be taken down. That had been approved by the Council. The MAYOR said they had only cepa to the Fxterkt of approving a recommendation. It seemed to him they ought to Co farther, and give a distinct inierncticn to the Finance Committee to take the property down, and he did not think the committee would hesitate to carry out the instruction. Councillor JAMEI EVANS said this was purely an PvtpiOn of the epirit of the resolution pass.d by the tS re-‘t Improvement Committee, and, reading between the lines, meant the putting off to a further date the taking down of the propene. He moved as an amend- ment that the minutes be referred back to the commit- tee ; and in the meantime he had no cloat they would get a strong and emnhatic resolution from the Street Improvement Committee to the effect that the property must: be taken down. The Finance Committee knew as well as the Street Improvement Committee that there was pronerty in Lyme-street which could not he acquired just now, and they knew too that this resolu- tion conld not be carried out until that property was acquired. He moved an amendment that the minute be referred barn. Councillor BENNETT asked for the re olution passed in 1899. The MAY said it was passed by the Street Improve- ment Committee in Anril 1829. It was to the effect that the Finance and Estat,-, Comm tee he requested to pull down the property at the corner of Lyme-street for the purpose of widening the thorouchfaree. That resolution, he said, was adopted and confirmed by the Council. He also read the res-lution of the committee of the 3iid September to the effect that the petition be referred to the Finance Committee, with an expression ,t-.f opinion that the property should be taken down.</p>
