        <head>FEJ,YECTICN IS TOUCHED AT ALL POINTS EY</head>
        <p>FEJ,YECTICN IS TOUCHED AT ALL POINTS EY</p>
        <p>90, 4 • 6•••' g.m,l ‘ll6 r Frsc, " t 71,47 273 g, hk-A. . ' t24\4' .4;1 4`.i'l\t ra w&amp; ad:auk</p>
        <p>That resolution was also adopted by the Council, but neither of them went beyond a request t ) the Finance Committee to deal with the matter. The Council had act yet directly given instructions to that effect. Councillor Satt,Lcock seconded the amendment. Alderman MONKS said Councillor J&amp;ITV-S EV&amp;O3 eVi— dently kt.ew more of the details of this matter than the Finance Committee. Members might be quite sure that the Finance Committee has reasons for ue.ferring pull- ing down this property. Nothing has been said and nothing need be said, but it was evident to the minds of members where the motive power in regard to this matter really came from. There were d ,tails in eon. nection with this matter at the present time which it was not advisable to make public, an I much as he would like he Could I.IOG now. say what he had in his mind, merely because it would rather prejudice the case and weaken their hands in dealing with it. Tney would lose £l5O a year at least by taking down this property and in •-,helr present condition of Affairs, that was not to be undertaken lightly. There was the further quest- tioa that even if the property did come down it would - not remove the block in the street, because they could not touch the narrowest part of the street. Alderman 8A:co:4 said the matter was simply one of ' finance. It was a question where £l5O a year should be sacrificed for an,improvement to the street. Alderman HurcniNsoN supported the aclon of the committee, remarking that at the present time £l5O a year was a serious sum of money to lose. CounCillor TINNION also spoke in support of the corn- naittee's recommendation. There were negotiations going on and to take action now might affect the matter of compewicion, Councillor A. J. JOLLEY repudiated the idea that the committee hail not been perfectly frank and straight- forward in this matter. He did not think anything could be gained by postponement.</p>
        <p>Alderman SHAW GREEN, in reply, said that Alderman Moults and Councillor Jolley had expressed the matter to his satislae,tioa. If the property Came down h; must come down as part of a proper scheme. They would ask the Street Improvement Committee to give them an estimate of the cost of wPening Lyme-street through- out. The Committee was willing to carry out any direct resolution of the Council, but very important financial considerations depended upon what was done with their property. Too alternative to widening the street was to take a slice from the corner proper:y. In nis opinion this was not the worst part of Horsemarket- I street. That portion between M3ssre Milling's and Mr I Leigh's shop was far worse and traffic there was more congested. vote was taken, and the amendment was lost by 12 votes to 9. The minute 3 were then confirmed. STREET IMPROVEMENT AND -LIGEITINiI COMMI TTEE, THE PARK IMPROVEMENT. ---</p>
        <p>At a meeting or the Street Improvement and Lighting Coinrnittee it was resolved tuat the tender of Messrs Picksley, Sims and Company to supply and erect orns- m,ntal railings aed gates for the Park at the corner of Kendrick-street and Cm 03 field-street at £207 103 100 ccep :ed. At the same meeting it was resolved that the tender of Ilttsira S T Ward awl Co. for the taking down of the stone walt at the frent of St. James' old burial ground and the removal of the gravestones frcm that around and the re.erection thereof at the Warrington Cemetery at £4O be s ccepted. Alderman BUTTON moved the confirmation of the minutes. Toe motion was Pconded by Councillor HEI,TNETTAnd agreed to. THE NEW MEDICAL OFFICKR AND THE PREVENTION OF TUBERCULOSIS. .</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Health Committee it was resolved that Dr John Guest Gornall be appointed medical officer of health for the borough, that he be paid a salary of £5OO for his eervices as medical officer of health, medical superintendent of theh f if ..osp.tal for _n_ect.ous iiseases, and public analyst, that he do not engage la private practice. and that three mouths no:ice be re- quired to terminate the appointment.'‘ Toe Medical , Officer of Health was authorised to take such adminis- trative measures as he may deem necessary with regard to the prevention of tuberculosis, and it was resolved Lhat the erection of s new pavilion at the infectious diseases hospital, together with alterations of the exist- ing buildings, be proceeded with, and the Bnough surveyor was instructed to have the tender amended leaving out the erection of the administrative block. It was further decided that the members of the m.dical profession in the borough be requei,tsd to notify such cases of phtbisis as in tneir opini4n it is desirable, and that the following scale of fees be adopted .—For each report of a case not previously reported, when reported by a private medical attendant, 2s 61 ; for each report of a case previously</p>
        <p>reported from another source, is ; for each report of a change of address of a prottrisicat address, the new address being reported, is ; for each fresh case reported from a public institution, is ;for each change of address reported from a public ins ituzion, tne new address being reported, is, Also that tlis committee record with regret the loss whilla the Corporation have sus- tained by the death of Ingleector Norman, and their appreciation of the manner in vvhica he fulfilled the duties of inspector of nuisances during a peried of 25 years and that Mr Walter I Flood ,one of the assistant in- spectors of nuisances, be appointed chief inspector of nuisances for a period of three months at a remunera- tion to be hereafter fixed. Alderman Stereos, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, intimated that D: Gornall was to give up all private practice. The Local I-al/ammo/It Board would provide half the s teary, £253. It was felt that the doctor r4laould have a free hand in dealing with tuber- culosis. There was an error in one of the minutes which referred to the new pavilion at the infectious diseases ,hosnital. II should be the smallpox hespital. The Mayon seconded, and said he was sorry to have to correct a little error in regard to Alderman Siitloe's statement abut the payment of the Medical Officer's Salary. It was quite true they had been receiving a contribution from the caunty suthorities, but a; War- rington bad become a county borough, and was severed from the admieistrative county, the amount they had been receiving would be c smmuted and taken into account in the filen:jai adjustment as b:tween the two bodies. Whilst it was correct to say the etfeet was tle same, they received the capital aid ia the first instance and the amount of their exchequer grant was propor- tionately larger he the amount involved. 'There was another error of the Alderman's. (Laughter.) I; was i agreed, after grave consideration, and a due sense of their responsibility. t) call the new eetablishment at Sankey a sanatorium and not a smallpox hospital. (Laughter.) The MAYOR afro alluded to Ntr Flood and said the arrangement W 99 teutativ r. They were, however, all extremely hopeful that he weuid be found equal to the appeintment. vv•?re anxious to make it possib'e for their own led; to begin at th 3 bottom a sel get to the top. (Hear, hear.) TOP minutes were passed. WOOD PAVING IN FRONI' OF PLA.CES OF .WORSHIP. Alderman HEaTON proposed the minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Committee and alluded to the resolution— That the Surveyor be instructed to report upon the cost of paving in front of all places of religion in the borough with wood "—said it was not their iuteratim to Se pave the street exeept it was being taken up for some purpose, or was le ring paved for tie first time. They would not take up a street already.paved and pave it with wood. .</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION seconded. 4S Councillor F R ROBERTS said he could not understand the conduct of the committee with regard co this wood paying in front of places of worship. Ile had noticed in passing the new Catholic Chapel in Latchford that the street fronting the building 1:948 paved with wood . blocks and th side street paced with granite setts, and the side was about 3 times the length of the front- age. It seemed rather absurd, if the idea was to lessen the noise of the traffic. Cmacillor TINNI)N said the committet were anxious to d, all they could to increase the comfort of wor- shippers, but the committee had bad a report from the Suryeror, which state.d that the cost of wood-pavinu in front of olaces of worship in the town would be about £15,000. (" Od.") The committee hid not thought the coat would to anything like what he had stated. For this fins ncial year at any rate they could not take the matter in hand. (Bear, hear). The vary hic:h price of the wood books hsd to be takm into considera- tion. They were up to 25 per cent, more than they were two years ago.. The minutes were passed. SCHOOL ATIENDS.NCE COMMITTEE—LIBOUR</p>
        <p>September, 1901. Sir,—With reference to your letter of the 30 July last, oo the eubject of the table of fees proposed by the Town Council to be mad payabl to ministers and sextons under Section 3 (1) of the Burial Act,1900,1 am directed by the Secretary of State to say that he is prepared to approve the tees for ministers but as regards the suggested fees to the sextons he has power to approve fees for services rendered only; and that if the sextons have not rendered and do not pro- pose to render any actual ssrvices at interments, in person or by deputy, their existing fees seem to be abolished, suhjCt to compensation as provided in the said Act.—l am, sir, your obedient servant, CaastEs S MURDOCH. The Town Clerk of Warrington. It was resolved that the Town Cierk be instructed to arrange terms of compensation to be paid to the clerks and sextons on their offices being abolished. Councillor Jorrx Evass, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, said there was nothing which called for comment except the letter fiom the Horne Offiv, which the members were very glad to receive, because it cleared the way for a settlement of the claims of clerks and sextons. Councillor DONt&amp;N seconded, and the motion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>Loot! Government Board EXTENSION OF TEE BATHS. At a meeting of the Town Hall, Park and Baths rCeoomeittdtettotshaenTotoiownn of ltebrok reported tat he had to the borrowing of the Ruin of £3,067 for the extension to of the public baths, and it was resolved that the altera- tion of the batas according the amended plans be proceeded with. Alderman BOur.)N proposed that the minates of the committee be passe&amp;—Covinciilor Somuonar seconded, and the motion was avroed to. THE GAS COYIMITTEE AND THE STREETS - At a meeting of the Gas Committee, held on the 30 di Auguu, the engineer complained that be had not e received an 7 notice from Mr Ewan as to where and only find n hnedporooptos.beyi oopoenrasitnagnettliv watching the work. Tie streets, and that he could sioggested that it would facilitate the work of the department if the Tramways Committee informed him from time to time the portion of the road it is proposed to take up, and the exact track for the trim rails. It was resolved that the engineer be requested to meet the tramways clerk of works and plum; out to him the position of the gas mains and agree as to the best means of carrying out any alterations to the mains which may be deemed necessary. At a meeting on the 13th September the following resolution of the Tram- ways Committee was read and agreed to : " That the Gas Committee be requested to allow the removal of part 61 the gas offices at the corner of Lower Bank- street and Mersyy-street to effect an improved entrarme to the proposed car sled, and . that the engineer be requested to submit a sketch of the proposed alterations " Alderman HEATON moved the confirmation of the minutes. which, be said, explained themselves. All the milters referred to were in progress. He hoped they would he able to come to terms with the Tram- ways Committee. Councillor JOHN ROBERTS seconded.--Carried. RIVERS COMMITTEE. INSUFFICIENT WATER IN THE RIVER. At a meeting of the above committee, a letter war read from the Ma.r.chester and Liverpool Transport Company complaining of the " insuril:dent water in the river," and asking the Corporation to compel the. Ship Canal Company to dredge to the necessary depth. It was resolved, that the Town Clerk inform the corn- , pany that notice requiring the Manchester Ship Canal Company to dredge the river had been served upon the company. , The minutes were passed, on the motion of the I MAYOR, seconded by Alderman SurroN. THE APPLETON WATER SUPPLY.'</p>
        <p>yours received this morning, with Cony, of resolution by your Couacil giving this Council 255 square yards of land in the above lane conditionally. My Council this afternoon have directed me to reply thanking you for your consideration, and I am instructed to lay suitable paths on both sides of the road when the improvement be carried out. —Yours faithfully. B. S. Newcouno, J. Lyon Whittle, Eq. 1.1 was resolved that the Wooer of 1 Messrs James Simpson and Company,Limited, to supply 1 the steam engine and shafting for driving a dynamo, and various machine tools and dynamo with the neces- sary wires aia 1 fittings for electric lighting at Del ph- lane pumping , station, according to drawings and specifications. at £525, be accepted. Councillor JOHN EVANS proposed the confirmation of the minutes. He said that although there were several important matter under consideration he taought they explained themselves. Alderman BOLTON seconded. Councillor BENNETT alluded to the resolution about the strip of land in Dtapa-lane whin is was proposed to give to the Rural I 4 strict Council. He said he was not quite clear why the Corporation should give land to that oody. No one appeared to be inclined to give anything to tee Corporation, and he thought they were in a pot- , Lion to ask the Rural District Council to pay for their 1 public improvements. There might, however, be circumstances connected with the benefaction of which he was not aware. They were, on the face of the resolu- tion not very obvious. COuncillor JOHN EVANS replied that if Councillor Bennett would read the reolution he would see what the object was. The District Council had to make a suitable footpath to the satisfaction of the Engineer. It was necessary to have this width of road in order to have a coovenient entranca to the works. Councillor TINNION alluded to the resolution with reference to the preparation of plans for the new reser- -1 I voir at Darestuary.and asked how far the Council wound be committed if it was agreed to.. He took it that what was prposed was only a sort of preliminary proceeding If he voted for the resoletion it would be on the dis- tinct understanding he had named. He did not wish to be told that having agreed to the preparation of plans 1 the Council had sanctioned the whole scheme.</p>
        <p>Toe MAYOR : You will bs absolutely uncommitted. The Council :yin be free to take any action it likes. All that the resolution involves is the necessary Excondituro for plans and nothing more. Con- firming, he said he hoped the Council, when the time came to discuss the matter, would not be blind to to the fact that manufacturers required water quite as much as other people did for cl-nnestic purposes. Unless the Corporation could supply good water tor manufactur- ing purposes they might shut the door to a further ex- tension ot manufactures He spoke now with an important experience derived lately. Alderman Suaw GREEN suggested that when the matter was before the Council two years ago the Council then definitely committed itself to the Daresbury scheme and the Council obtained from the ratepayers a alandate to carry it forward. I The MAYOR : You are right as far as you co, but Councillor Tinnion will be within his rights in discussing any proposal to carry out the work. Alderman MONKS : Are we compelled to supply water to manutac!urers ? The MAYOR : No, not in the earnn way a s we are com- pelled to supply water for domestic purposes. It 33 simply a question of policy. The minutes were then agreed to. FURTHER TRAMWAY DEVELOPMENTS. CARS TO BE PURCEIASED. THE WARRINGTON AND NOR THWICH LIGHT RAILWAY. At a meeting of the Tramways Committee it was resolved that the Gas Committee be requested to allow the removal of part of the Gas Offices at the corner of Lower Backstreet and Mersey-street to eff et an improved entrance to the prsposed car shed, and that the engineers be requested to submit a sketch of the proposed alterations. A letter was read from the eas engineer denying that he was responsible for the delay in the construction of the tramways, in answer to the resolution on page 337. It was decided that the atten- tion of the engieeers he called to the unsatisfactory Paving of Winmsrleigh-ltreet. A letter was read from the solicitors to the Warrington and Northwich Light Railway Order, stating that the promoters had not accepted the condition of the payment to the Manches- ter ship Cavil Company of the easement for the swing bridges at s,tpckton Hnath and Boutsford-road. At a meeting on the 18th September it was resolved that the tender of the British Thomson Houston Company, for eight trara cars at £5,058 15s, be accepted on condition i that the car bodies are provided with A " Birkenhead or " Exhibition " staircase, double sliding doors, iro° scroll work in the handrail, and other irnprovementa introduced by Messrs Milne and Company in the new cars supplied to the Birkenhead Corporation. Messrs Preece and Cardew submitted an estimate of the Cost of</p>
        <p>nstru s, i equipping tramways Nos. 4,5, and .0 and it was resolved that toe engineers ba instructed advertise for tenders for the above work. The draw- ings were submitted by Messrs Blackwell and ComnanY, Limited, of coat of arms and monogram for base and external view of junction box, wnen it was resolved that the drawings b 3 approved, subj -et to the words " Corporation of Warrington" bsing substituted for the monogram, and that Cooper and Howard Smith's anchorj dnt be used in the construction of the Waders- pool Causeway line (instead of sole phs. es) at an extra cost of £l7O It was also resolved that the roadway between the lines on Wilderspool Causeway be asphalted. The MAYOR moved, and Alderman SUIT IN seconded, the adoption of the minutes. Alderman MONKS alluded to the question of the cars, to which he called attention at the last meeting of tie Council. He said that subsf spent events had proved the wisdom of deferring the matter, because the report which had been given to him turned oat to be rather unfounded. There was no reason why the order should not be placed on the lines originally inten led. Alderman Elnanon called attention to the last recom- mendation of the Committee, and said he was not aware that the Paving and Sewerage Committee had been dis- citarned from their duty of deciding what a street should be paved with. The MAYOR : In your capacity as a member of the Tramways Committee you must talk to them (Laughter.) Alderman HEAT )74 Toe resolution of the Tramways Committee came at a time when we had decided to do it. The MAYOR: It is an instance of great minds thiak- ing alike. (Laughter.) Cooncillor TINNION said the Paving and Sewerage Committee was responsible to the Council for the paving of the streets and he did not, admit th 3 principle that the Tramways Committee had any rignt to tell them how they should pave a Street. Councillor Evans : I believe the recommendation c rine from the Paving and S fwerage Committee. It was decided to alter the resolutieu of the Com- mittee to read "that it be a recommendation to the Paving and Sewerage Committee," etc. The minutes were then passed. THE, DEFICIENT SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION. COUNCIL'S AC nos. COUNCILLOR BENNETT AND THE CENSUS RETURNS. The following important letter from the Board of E location was read at a meeting of the General Pur- poses Committee on September 23rd :—" Whitehall, London, S.W., 11th September. Sir,-1 am directed to inform you that the Board of Education have under consideration the question of the provision of adequate public elementary school accommodation in Warrington They propose to issue a notice under Section 9 of the Venicatary Education Act, 1370, calling for the pro- visi In of ndditional accommodation for 250 bus, 250 girls, and 300 infants in the Marsh House-lane district, and for 150 infants in Be vosey-road district, and for 100 and 150 infants near St Barnabas School acid St James (Latchford) Church respectively. Before issuing the notice, they will be glad to consider anv observations tour c immittee may wish to offer on the matter.—l have the honour to be, sir, your cb hEent servant, JOYIN WRITE. J L Wnittle, Esq., Clerk to the Borough School Attendance Committee, Warrington." It was resolved that the Chairman of the School Attend- ance Committee be requested to call a meeting of the influential ratepayers of Warrington to discuss the above matter. The committee approved of the suggestion :from the Technical Instruction Sub-committee that a , " time "light be fl.sted at one minute to ten each night, and undertook to mils:: the necessary arrange- ments and provide the energy in connection with the clock now being specially made for the New Technical School building. Leftern were read from James Fair- dough and Sees, Ltd, and this Manchester and Liver pool Transport. Company, objecting to the proposed widening of Warrington Bridge by means of cantilevers. The following telegram to the American Minister on the assassination of President M'Kinley was read :—" In- ha.bitints of Warrington join me in expression of deepest sympathy with American nation in present terrible distress.—RonEnns, Mayor, Warrington." The Maxon moved ton confirmation of the minutes, and said the Town Clerk had suggested to him that he should make an explanation as to the resolution referring to the mi.tter of the threatened School Board for Warrington. It was noasidered desirable for important reasons that the Chairman of the School Attendance Committee should defer calling the meeting for a short time. The instruction of the Committee would, how- ever, remain on the minutes. The Chairman would exercise his discretion during the next few weeks, as to the date of the meeting. Information bad come into his possession which be was not at present in a position to disclose. Alderman SHAW GREEN sec coded and called attention to the minute with reference to the time light. It was hoped by means of this light that they would be able to let the inhabitants know when .it was exactly one minute to ten every evening. It had not been decided a here the light should be flishel, but there was a general impression that Trinity Church tower would he a suitable place for the purpose. A strong electric light would be shown and this vonuld enable every rate- payer to consult his watch and decide the exact time. Councillor BENNETT alluded to the census returns,and remarked that WarringtmenjoYed an unique distinction as sts.contained more males than femates, the males nuniberine 32,323 and the females 31,919. (Laughter.) 'That would no doubt be '1 tidings of comfort and joy " tn the unmarried females in the rest of the United Kingdom. (Renewed laughter.) The MAYOR : In that respect Warrington is only keeping up its reputation. At the last census the same fact obtained. Councillor BENNETT: Itis very Unusual: The minutes were then agreed to and the Council rose.</p>
