        <p>Death of Cheshire's Deputy Chief Constable Tile death occurred on s Saturday morning of Colonel Cope, Deputy Coief Constable of the County of Cheshire. Colonel Cope had I.).en suffering from cardiac affection for about a month, during which time, he was attended by Dr Brierley (Tattenhall) and Dr Dobie (Chester). Apart from his colleagues in the police force and a few personal friends very few people were aware of his illness, and when the announcement was made of his death at nine o'clock on Saturday morning it came as a painful surprise. The deceased, who was a native of Tettenhall. Staffordshire, was previous to coming to Cheshire a captain in the 14th Yorkshire Regi- ment, now the Prince of Wales's Own West York- shire Regiment. He served in the Crimean War, also in the New Zaalaad campaign, and received the service medals of both. One of his most event- ful experiences in the police force was when he was in charge of the police during the salt riots at Winsford, on which occasion he got struck with a stone. The deceased Colonel was 63 years of age.</p>
        <p>CCUMERCIAL FAlLl7liES,—According to Kemp's Mer- cantile Gazette," the number of new bansruprcy proceedings in Eagland and Wales gszetted during the week ending September 28th, was 73. Tho number in the corresponding week ot last year was 81, showing a decrease of 8, being a net decrease in 1901, to date, of 180.</p>
