      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <p>THE NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Warrington and Swinton Lose Two Points.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the representatives of the clubs form- ing the Northern League was held at the George Hotel, Huddersfield, on Tuesday afternoon, under the chair- manship of Mr J Gifford, of Huddersfield. Mr H Stones (Elunslet), tendered his resignation as a member of the Committee, and it was accepted, and Mr Bairsto was appointed in his stead. FinQs for late starts were inflicted as follows :--Brig- house Bangers on September 14, 153 ; Halifax on Sep- tember 7, 63 ; Bradford, September 14, 203. The exphsnation of she Brighouse Rangers Club for a late start on September 28 was considered satisfactory. Mr McCatcheon, referee in the match between Brig- house Bangers and Leigh, reported Norbary, of the latter cluo, for attempting to kick an opponent. In was resolved to suspend lornury up to and including October 12.</p>
        <p>_ It was ordered that in Northern League matches con- veyances must be provided to and from the Eailway stations, the dressing-rooms and the grounds. Iwo points were deducted from the scores of the Swinton and Warrington Ciub3 for breaches ot Pro- fessional Law 2, in azcordauca with Northern League By-Law 38. The Burgess-Davies Case. Mr Brimelow, toucti judge ia trio match played by Warrington and Batley, reported Burgess, ot the former club, and Davies, a Batley player, ior fighting. It was decided to adjourn tree consideration ot the complaint until Friday, for the attendance of the referee.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION. RATCLIFFE GETS A Cup MEDAL.</p>
        <p>On the conclusion of the Northern League meetinz the Northern Union meeting was proceeded with, under the presidency uf Mr J H Houghton, S. Helens. The Mauningham cTb were fined one guinea for breach of professional law 2, in having signed on a player named Uriflings, who bad already signed a professional form for Keie„bley F.C. Th 3 Todmorden clop was found guilty of breach of professional law 2, in having signed T Smalley, of Rochdale florae's, he baying already eigned as a professional for that club. Todmorden were fined loi 61 and Branaley were suspended up to and includ— ing o:itober 31. Hull Marlborough club was foued guilty of breach of by-law 15 and were fined 103 61. Stockport club was fined a guinea for breach of profes- sional rule 1, sub-sectioo C, aryl the Committee rein- stated L Foster from date. The Union decided to allow each cour.ty committee to draw upon the Union to the event of £5O in any season, to be espeaded in the encouragement of junior football. The rules of the Furness and Dis.rict and Manchester and District League were approved. An application made by the I Yorkshire Senior Competition for an alteration of their by-law 23, to allow the compet,tion to deal %vial their own reported players was granted. The Warrington Comcalt;ee were given permission to present a cap medal to E Ratcliffe, woo took part in the ties last. season.</p>
        <p>THE "AGONY" COLUMN. An Unhappy Man's Experience. The quaint descriptive title given to the collection of passionate and pstirtfl appeals to be found in the daily paper covers a multitude of woes, but the briEf mes- sages convey only enough information to the casual reader to arouse unsatiefied curiosity. What depths anguish may reach is shown in the history of a Birming- ham man named Vernurn. employed in a small jewellery trade, which, like many others, has been greatly in- jured by the South Africa° war. A press representative sends notes of a coaversation with Veraum, which are reproduced here in his own language. "Some months ago I was seiz -A with agonising pains in the arm, from. the elbow to the palm of my hand ; just like dogs gnaw- lug at ine,so severe were the pains. I consulted a doc- tor, who treated inn for rheumatism, but I felt no ' better ; in fact, I could feel myself gradually becoming worse. Then I tried the Q leen's and the General Hospitals, and other institutions, being treated at each, but it was all no use : I remained in the same disabled state. At the Genera Hospital the doctors said nothing, so I q iestioned the nurse about my complaint, for I was graoually losing the use of my limbs. I told her I coulchet walk, and coulda'a do anything for my- self. I was unable even to dress myself, and had always to be fed. She told me mine was a serious case, and that I had paralysis. This was ceofirme.d by the fact that the battery was applied three times a week. Finally I wis told that it would be two or three years before I could get to work again." " That was a very bad outlo)k for a married man." " Yes, and I had a family to think of. But one day I was reading in the Argue abut Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people, and my wife said she lead confidence in thoae pills. So away she went for a box. After I had taken the contents of four boxlhs of the pills there were tingling sensations in my fingers and toes, and I began to feel the use of my fingers. Ju3t before I couldn't hold anything ; now—well, give me your hand." And by practical demonstration he showed that his grip had returned. " My recovery was quite rapid after the fourtn box. The power to use my limbs returned, and my strength increased greatly in every way. I took twelve boxes of Dr Williams' pink pills altogether, and my friends were all delighted at my complete recovery. lam now well, and ready for work. I have to thank Dr Williams' pink pills alone far my ecovery ; they are the wonder of the age, and I am more than willing that you should pubish what I say. I can walk ten or twenty miles, though once I thought I should hardly be able to move again." And Mr Vernum's looks did not belie his remarkable story. He seemed energetic and strong when seen at his home, back of 38, New Spring-street, Brookfields, Birmingham, as able as he is willing to be again the breadwinner ot the family. The happy issue out of his troubl a is only what many thousands of others have also enjoyed by the same means. Rheumatism as well as parelyeis, whose first signs are frequently mistaken for the former complaint, has been cured repeatedly by Dr Williams' pink pills, which act on the blood and nervous system by restoring wasted tissues and imparting strength. Other disorders cured by Dr Williams' pink bills include impoverish- ment of the blood, scrofula, rickets, coronic erysipelas, consumption, at :cilia, pale and sallow comnlexion, loss . of appetite, palpitations, pains in the back, sciatica, neuralgia, early decay, also the ailnients from which women alone suffer. The pills are geld in wooden tubes, with pick paper wrapper bearing the full name, Dr Williams' pink pills for pale peeple,in red. If not readily obtainable send two shillings and ninepence for one box, or thirteen and nine for six boxes, to the manu- facturers. Dr Williams' medicine company, Holborn- viaduct, London, but when purchasing at a shop see that yam obtain the genuine pills, which have cured thousands, by asking for Dr Wildams' pink pies, and refuse to be put off with worthless imitations that never cure anyone.</p>
