      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>OCTOBBLI 4ra 1901. 4</p>
        <p>WE are now within a The Unending week of completing the War.</p>
        <p>second year of fighting in South Africa, and all eyes are naturally turned towards the scene of hostilities to descry, if pos- sible, some signs of an approaching close ; but any ' such indication is conspicuous by its absence from the field of vision. The last hopeful refer mice of the COLONIAL SECRETARY to the subject before Parliament closed was to the effect that the operations were now in the final stage. The Boer forces, he said were completely broken up, and were now to bo met with only in two's and three's. The inference implied, if it was not stated, was that the end could not be far off. That was in the beginning of August, and now, after two months have nearly elapsed, we are con.: fronted with the •attle of Itala, which has been described as one of the deadliest fights of the cam- paign. How far the British arms were successful is a matter of doubt, little or no official informa- tion having been published with regard to it. A year ago, when it was thought a convenient and opportune moment had arrived tor a General Election, it was represented by the Government that the war was practically over. The country accepted in good faith the announce" rnent of Ministers, and returned them to power to complete the settlement of South Africa. But the settlement has not begun yet, after twelve months; and, instead, we are given a repetition of the former assurances of the end, only to be one more disappointed. It is no wonder that even the most staunch supporters of the Government in the Press are calling out for an explanation, and denouncing the miserable and humiliating pass to which matters have come. The policy of conceal- ment and distortion, which is being followed more determinedly than ever, only aggravates the anxiety, and prompts the fear, characteristically eipressed by a Ministerial organ, that the war is going to become one of the permanent institutions of toe War Office. If the million a week or more that is steadily finding its way out of the exchequer for war purposes is to continue running out milch longer, the country cannot possibly stand the draining of its resources, and we shall be within measurable distance of national bank- rui.tcy. The situation will not be improved by the humiliation of Great Britain in the eyes of foreign nations which failure will imply. Many of our continental rivals have predicted such a failure, and it will be a keen relish to their jealous disposi- tions if they cm point derisively to the fulfilment of their prediction, as they are already begin fling to do. It is quite true that gaerilla werfare is always elusive and immeasurable ; and, as an illustration, a very serious " incident " was only recently reported at the expense of tiie American forces who are still engaged in the neighbourhood of Manila, after three years' fighting. But the latest development of Boer tactics is not quite of this nature, and the huge disproportion of the British army in S Jan Africa to the opposing forces, which have been reported as being con- stantly worn down, makes it impossible to doebt that the chief factor in the unexpected checks and reverses now being experienced is pure blundering and mismanagement. This hypothesis, unfortun- ately, is favoured by precedent only too marked to escape recollection ; and the fresh hints that are arriving as to misunderstanding between the War Office and the Commander of the Forces in South Africa supply very strong corroboration.</p>
        <p>MR. RIDER HAGGARD is Back to the Land. drawing to a close a I</p>
        <p>series of articles which have appeared periodically in the Press during the greater part of this year on the state of agricul- ture in England. If his concluding surmise is correct, the agricultural population have been obeying a stern economic law in deserting the ' rural districts for the towns, and there is little inducement to place before them with a view to bringing them "back to the land," which has been inhospitable to them in the past and does not promise much more generous treatment in the future. Our author can find no royal road to a ri.nal Utopia, and all he ventures to offer is a series of palliatives for existing evils. It is open to be said that men no longer dream of Utopias. The struggle for existence is no less severe in town than in country. There are not fewer evils to be contended with in city life than in rural exist- ence, and if men prefer the former to the latter it is because it affords readier access to the conditions that go to ensure success in the conflict and greater freedom in their use. In town and country alike thsre are cer- tain qualities, such as courage, persever- ance, thrift, industry, intelligent, well-informed application, which usually go kby the name of enterprise, which never fail to command success and without, which success can never be assured in any department of business. Perhaps it may be that agriculturists have not sufficiently under- stood the value of these qualities in the past, and have preferred looking to others for help in the form of "palliatives," that they have not succeeded as they might have done. Certain it is that where some have failed others have pros. pered, and are prospering to-day. These are they who possess and exercise the qualities mentioned in a greater or less degree, and they compel Mr. RIDER HAGGARD to admit that " as yet" agricul- ture as an industry "is not actually ruined." though the threat is there, hanging like the sword of Damocles, ready to drop on the neck of the ill-fated farmer as soon as prices go down a penny or twopence per pound. It is to be feared that his sojourn so long among a class who are gram- lalers by second nature ha l made Mr. HAGGARD an inveterate pessimist. Tile mills of GOD, he says, seem to be fighting against English agri- culture. He forgets how much that is put down daily to the said mills is really attributable to the immobility and unadaptiveness of human nature as represented in agriculture. There is, or has been, the disinclination to introduce machinery, for instance, for decades after it has been bearing rich fruit in every other industry,and even after its utility has been tried and proved in other countries. There is the inveterate tendency to treat a rural labourer as a beast of burden instead of a man, with the result that he is now deserting his thankless occupation for fields of labour where he is better treated. There is a similar anachron- ism and misplaced conservatism in many of the methods to which agriculturists still hold on,which are fatal in their results to the industry in which they are employed. Then there is the huge anach- ronism of the land laws, and the claims and pre- tensions of the land-owing class,with its grasping' greed and insane selfishness. These are all thino°l for which the mills of GOD are not responsible but which they are grinding to powder, slowly but exceeding small. The modern trend of politics has left, high and dry, reforms of the land laws, which used to be spoken about a generation ago, and are now completely forgotten in the hurry and rush of present-day incident. These anti- quated laws accentuate the innate immobility of thellagricultural organism, and greatly retard the development of the elastic quality which is so desirable in the interests of a large, useful and really deserving class. When our agriculturists cease to look for doles at the expense of the general community ; when they no longer regard protec- tion as the best remedy for foreign competition— then they may be disposed to turn their attention to the real sources of the evil, and by dividing the responsibility between themselves and the legisla- ture introduce a new era of prosperity for a valuable and really invulnerable industry.</p>
        <p>How many cyclists are there in the United Kingdom ? An answer to this somewhat familiar question has been attempted in the "Morning Post "by Mr H H Griffin, whose authority in all matters relating to cycling is not to be denied,and from a million to a million and a quarter is the estimate. There are, of course, no existing data on which an accurate calculation can be made. In France the counting of the cyclists is an easy matter, all machines having to carry a numbered tablet ; and the fact stands beyond dispute that 97:5,000 persons paid duty on their machines in , the year 1900. Cycling may be regarded aq almost if .iot quite as popular across the Channel as with us, for if the bourgeoisie de not use the wheel for purposes of plea- sure so widely as our • middle-classes, the town workman in France more frequently cycles to and from his occupation than his English counterpart. There can be no doubt, however, that the total of motor cycles registered in France, viz, 11,200, is very largely in excess of the num- ber which career about our own roads, to the great distraction of the record-keeping police. For comparison with the popularity of the cycle in Great Britain it may be mentioned that the num- ber of horses, as officially returned, does not ex- ceed a million and three-quarters, so that there is every likelihood in a few years of the machine outnumbering as well as outpacing the noble animal which serves mankind so well in sport, in war, in travel, and in commerce For die whole world Mr Griffin places the total of wheelers at between _eight and nine millions; but we rather incline to think he has under-estimated the use of the cycle in remote corners of the earth.</p>
        <p>Quite a number of articles have been written lately in trade journals as to the decline in the wood supply. The shortening of stock at d the re- duction of forests are not events that can be pointed to-as occuring in a single day or a single year.By virtue of transit along the waterways,when one source is a little below the mark we seem able to make up for the loss from another, and it is only by a wide look round that the prospect can be fully appreciated. The Germans have given the most attention to this subject,and they have come to the conclusion that unless better provision is made, or an invention reduce some of the present applications of wood, the next generation will have to use it thriftily at a higher price, and the genera- tion after that will, unless replenishing has gone on in earnest, have a bad time. Sir Herbert Maxwell has presented the English view in the Nineteenth Century for this month. We have in Great Britian and Ireland three million acres of woodland, which is as if the entire counties of Oxford, Worcester, Warwick,Leicester, Northampton and Nottingham were covered with a" boundless contiguity of shade." It is reckoned however; that even if they had received for a hun- dred years past the most skilful treatment they could not have been made to meet more than one- third of the demand now supplied by the foreigner. From lack of proper attention, however, the timber leaves much to be desired, both in quality and rotation of crop. Sir Herbert Maxwell has to acknowl3dge that the State woodlands are fo r the most part unremunerative. Some of them Involve a large deficit. But he is not discouraged</p>
        <p>by that fact. His answer is that they are 114 conducted on commercial principles. He has, 0° paper, elaborated schemes for making fcrestrY highly profitable, The cynic may say they recall those ingenious tables for making an income out of poultry-keeping—always assuming.that the he° would lay eggs automatically; but Sir Herbert has been thorough. He would put a mortal btin on rabbits, and there are other indications that his scheme has been well thought out.</p>
        <p>0 The question of • the Mayoralty of War" rington has now advanced as far towards finality as is possible prior to November 9tb• Mr Parr has accepted a new and now lunalai" mous invitation from the Town Council the outcome of the conference between the Mayor and the Mayor-elect held recently. Alderman Roberts and Mr. Parr have been on the ineaV friendly terms with one another all through, though the same can hardly be said of their re- spective supporters. In some aspects the Mayor- alty question has not been the pleasantest of things 'to dwell upon, and we are glad that it has come to so amicable an issue. There can be no doubt that Mr .Parr will worthily uphold the dignity of the office, and we cordially welcome him to the prospective position of chief magistrate of the borough. We, of course, sag think Alderman Roberts has the prior claim and hes just grounds for dissatisfaction with tbe treatment he has received from a number of hie ' fellow-members of the Council. With this, how- ever, Mr Parr has nothing whatever to do and his gentlemanly forbearance and deliberation dur- ing the past few weeks have earned for Linn both the respect and the loyal allegiance of every townsman, Liberal and Conservative alike.</p>
        <p>A very familiar figure has, been removed frorn Warrington by the death of Mr William Diggle at the venerable age of ninety years, and none wile knew him can help feeling regret at his demise- " Billy" Diggle as he was familiarly called by generation which was unborn when he was in the prime of life, was a bit of a crank, but a distinct!, harmless and amusing one. He had notions not few. Generally they were for the improvement of something, the water supply and the water in particular. Mr Diggle was reformer of the ardent type, and he did not hesitate to come before the public with his views. He would call meetings and give addresses —to an unsympathetic and facetious audience as s rule—and the meetings eften resolved themselves into duels of wit between lecturer and audience. Though greatly outnumbered Mr. Diggle was al"' ways a match for his critics. He was a patria chal looking fellow was Mr. Diggle,with long white beard,rheumy eyes,and;attired in a capacious bledt coat with fathomless pockets. In these he carried his "stock in trade "—usually a bottle of loo5! aqua pura and two or three lime-choked water pipes. Though a house-fast invalid of late how' clearly his face and form recur to one's mind We axe sorry he is gone, for he was a "good sort" was "Billy" Diggle.</p>
        <p>Ltriar" did Do&amp; ioo )re mg&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Two greet conferences held in London during the present week have discussed matters of at leas' as much importance to the Provinces as they cao possibly be to the metropolis, but representatives of the labouring classes in deciding where theY shall annually assemble, like other people have sometimes more than one object in their miod'sf eye. The partially representative conference delegates accredited from six hundred British Friendly Societies met to consider the Old Age Pen— sions question, and on the second day passed resolution in favour of approaching the Govern— ment with a scheme for providing State pensions for the aged thrifty. The Government, despite their often reiterated promisee, have declined to take any initiative in the promotion of a pensions bill,r a kea nad selectionth responsibilitytrom thehlaasrgne oriwu aidevolved that u:_o: the friendly societies. The difficulty with the deli,— gates was, not to find schemes for discussiontbuhCal become available ; each of which is, of 'course,neq by its author to be a proper and perfect solution v the problem that has puzzled a generation of econ°, mists and statesmen. These range frcra a rate-aideo to the free and universal, through a bewildering series of gradations. indeed, an ;amendment Wes moved at Tuesday's conference, declaring that n° scheme of the kind would be satisfactory that not include all classes, but the delegates would IT have it and succeeded in passing their resoluti( unanimously.</p>
        <p>Never have the delegates to the Amalgamated Societx of Railway servants' Conference had a more important subject to consicer at their anntle,l gathering than that arising out of the legal dee" sion given in the case of the Taff Vale RailwaY, • strike. The Conference opened on Monday sh", closed yesterday. Mr Bell, M.P., the secretary, not affect to ignore the importance of that dectsiop in his annual report and has this week frank], admitted that the outlook is very grave: "1:13! present position of trades unions," he says, "is now a serious one ; for they can be held liable for atlY,, unlawful acts committed by their officers nau members, and damages may be awarded against the unions.' As a matter of fact, it may be added, the Taff Vale Railway Company, are now proceed; ing with an action against the Amalgamate' Society of Railway Servants to recover dimages. 011 account of the loss sustained through the strike. and if the action saould prove successful the prig' rnent will have an important bearing upon Lb future, not only of the A.S.R.S., but of all trade unions in the kingdom. At the same time it is onll natural tat railway servants should be exclaimivg bow strange it is, if the recent decision be coffee, that the true state of the law was not discoverer', before. However, the Parliamentary committee 0. the Trades Union Congress have also the matter 1" hand, and are understood to be taking steps with view to securing fresh legislation upon the aubjeet. and in the meantime unionists are urged to reture only labour representatives to Parliament.</p>
        <p>One of the most welcome items of news in a pre- vincial, and indeed in a national sense that have appeared this week, has been that which related a definite move taken towards the settlement 01' the Grimsby Strike. Is was announced in all the ! morning papers on Tuesday that at length, the ' owners had decided to accept arbitration. The meaning of this, of course, was that both wo,slsl abide b 3 the award of the arbitrator or ar.hl,.: trators, the men by their recent ballot-vote haviht ' declared almost unanimously in favour of arbitr? , don: The protracted strike, and the deplorabl . conflicts arising out of it, had already challenge • widespread and sorrowful attention. In the OW! of Grimsby itself, where the severest privatloo.; have been endured by thousands of persons, of them helpless women and children, the news a modus vivendi had been reached was received v 4, many demonstrations of joy; "glad tidings deed, in many a semi-desolate home and sore'i', pinched family circle. The military have all be!, now removed from the town,which should qeee awaken from its industrially cataleptic state. .1,7,,, moral of the Grimsby strike does not essentis' differ from the "morals ' afforded by, or at rate deduced by various writers from, other dli pates of the kind. Ihe mere philosopher perhaps always go on wondering why, if arbitratied was to be eventually accepted, it is not acCeP,faeof until the resources of owners and men alike, ahuii.. the town itself, have been squandered and we nigh exhausted,</p>
        <p>-- Another Labour item of more than ordinarY West, and of some importance as relating t° tr supply and price of coal in the now rap'dlY proaching winter months, is that which tells us ee a reduction of the Suuth Wales miners' wageB t,tinra- and threequarters per cent, as the result elusion arrived at this week by the Sliding Committee.</p>
        <p>• There is much in the Board of Trade BePorJO w' published, both to make glad the heart (t the te,76; perance reformer and to " give pause " to 010t,1",:t,,, class of readers England has long had the or otherwise, of being a pre-eminently beer-dr.7..1 ing country, and it is something to be thaoks:A:o for to know tbat we,a, a nation. drank less of t'or liquid last year than in the year before. It tnagecl may not, be that the absence of some two huori ‘ thousand " absent-minded " ones in South Al</p>
        <p>' acCounted for the difference,which acco•ding to the , report is that of one gallon per head of the popu- "llium. It is at the same time informing to be told that we (as a nation be it observed) are not the heaviest drinkers on earth booking at the riguro relating to the amount of traffic annually, it might ,naturally be thought we were. But it is a relief to find that such distinction belongs to somebody else, the Bavarians and Belgians to wit. Strange ae it may appear, Bavaria and Belgium are reputed to be the two most flourishing countries in the world, while their morality as a people is by no means on a low scale. If we could really believe all that we read, temperance People might be well advised to hasten slowly in their efforts to improve public houses out of exist- ence. But in order properly to understand what We read it is sometimes necessary to ponder care- fully between the lines, where. all unnoticed, may hang many a tale. The Government finds in the drink traffic aready source of income. For every hundred pound sterling it rereives in this shape, thirty-six pounds come from the tap-room and the cellar. It would not be " business "for the Board of Trade even to tell all it knows. The reader must find out what be wants to know more than he is told by inquiry, or in the best way he can.</p>
        <p>It has been said and repeated many times within ' the past few weeks that the 3lotor Car has as many Opponents—Some use the stronger word " enemies" —as the railway engine had to encounter in the early days of steam power. But among all the ob- jectors, opponents, or enemies that have yet raised a voice against the motor car, .there has not yet ap- peared a single consumptive individual, and much less a member of the Society for the Prevention of Tuberculosis. Dr Dawson Turner argues that auto-' mobilism is a cure for consumption. Sceptics may smile, as they have done before,and felt sorry after but the doctor broaches it in all seriousness, and ns as clever and expert as a physician as as he is as an automobilist. The suc- ess of the open-air treatment of consumptives has already been accepted as proven by everybody who has studied the subject, and Dr. Turner shows that a drive in or on a motor car is the best and most suitable way to inhale oxygen. It is admitted that cycling is not so favourable inasmuch as it makes too heavy a demand upon the strength of a consumptive invalid, while ordinary riding or driving is too slow to be beneficial. The motor-car supplies the proper means, and its adoption as one of the modes of travelling promises to become more general. For, if Dr. Turner's principles are correct, there is no reason why, except on the ground of expense, motor-cars should not be introduced as part of the outfit and appointments of our hospitals and infirmaries. It might, indeed, be possible to make it pay the county or borough authorities to Provide thete modern vehicles for the use of con- sumptive individuals and those pre-disposed to con- tract the disease. Anyhow, the idea seems to be taking root rapidly, and there is no knowing what it may yet bring forth.</p>
        <p>Local health authorities in some of the larger cities and towns of the prpvinces are at last begin- ning to take up the 'ice cream question "in earnest. Nauseating descriptions of how this delicacy is produced for consumption in the streets hare been furnished by many towns, and there is now a prospect of something being done by the authorities in tee matter.</p>
        <p>111</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>SUICIDE OF A NEWTON RAILWAY PORTER.</head>
        <p>SUICIDE OF A NEWTON RAILWAY PORTER.</p>
        <p>On Friday evening a railway porter employed at Newton-le-Willows station named William Thomas Jenkinson,commLted suicide by drowning. Jenkin- g.on had been in the employ of the London and North Western Railway Company at Newton for something like fifteen years, and last week was on night duty, and it is said there bad been some trouble at the station, but deceased was not in- volved. He should have teen on duty at seven 'o'clock on Friday night, but as he did not turn up inquiries as to his whereabouts were made at his house. It was feared he had made away with him- self, and shortly after nine o'clock a man named Arnold discovered Jenkinson's body in a pit at Highfield losS. Lowton, a abort distance from deceased's residence. The deceased was married and leaves a widow and two children. The Inquest. A Farewell Note, Mr S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquiry at the Traveder's Rest lan, Lowton on Saturday morning into the circumstances attending the death of the young</p>
        <p>man Jenkinson. Alice Jenkinson, widow of the &amp;ceased gave evidence of identification and said her husband was 33 years of borne At bail-past one last Friday afternoon he left 'laYing he was going for a abort walk on ths Moss. u never haW him alive agaln. fle had not to go on duty until 6.45. Be was brought home dead at a quarter-past nine at night. She did not know of any trouble ; there had been none at home, and she had no euspicion that he intended to make away with himself. The Coroner : Did he make any complaints as to any</p>
        <p>trcuble at the railway station or with 1319 masters ?</p>
        <p>Witness : None, Whatever. The Coroner handed the wittiest a pocket book which had teen found in deceased's clothing, and asked her if She could recogoiz t the writing. —Witness said ribe could. The Coroner : What does he mean when he says " We have had no comfort since last Friday."—Witness Understood there was something at the station, but deceased was not mixed up in it ; there was no trouble att home. The Coroner read the note which deceased bad written in the book September 27cle. Dear Alice, c.nnot stand this any longer. I cannot keep coming home and causing you all this misery. You know that I have not had any comfort since last Friday. • WOat I did was only in fun, but people will make it out otherwise and wrist with suffering again with my back I feel wretched, what with the pain and misery we have had this week Rlndly forgive me for doing this rash act, as I think it the easiest way to get relief. Give my best love to L'etie and Harry and tell th.m I hope to roeet you and them ha Heaven, so good-bye, from your broken-hearted husband, Bill. P.S. Also give my love to all my friends, and goodbye. Please give my love to mother, Sannie, Ben and Minnie and tell father to forgive tope. too." The Coroner : What does he wean when he refers to a pain in his back P—Witness : He hurt his back last Wednesday at the station. Sergeant Bancks said he had seen the Station Master And it Feemed that some of the porters had played a Practical joke on one of Smith's newspaper boys. The hot' asked to have it reported by the Station Master,buc tell: Richardson did not think it worth while. There was no complaint made agaiost deceased and there was tothing for him to answer. Some of the staff chaffPd but there was nothing in it so far as deceased was concerned. He might have been with the lot at the time, but there was no complaint officially against him. Pis brother was involved and be seemed to think of the tr^uble that he was ie. David Bates, of 319 Newton-row!, Lowton, said tit-ceased was his brother-in-law. When he saw him between two and three o'clock on Friday afternoon, he (lid not notice anything peculiar about him, and he was astonished to hear that he bad been found drowned. It was only on Friday night that he heard of the bother at The station. Emmacuel Jenkinson, brother of deceased, said he went to Flighfield Moss pit,, which was lon a large Piece of waste land near deceased's residence. The coat doubled up and the cap inside were lying on the hank. The pocket book produced was in the jacket '.4°„,,011et. His brother had been on the railway all his '"e• Witness had never heard of any trouble until it ~se mentioned on the Friday night. He last saw his brother alive at half-past five. He did not then appear 8,:"1:Inge, and witness never suspected that he would take away his life. The Coroner in summing up said it was clear from 130 evidence that deceased bad taken his own life. He has gone to the place, taken off his cap and jacket and written the note. If they said he had to away his own life, then they would have ° t'aY what was the condition of his mind. The law het ed that a pereon was of sound mind until the con- a tr ti? was proved. The deceased was worried and it „OPsared from the note that he was wretched. The ,- hole thing pointed to the fact that be did not know what be was ding and that he must have been out of We Mind at the time. • . tv_lllge jury came to the conclusion that deceased had -th4erl away his own life and was of unsound mind at time. The Coroner remarked they were all very tty for the widow and everyone concerned. Vr • • --bUcT INSTITUTE BOWLING CLUB, CABLES- , TOWN. bowling handicap for Mr A. Tunstall's annual i„!!) and the entrance fees, took place on the h"eenhtitute Club green The handicap should have e De played the previous Saturday, but was post- , "led owing to the rain. There were 30 entries, wil4 the day tieing fine, some good bowling was tettessed. The first prize of a guinea was carried " S•pottiswood, second prize 7a was taken by • •;helerton, and the third prize of 5s given by Mr ugleton, Was won by Mr J Bedworth. Emu., GONE—ALL IS GONE, but there is hope for , Ha Dew. Pekes in Mellor's New System of Treatment. Par- kelee.teasFree.—lidrLiors. bye specialist, 2E6 Oxford-street "c Cr. Don't delay. Write at once. (6) Advt.</p>
        <p>I</p>
