      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A NEW CHURCH HOUSE FOR RUNCORN. On Saturday afternoon a new church house situ- ate in Church-street was formally opened by Mr J W Sidebottom late M.P. for the Hyde Division. BOWLING TOURNAMENT AT BLACKPOOL. On Saturday, in the first round of the bowling tournament T Richardson, of Runcorn, beat Jain,of Aspull. In the second round on Monday,Richard- son, beat J Whiteside, Blackpool, by 21 to 18. THE QUESTION OF MIXED CLASSES AT BALTON, In a short time the standards from the 3r4 up- wards in the girls department at the National, schools will be transferred to the sirammar School and the junior boys class at the Grammar School will be removed to the girls school. NEW POOR RATES. At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, the magistrates, Messrs H Reynolds, and P J Norman, confirmed new poor rates as follows :—Stockham, Is in the to realize £34 10s 10d ; Hatton, is Si in the £, to realize £339 17e 61; and Norton, Is 2d in the £ to realize £363 7s 4d. DEATH OF AN OLD RUNCORN RESIDENT. The mortal remains of Mrs Ellen Rhodes, aged 75 years, one of Runcorn's oldest residents, were in- I terred at the Runcorn Cemetery on Thurs lay afternoon. The deceased lady succumbed on Sun- day evening last to a sudden illness. There was a very large funeral, several public gentlemen being present. DEATH OF A RUNCORN LADY. We regret to announce the death on Monday, of Mrs Baguley, wife of Mr Joseph W Baguley. of Woodfalls, Stockham. The deceased lady nad been in failing health for some time, and the end was not altogether unexpected. Mr Baguley is well known and highly respected in the neighbourhood, being the local representative on the Runcorn Board of Guardians and Rural District Council, and much sympathy is felt for the family in their pain- ful bereavement The deceased was 54 years of age. ACCIDENT ON THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>The steamship Cathcard belonging to Messrs Pass Owen and Co., Manchester, met with a serious accident in the Ship Canal on Thursday morning. She was inward bound tc% Manchester light and when near the Weaver estuary struck her quarter against the sluices. This caused her co dash ' ashore right across the canal where she went on the Weaver embankment. She still remains high aad dry. The damage done ta the vessel is consider- able and she will not be able to proceed to sea without being repaired. THE MAYORALTY OF WIDNES —ALDERMAN W W GOSSAGE ACCEPTS OFFICE. . On Tuesday afternoon an important and interest- ing meeting took place at the Widnes Town Hall, when the committee appointed to wait on Alderman W W Gossage with ne proposal that he should be- Dome the mayor of the borough in succession to Alderman G I Neil in November assembled, and an interview took place with Mr Gossage. Alderman Gossage accepted the office,and he will no doubt till the mayoral chair in a manner in keeping with the dignity and welfare of the town. THE SALVATION ARMY, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday last the Army corps at Runcorn represeated " The Old Christian Mission " or the Salvation Army of 35 years ago: On Sunday the Army made a tour of the streets under the leader- ship of Captain Young. He wore an told fashioned quaker hat with a quaint black suit and the ladies were attired in neat dresses with small bonnets. The streets were not paraded in the usual military manner but in the way which it was done at the inception of the Army movement. Naturally—very large crowds were attracted by this novel method. In the evening Captain Young delivered a stirring address on the text' And the door was closed." There were two concerts and the proceeds which were most satisfactory will be devoted to the local un d. SENSATION AT RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The congregation at Runcorn Parish Church had a startling experience on Sunday morning. Every- body was standing and the organist had sounded the chord for the last hymn,when there wag a great noise, as though a cannon had been discharged, at the west end of the edifice. Nothing was to be seen, nor was there a repetition of the noise, and the service was concluded with the customary de- corum. It was then found that one of the clock weights, a very heavy piece of metal, bad become detached from its fastening, and hal crashed through the clock box on to the belfry flooring, which, though damaged, was strong enough to pre- vent the weight penetrating and falling into tne surplice chamber The incident caused quite a sensation. Sr PAUL'S WESLEY GUILD.. . .</p>
        <p>. . The annual meeting of the above, was held in the Guild Parlour Brunswick School, last Thursday week. The Rev Alfred, Wells presided. The annual report was read by Mr J Hosker,secretary. Mr J Greenhalgh presented the treasurer's report which showed the guild to be in a prosperous condition The election of officials for the ensuing season then took place as follows :—President,Rev Alfred Wells; vice-presidents, ktesqrs E Taylor, J-Povah, George Hayes, and J H Pickthall ; general secretary, Mr F J Hosker •, assistant, Mr Ezra Darlington; trea- surer, Mr James Greennalgh ; roll secretaries,Messrs Wesley, Coventry, and D Moore ; pianist, Mr W A Lightfoot. Members were also elected for the following committees, Bible and Prayer Union, Literary and Reading Circle, Recreation, Social and Musical, and Look-out Committee. A proposal to have the. Reading Circle once a month was deferred until the opening meeting on October 10th when the matter will be fully dealt with The Gym- nasium will be opened in the course of a week or two.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN _WOMEN'S __IEMPERINCE :ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The Presidenc and Committee of the Runcorn Branch of the B,W.T.A. held an " At. Home " in St. Johns Schoolroom last Wecnesday evening. About 300 young ladies responded to the invitations sent. Mrs Baker received and welcomed the guests, and Mrs Leftwich, of Liscard, President of the Lanca- shire and Cheshire "Y"Unien. spoke for a short time about Young People's Societies.—After a cup of tea and social converse, Mrs Baker took the chair, and a short meeting was held, during which a "Y "or Young People's Branch was formed, about 160 young ladies joining as members. The officers elec- ted were :—President, Mrs Baker •, vice-presidents, Mrs T Simkin, Mrs R Barron, and Mrs Cameron; Secretary, Miss Bellamy ; Treasurer, Miss Emily Simkin; and a committee of young ladies repre- senting each church in the town. Mrs Leftwich and Miss Jones, of Liscard, and the Misses Simkin, and A Davies contributed very much to the pleasure of the evening with songs and recitations, Miss Forrestel kindly accompanying. The officers will be very pleased to receive the names of any young ladies who would like to join the" Y " Branch. EPIDEMIC AT HALTON. In consequence of the spread of measles the schools at:Halton have'been temporarily closed. HALTON WESLEYAN TEMPERANCE FOCIETY. The connection with the above society a lecture was delivered on Monday evening entitled "Is the food value of brewed liquors proportionate to ite cost," by Mr El Baker, P.I.C. Runcorn. Mr James Looker presided, and he was supported by Messrs A Rowe, temperance secretary, D Bisbrowne, W Looker and A Woodier. The chairman contrasted the position of Halton and Manchester. In the latt3r place there were 183 of the population to every public house while in Balton there were but ' 180. The lecturer pronounced the foodv slue to be altogether out of proportion to its cost and de- monstrated chemically the different quantities of nourishment contained in beer and bread. There was also a lose of food in the process of brewing beer from barley.—Mr Bisbrown intimated that they intended to open a temperance branch at Halton and extended a hearty invitation to those wishful to join. A vote of thanks was passed to the lecturer on the proposition of Messrs W Moores and W Looker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>rll E EXAMINER</head>
        <p>rll E EXAMINER</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. --- [BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>The fall of the leaf again reminds U 9 that the mell ow season of autumn is upon us once more. The summer with its radiant sunshine is now be- coming a thing of the past and instead of the glorious beams peeping over the top of" you high eastern hill" and flickering in through the open casements the morn in russet mantle opens aull 1 with the heavy dews peculiar to the season. Before now the harvest has been safely garnered in and tile country has already begun to present that bare and uninviting appearance which makes a towns- man shudder at the thought of living his life in the country. The hedgerows, too, have not that trim and graceful appearance they had some weeks ago for the young shoots and tendrils still vie with each other in Vieir struggling growth. Along the roads the decaying leaves which fall from the overhanging trees aid a weird picturesqueness to the scene, while the dark mould, relieved only here and there by dull patches of green,add an additions sense of desolation. Bird life, however, has notl yet ceased its note of gladness, and in the morning the lark can be heard chanting its matin song. In a few short weeks,however,all this will be changed The walks will be neatly tidied and swept, toe, hedgerows cut and trimmed, the tall nasturnium tne luxurious canary creeper,and the ruddy Virginia will no longer be allowed to bang listless on the wall but will be swept away. At the first breath of winter "the voice of toe bird will no more be heard," and all nature will yield herself to the embrace of her chilly master.</p>
        <p>The distribution of prizes to the successful night students at the Board School on Monday was a success in the truest sense of the word. Few per- sons realize the vast amount of silent value which is being performed by the Continuation Classes. One of its most important departments is devoted to educating those who have not bad opportunities in their youth for self-improvement. Such men are taught apart from other studenss, and are given a thorough grounding in the rudimentary elements of education. Judging by he large number of awards to working men well advanced in life it is evident that the value of such a process is being gradually recognised by the workingmen of Runcorn. It was more than a t inspiration to educationalists to see some of the horny-handed sons of toil step forward for their well-earned award of merit almot like bashful schoolboys. It is an addizional incentive to press forward in the good cause and Mr Moulton in his endeavour to do good by stealth, blushing to find it fame deserves the encouragement of all and the hearty thanks of the community.</p>
        <p>The most pleasing feature or the meeting fr3m an educational point of view was the vigorous and eloquent speech delivered by Mr F J Norman. The education question is one on which this gentleman is even more than a past master, and on Monday night he was in one of his happiest moods. After dealing with education in general and the local classes in particular Mr Norman referred to the Cockerton judgment which dropped like a bolt from the blue among educationalists,in an illustra- tion which is worth repeating. Everyone knows that an illustration will sometimes carried convic- tion where argument fails. S 3 that from the broad standpoint of the education question it deserves attention. Mr Norman said " With all due deference to Mr Cuckerton and the party who sat behind him, in my opinion they had been far wiser had they followed the example of Nelson at the battle of Copenhagen and put their blind eye to any technical illegality which existed. My candid opinion is that those , gentlemen were more concerned to degrade School Boards than to maintain the majesty of the law. Had they lived in the days of Nelson they would have been more concerned to court martial him for using his blind eye than to honour him for winning such a magnificent battle."</p>
        <p>The quotation very aptly lays bare the motive of the hyper-critical gentlemen who;:found the flaw in the garment of the act and at once pro- ceeded to rend it wider. It is of course, apparent to any school boy, as Macauley would say, that the blatant announcing of the illegality did not arise from any love of truth but from the mere vulgar policy of destruction rather than construc- tion. The twentieth century educationalists would have done better had they acted on the motto, "Be ye wise as serpents and harmless as doves."</p>
        <p>As the majority of taxpayers are aware the mem- bers of the Sehool Board finish their term of office some time in November next. The present com- position of the Board of nine is four progressives or unsectarian supporters of the Board system, to five adherents to the Voluntary Schools. At present it is much too premature to argue what will happen in the course of the next two months. Already, however, so keen was the struggle three years ago, people are beginning to ask whether there is going to be any contest. As a famous old woman once said, " Well ! Well ! we shall see what we shall see." Artemus Ward once remarked, " Never pre- pbesv until you know.' and therefore it would be unwise to predict anything with respect to any election.</p>
        <p>The Brunswick Mutual Improvement class has again commenced its winter session for the fortieth year since its inception. It is unquestionably the oldest debating society in the town and is a splendid training ground for the would be orator. Its president, Mr. Simister has the unique distinction of having been the figure head of the class from its birth. During the time he has pre- sided over its destinies many men have been turned out into the busy world unquestionably the better for having been under its influences. Many of our best local speakers gained their readine.s of utterance,called by Irishmen "the gifts of the gab" by the facilities offered. Toe class is all but un— sectarian and it presents a splendid opportunity for improvement to those who wieh to rise en steppings to higher flights of eloquence. It is half a.pity that a Parliamentary debating society can- not be started for the youth of the town, bur, per- haps like Jonah's gourd it would rise in a night to 'perish in a night similar to the one founded some years ago.</p>
        <p>The Cuaticil at their meeting yesterday very wisely referred the minute rd rting to the Co-operative slaughter house back to the Health Committee for fur- ther consideration. Up to Belll3 twelve CrleLltbS ago the Society bought all their pigs ready killed. Wishing, however, to retain the blood of the animals for manu- factured foods they built a siaughter-house so that the pigs might be killed under personal supervision. To this end they spent a sum of nearly £2OO in making their slaughter-house as up to date as possible. Unfortunately the Co-operative Committee had forgotten that they required a license from the local authority before killing could be permitted. This application came before the Health Committee in the usual way and the committee somewhat nnwiscla recommended that the request be refused. The Council has however, taken a mole open view of the situation. They recognize clearly that if they refuse to legalize slaughtering of animals:at the Cc-operative Society's slaughterhouse they will be compelled to take scion against the innumerable persons who slaugh- ter pigs at the back of their houses under the most insanitary conditions. Last year the Co- operative S-ores purchased nearly two hundred animals which had boon slain in the town. The public slaugh- ter house is not all that can be desired and it seems to savour of a" dog in the manger " policy to prevent the society carrying out what the Council itself is not as yet, properly prepared to do.</p>
        <p>It is a pity that the Council have not been fortunate enough to persuade the London and North-Western Railwa) Conanany to give us a better service of -trains. Of course we know very well that the Company will not move in the matter unless they are convinced that pecuniary benefit to themselves will accrue. It is how- ever, au open queation whether it would riot pay them in the long run. There are many business people who would use the train far more frequently than they do at present if such were the case. and there are many working men who would be tempted out of town if the connections were more convenient. There is really no reason why the excur- sionist should not be t.ermitted to come back from Liverpool before six o'clock at, night for the train previous to that time is a purely local one. This pecu- liar state of things is, however one of the idiosyncracies lof the railway people and Runcorn being almost like , the town described by George Eliott as being " ten miles from nowhere" makes the situation decidedly awkward and unpleasant.</p>
        <p>FIRE AT THE RUNCORN THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Oa Sund at mid-dav, consioerable excitement was occasioned in the town by a fire at the theatre. Before the fire brigade could reach the premises, however, all danger Was over. thatills to the prompt action of John Dobson, of Gilbert-street, who discovered the fire, and I other persons who happened to be in the neighbour- hood at the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>FRI DA Y OCTOBER 4 1901.</head>
        <p>FRI DA Y OCTOBER 4 1901.</p>
        <p>VICTOR;A ROAD BOARD SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Annual Distribution of Prizes.</p>
        <p>The annual distribution of prizes took place on Mon. day e coning at Victoria-road Board Schools. There was a large audieoc3 composed of adults and juniors. Mr F J Norman presided and he was supported by Mrs Norman, the Rev J Chambers, Messrs W J Cunningham, J M Frith, J W Lightburn and J 1? Moulton. „ Mr Moulton reported that the session ending the 30th April was the 11th for the men and boys and the Bth tor the girls, The men and boys met on 64 evenings and the girls 46. Toe number of men and boys admitted was 106 arid the girls 59. the average attendance per even- ing of the males, was 66, and the females 35, a total increase of 20 over last year. The government grant in the men's and boys department amounted to £7B 6i 61, girls £29 las 61 being an average of 17s iu the girls and £1 33 81 in the men:B department. Under the code known as the 171161 limit there was a reduction of £1993 61, making a net sum payable of £9O 16s 61, an increase of £lB 45 over last year. The class for adults had had a most successful session. Mr Balance, his Majesty's Inspector, who had paid several unexpected v.sits, reported that the girls were taught with intelligence and care, and the boys were under good control. Toe only necessary thing required was a cookery class. Mr Norman said he regretted to inform them that Mr Balance, his Majesty's Inspector, would not be with them, which was almost similar to endeavouring to play Hamlet without the prince. Be had always regarded him as the patron saint of the continuation class and he had told them to their great delight that it was the most successful class in the district. Mr Moulton had given them a detailed account of the work of the session but it might be interesting to them if he referred from a more national standpoint to the subject of educe- tion.lt was pretty well agreed that for a number of years the Britisher lagged tremendously behind in the educa- tional race, He was not going to discuss the cause or , tell them what they thought was the reason because many of them thought differently. If they went back to the Franco-German War every man in the French and German armies could read and write. At that time the Eoglisti people could not do one or the other. At last they woke up. Classes were started in 1867 and in 1892 there was an•average attendance of 45,000. This went on steadily increasing until it reached an average attendance of 65,000. Then Mr Ackland came on the scene who was a perfect terror to educationalists. Be made things lively all round and a decided progress was the result. In 1892 he extended the cods, the age limit, and did a lot of remarkable things which made thelclasses go up by leaps and bounds until in 1900 an average attendance of 206.000 had been achieved. With respect to the age limit thy thought 18 at first old enough; then they grew wiser and said 21. W'aen Mr. Ackland arrived on the scene be said let them all come if they liked, and thus for the first time on educa- tional record Eaglishrnen were put on the same footing as their, Seotch friends had been for years. The age limit was abolished altogether in 1900 and he then found there was an average attendance of 80,000 pupils Over the age of 21. Some they had seen that night and he was delighted to see the prizes awarded to them. The evening Continuation classes had been most useful from an educational standpoint. When a boy left the ele- mentary school and bad got clear of educa- tional schemes it was hard work for him to keep uo his stu lies. These classes, however, enabled him to keep up his elementary education, Older men, too, could 'acquire edue.ation which had been denied them in their youth. .He knew of no educational system which enabled men to " rise on stepping stones of their dead selves to higher things " than the educa- tion which linked the earlier and the later studies to- gether. There was a little misunderstanding existing in the town which should be removed' There was not the slightest reason for friction tetween that and, any other organization and there was no overlapping be- tween them and the Technical Institute. Lie Con- tinuation Classes only gave a most iu limentary educa- tion and there was no pupil attending the Board School for advanced instruction. The evening schools too, served as a feeder to the Technical Institute. When a pupil had progressed in any parieolar subject he was strongly advised to go to the Institute. If their educa- tional friends would clearly grip the situation they would not hear much more about overlapping. For a number of years Sir Jonn Gorst and the Duke of Devonshire had gone up and down the country singing the praises of educational classes. Every kind of educs. tionalist could meet and work without religious diffi- culties or party squabbles. Twelve months ago it was discovered they had been en caged in most illegal prac- tices. He regretted that the Government had not made up its mind to levii23 Snot:a boarde. They had chosen another way and had decided that School Boards must act under another authority. So they now carried on the classes by the grace of the County Council. He congratulated the prize winners on thi ir RUCVBiO4. To those who had failed he offered a word of consolation. If they had put the best they had in their work it did not much matter whether they got a prize or not, Elucation was the finest investment they could have, and it irado life brighter and happier. It was incumbent upon everyone to qualify himself as best be could as though the Empire rested upon his shoulders. Such work was as noble as the man fighting in South Africa for both were helping to build up the Empire. The prizes were then distributed to the successful students by Mrs F J Norman, each receiving quite an ovation of applause as he received his prize or certifi- cate. The following is a complete list :</p>
        <p>PRIZR LIST: GIRLS DEPARTNIRNT.</p>
        <p>FOR ATTENDANCE AND GOOD WORK :—Minnie Carter, Lucy Bradshaw, Florence McNOughton, Sarah Harrison, Rlien Summerfield, Gertrude Hill, Clara Lines, Florence Hunt, Beatrice Marsh, Ethel Lightfoot, SCIENCE :—Evelyn Hitchmough, Edith Spira, Rose Spira. NEEDLEWORK :—Beatrice Britland, Mabel Williams, Hilda Williams, Louisa Cropper. SICK-NURSING :—.ltva Gayter, Ada Holt, Kezia CERTIFICATES AWARDED DT THE UNION OF LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE INSTITUTES.</p>
        <p>ARIYHmETIC (FIRST CLASS) ELEMENTARY GRADE. Annie Quinn, Minnie Carter. Marjorie Faulkner•• SICK-NUReING (ELEMENTARY) 2ND CLASS. Minnie Carter. MEN AND Bora' DEPARTm ENT. The felloving gained prizes for successful work and regular attendance . MEN'S ('LASS.—H Bell, G ;Moores, G J Lightfoot. J J Dirchfield, J Woodcoc J Moores, Smith, J Parry, F Crompton, J Sorutton, H Houghton, B Ellison, A Ilielslorton, U Glover .</p>
        <p>JAROoR CLA.6B:—G If Higgins, vir NlChohl, G Curzon, R Gibbs, P Douglas, V Ball, J T Lewis. If Grubb, J Bailer, J Littlemore, A Hewitt. W Shingler. W Woodward, A Bell. J Hindlev. A Allison, J Welch, a. Froward. 3 Ellwood, N Stancliffe, W If Tudor, T Clare, 11 Battersby. J W Owen, A Clarke. F Harrison. T Johnson, J FC Roberts. The following gained certificates frOrn the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Institutes : ARITHMETIC (IST CLASS) B Roberts, A Draper, A Fox, M Owen, R Gibbs. COMMERCIAL CORRESPONDENCE AND H NDWRITING : (tst class). A Draper. R Gibbs ; (2ni class). St Owen. N Jones, hi Owen. E Roberts, W Turnbull ; 2nd Class . BOOKKEEPING :—Pass. A Bell, Clarke, A Fox. E Weave, B Ellison, R Gibbs, G Higgins, G Howard. During the evening a most istere3ting musical pro- gramme was submitted. The Misses Wright and Simp- kins rendered in a most charming manner "Friendship' the letter lady afterwards singing that remarkably pretty song known as " Suashine and Rain." Mr Branch Was encored for a capital ref:tiering of " Tnis Boatswain's story " " True as the Corr pa and " Thess '' Tin Gan Gee." "The Tor M mker c'73""sa with much espression by Miss Wool while Mr J Moores gave 'When other lips." -</p>
        <p>Or the proprsition of Mr Cinnineham, seconded by the RAV J. Chambers a into of thanks wag passed to Mrs F J Norman, Mr Norman and the artistes. Mr. Norman havine, responded the meeting concluded with the singing of the national anthem.</p>
        <p>Warrington Apollo Male Choir. A social evening in connection with the above was held in the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan School- room on Saturday last, there being about 30 friends present. An excellent tea was provided by Mrs Thomas Brough after which the evening was spent in singing &amp;c. Mr j Moss after making an eloquent speech presented Mrs T Brough with a handsome coffee pot given by the members of the choir. Mr J Moss also presented the conductor with a splendid silver mounted baton the inscrip- tion reading " Presented to Thomas Brough by the members of the Apollo Male Choir." Mr and Mrs Brough who were taken by surprise thanked the members very heartily for the kindness they had shown to them. Votes of thanks were given to Mr and Mrs T Brough and the chairman (Mr W Myles) which concluded a very enjoyable evening. Opening of the New Police Buildings. The new police buildings will be opened by his grehip the Mayor (Alderman Roberts) on Friday the 11th of October, at twelve noon. The interest- ing proceeding will be followed by a lunch at the Town Hall. Great progress has been lately made in the building operations and it is anticipated that the whole of the magisterial portion of the premises will be completed by the above date.</p>
        <p>CRAW FOR D'S CREAM CRACKERS:</p>
        <p>CRISP:AND CREAMY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held on Thursday morning at the Council Chamber :—Present, Messrs It H Posnett in the chair, W Handley, F Wis- wall, E Liaison, D Bisbrown. Jli Salkeld, W Jones, T Dean, M Sweetman, H G Grice, 8 Taylor, J Wilson, E Gatley, 8 Walton, J Pritchard, F A Lake (law clerk), E Marshall, (secretary), J Wilding (surveyor), and Dr McDougall (medical officer of health).</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>It was reported that during the month of September there were 31 deaths,nine of which were due to typhoid. There were also two inquests In the corresponding month last year there were 27 deaths and there were 51 births last year against 43 this year. Tne report was approved, Mr Handley remarking that it was very satisfactory considering the wave of typhoid that was sweeping the country.</p>
        <p>_ MORE SHRITB3 F Hi THE COMMON. ACCEPTED TENDERS. _</p>
        <p>The Highways Committee recommended that the fol. lowing tenders be accepted :—Mr J Horsefield, Runcorn for the supply ot 50 tons of limestone chippings ; the Pwlltieli Granite Company for 59 tons granite chipplugs It was recommended that a sum of £25 be expended in the purchase of shrubs for the common. The plans of Mr 8113a1ley tor privy at 42 Regent-street and privies for Mr L Holmes at 84, 80, 88, 90 and 92, Church-street and 1, 3,5, and 7. Qieen-street were approved. Mr raylor moved the confirmation of the minutes. Mr Grice thought that the minutes referring to the purchase of shrubs should be sent back to the com- mittee. It was not clear to his mind whether the £25 was part of the estimate of the money to be sp,sut on the common or not. He moved that ft be sent back to the commietee.</p>
        <p>Mr Sweetman seconded. Mr Bisbrowa was wader the impression that the amount was included :in the amount of £5O, that it was decided to spend on the Common. He intend_d to bring the question up at the ner; meeting. Mr Wiswall said that the money to be exp3nded on the specifications was for three items—the triangular portion of the Common ; finishing that portion in hand; and for the road running alongside. He was quite at a loss to know why it came before them in that form.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor explained that the reason the £25 was asked was because they were no: game prepared to go on with the larger scheme just yet. If the plants were not got in Oocober it would be too late and it would throw the planting of them hick for another sear. he other Question would be considered during the month and would some forward at the next meeting. 03 being out to the vot 3 three only were in favour of the amendment. Toe minutes were then confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE SLAUGHTEII-HOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE CO-OPERATIVE SLAUGHTEII-HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee res)lved that Messrs Taylor, Dean, Handley, and Jones represent the Council on the joint committui which is to elect a person to represent the Urban District Councils of Lvmm and Runcorn and the Rural District Councils ot Bucklow and Runcorn on the Manchester Port Sanitary Authority. It was reported that the Runcorn Co-operative Society had applied for permission to kill pigs at the rear of their premises, No 4 and 6 Church-street, Runcorn. The committee recommended that as the Council has sufficient public acoornmodation for slaughtering animals:it cannot see its way to grant any private liceoce. Mr Dean moved the confirmation of the minutes, and Mr Jones seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr Imison thought it advisable to have the report of the Committee appointed to visit the slaughter house before the Council definitely committed itself to the minute relating to the application of the Co— p2rative Society. It was necessary to know whether there was that adrqaate accommodation asserted. Be moved as an anlenornent that the minute be eliminated. Mr Grine seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr Wiswall said it seemed to him they were working in the dark, as they had got no report. Mr Dean did not think it fair to the sub-committee appointed to visit the slaughter house. The place was visited and Mr Hewitt who knew all about the matter was asked certain questions. It was reported that there was plenty of room for the killing of all the pigs of the Society, provided a little alteration was done which Mr tYi!ding had been instructed to do. The Committee felt it would be unwise to allow any Society or individual to come into the town and set up in opposition to the place provided oy the ratepayers at a cost. of £3,000. Mr %View,' Do I understand that there 14 suffi- cient accommodation for the killing of all pigs in the town even by those who do it without authority ? Mr Dean : Yes.</p>
        <p>Mr Taylor supported the amendment. There would be 35 of the Society's pigs to be killed in addition to the ordinary slaughtering. It migh", cause friation and it would not be nice to eat bumble pie. The !natter required careful consideration oefore arriving at a final decision.</p>
        <p>The amendment was eventually carried after further discussion.</p>
        <p>Mr Dean would not propose the minutes in their amended form and they were subsequently passed on the proposition of Messrs Wiswall and Imison.</p>
        <p>THE WATER AND DISTRICT RATE.—PROPOSED ALTERATION. Toe Water Committee recommended that as the sanction of the Local Government ['hard had been re- ceived for the borrowing of £1,959 for the erection of water tower, new boiler, crane, etc, tenders be obtained to enable the work to be carried out. was further recommended that the water rate be cot- !ected half yearly instead of quarterly. Mr Pritchard moved that the minutes be passed. It would be more convenient to the ratepayers to pay the water and d strict rate together on or,e demand note. Mr Taylor seconded, and the mnautei were approved.</p>
        <p>PURCHASE OF APPARATUS.</p>
        <p>The Technical InAtruclon Committee recommended that additional apparatus for the teaching of physic 3 and building coneruction be purchased at an estimated cost of £25 and £lO.</p>
        <p>Passed on the propositi3n of Mr Wilson seconded by Mr HanciTey.</p>
        <p>FINANCE-EEAUTIFYING TEE COUNCIL CHAMBER.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee recommended that additional provision be made in the Cmncil Chamber fur the portraits of the chairmen and that the chamher be repainted, with certain alterations, at a cost of £3O. Mr Imison moved the adoption of the minutes and said the cleaning was ur.geotly required. The proposed alterations would bt greatly to the convenience of the officials. The collectinz of the rate half-vearly iastead of quarterly would save a great deal of labour. Mr Salkeld seconded and the minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>MARKETS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Markets and Cemetery Coracnitt'ze recommended the precept be served on the overseers of the poor for £165 for the six monttri ending 31st Mmoth next for the repayment of bane and a precept for £6O for cemetery expenses for the same period. Passed on the proposition of Messrs Walton and Gatley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>TEE LATE CENSUS.</head>
        <p>TEE LATE CENSUS.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING STATISTICS. The Registrar General furnished the Council with th 2 following particulars relating' to the census : Inhabited ho sses. Persons. Males. Fernsles. Hunslrn U.D. Civil Parishes. 3134 16491 8333 81:6 RUNCORN WARDS : Halton Mersey .</p>
        <p>Central 1712 Bridgewater Newtown Heath 484 2539 111.4 1195 THE TRANSPORTER BRIDGE. _ ......</p>
        <p>Mr Wilson asked the Cnairmaa could be tell the Ccuncil when the work was going to begin ? The Guttman replied that the Local Government Board had given their consent to the undertaking, and that one or two technical points had had to be cleaud up. Ai this was tow done the building would go ahead, but he had no authority to giVB definite informa- tion until the L al Government :Board had given its final decision. THE TRAIN SERVICE PROM RUNCORN T) LIVERPOOL AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>CHESTER.</head>
        <p>CHESTER.</p>
        <p>Mr Wiswall informed the Council that he and the Chairman had waited upon Mr Dennett at L'me—itreet Station tut they had not succeeded in getting what they asked for. Additional trains could not be run to Chester and Liverpool without additional expense which the London and North Western Railway Company was not willing to incur. I Mr Dean pointed out that when he wished to go to Chester for a t welve o'clock engagement he bad to leave his home at 9 am. Mr Imison sympathised with Mr D?sn and others who suffered inconvenience by reason of bad train connec- tion and it was decided to again approach the London • and North Western Railway Ootnnany with a view to remedying the existing state of affairs.</p>
        <p>THE WAIL ME3WEIAL FUND. The Committee which have had in hand the arrangements for presenting to the Yeomanry and Vol?nteers, who have so far returned from South Africa, the public memorials, held their final meet- ing at the Town Hall, Earlestovvn, on Monday even- ing. Mr R Barton presided, and several accounts were passed for payment. It was reported that there was a sufficient balance from the fund to pre- sent similar tokens to the local Yeomanry and Volunteers still at the front, when they return hotne.—The Lancashire Watch Company, from ,Whom the watches were obtained, have invited the Committee to have a look through their works at Preecot next tiaturday, and the invitation has been eCeepted.—Votes of thanks were accorded to Mr W Hindley (treasurer) and Messrs Mawdsley and Mayor (secretaries).—Thanks were also tendered to the Town Hall keepers of Newton and Earlestown for the preparations they had made for the meet- -1 inge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>ANNUAL REPORT OF THE RUNCORN TECHNICAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL REPORT OF THE RUNCORN TECHNICAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At the Council meeting on Tuesday the above com- mittee presentad its annual report which showed that in 1900 there were 194 pupils against 103 in the previous year. The students fees amounted to £377 7s 63 com- pared :with' £348 03 81 for the previous year. The grant of the Board of Education was £331 03 93 against £299 5a 63 last year. The grant from the Cheshire County Council was £lOO. With reference to the even- ing science classes tne number of students on the registers was 158 compared with 197 last year, 100 were examined 34 being successful against 133 and 61 last year. The grant from the County Council was £393 an advance of £6B co the one previously made and a further grant of £23 had also been made. The Board of EducAlkon grants were £7B 7a 83 for Science and £5O 183 63 for Art Students fees came to £6O 14s 61, an increase of £lO 13s, leaving a debit balance of £37 2343. The Institute had not recovered its financial position owing to baying the new wing built. In the statement of accounts the principal items of expenditure were £1,031 183 51. salaries of teachers ; £llO 163 43, cleaning, etc ; £165 193 B.3,balance owing to theitreasurer(l9oo/ ;£l4B 9a 4d, apparatus and hooks. Tne principal receipts were £547 151 91, £lB4, reie by Local Authority ; £460 6. 113, grants ; £438 24 fees ; leaving a balance of £3723 41.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>DEATH OF A PROMISING RUNCORN</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A PROMISING RUNCORN</p>
        <p>YOUTH.</p>
        <p>We greatly regret to announce the death on Monday evening of Mr James Walker a young man of 21 who succumbed after a short illness to a severe attack of typkoid fever. The deceased, though young in years, had already won the esteem or the majority of his townsmen and was one of the most popular and promis- ing young men in Runcorn. Becoming converted some five or six years ago he associated himself with the Wesleyan Methodist body at St. Paul's. His talents were almost immediately recognized by the officials connected with the church and shortly after being a teacher in Halton-road Wesleyan Sunday School he was asked to stand as a local preacher. He worked strenuously at his theological studies and though engaged in work of a most laborious nature he was successful in passing the examination connected with the local preachers meeting with flying colours. For some two years he was a fully accredited local preacher and was on the point of entering upon further examina- tions with a view of entering the itinerant ministry when the fellx disease of typhoid laid hold of him with fatal results. As a preacher be was one of the most successful on the Wesleyan Methodist plan being gifted with a rare mode of expres- sion comtoined with originality of thought. There is no doubt that had Mr. Walker been spared to have run through his college course he would have been one of whom Runcornians in general and Methodists in particular would bave been justly proud. He was connected with all the organization of ordinary church work and was one of the leading members of the Brunswick Mutual Improvement Class. His early densise:has caused widespread sympathy with nis sorruvring parents. The remains of the late Mr J Walker were interred at the Runcorn cemetery on Thursday afternoon. The cortege left the hospital, afterwards callinz at the de- ceased's residence. There were representatives from St Paul's Bruns wick Sunday School, Halton-road Sunday School, the Brunswick Mutual Imp-ovement class and the Wesley Guild. A number of beautiful wreaths were sent and there a very large number of people was present.</p>
      </div>
