      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>DROWNING MYSTERY AT ST HELEN.</p>
        <p>I A particularly sad tragedy was brought to light at St Helens on Toursday moraine, the victim boing a young woman of 23, named Mary Jane Carroll, of Lyon-street. She was to have been married on Saturday morning last to a militiaman named Dennis Kllv, who had just returned from South Africa, and they met on Friday night to complete preparations. She was not seen again, and on Thursday morning her body was found in the canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>An open meeting of the senior Bible classes in con- nection with this church was held on Sunday, Mr J W Hayward presiding over a large gathering of membns and friends. G-uncill,r James h vans gave an address on "The Bible," and Mr Tarn Hanson, of the Manches- ter School of Music,sang "The Llst Chord" and " R 'eh cf ages." Tae coe.gregatinn were charmed by the beauty and sweetness of Mr Gal:13011'9 rendering of "The Lost Chord," but every heart was touched by the deep feeling with which he sang "Rack of Ages." In the evening service Mr Hanson sang, "Abide with me," and "Rock of Ag.es (by request).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Lymm Higher-lane Baptists.</head>
        <p>Lymm Higher-lane Baptists.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services at the above church were held on Sunday, when the preacher both in tne morning and evening was the Rev J W Vialey, of S; Anne's. Special hymns were song, and in the morn- inc the anthems " 0 worship the Ring" (Maunder) and " Praise che Lord" (E V Hall) were well rendered. In the evening "Praise the Lord,o Jerusalem " (Maunder) and " Hark, hark my soul " (Shelley) were sung. The s)los were taken by Miss Taylor (soprano),Mrs Hannan (contralto), and Mr S Hass&amp;ll (tenot). The building had been appropriately decorated by Misses D ivies, Gibbon and Brundrett, and Mrs Rogerson. and Messrs N K Roger ,on, George Diamond and I W Globon. _.. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Spiritua" list-Church. Warrington.</head>
        <p>Spiritua" list-Church. Warrington.</p>
        <p>Toe harvest thanksgiving servioes in connection with the Spiritualist Church, Bewsey-street were held on Sunday. The hall had been very effectively decorated, the platform receiving Ppeaial attention. Over the front were three arches composed of ivy, &amp;0., among which were arranged bright fl)wers, grapes, apples, pears, &amp;o. Oa the background of fhwers were the letters "0.P.5." (Order of Progressive Spiritualists) arranged in small onions, and in front of the platform a table bearing an abundant supply of bread, fi)wers, fruit, and vegetables arranged to the beat advantage. The windows also were profusely decorated.and altogether a capital display was the result., Mr W Johnson, of Hyde. was the speaker. We subject in the afternoon was " Nature's divine reve- lations," and at night" Some thoughts on hu,nan destiny," on which he based two thoughtful addresses. A choir, conducted by Mr Foster, led very Effectively a number of harvest hymns, and also sang an anthem, " Daughter of Zion." At night the hall was well filed, and the services were very bright and hearty. On Mon. Jay evening a tea meeting was held, succeeded by an entertainment at which a number of songs, instru- mental solos and recitations were given, every item being much enjoyed. Afterwards the fruit and vege- table were sold for the benefit of the funds of the society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>CHARLES COLLETTE IN "WHAT HAPPENED TO JUNES."</head>
        <p>CHARLES COLLETTE IN "WHAT HAPPENED TO JUNES."</p>
        <p>Those will were fortunate enough to witness Charles I Collette's performance of the American commercial traveller:in the famous com,dy " What happened to Jones" when he visited us come few months ago will not be surprised at the cordial reception accorded to him on the occasion of his" flying matinee "at the Court Theatre Warrington on Tnursday. It is very seldom that the critic of farce comedies can feel as genial and sincere in writing the customary eulogies as be may in the case of" What happened to Jones.' The adventures of the tray.iller in playing cards and hymn books. who, to escape arrest for i attending a prize fight finds t necessary to masquerade as the Bishop of Rillarat,are staged with a skill which leaves not a moment dull or unintelligible, and, played with a magnificent dash and spirit. M. Charles Collette has achieved a picture of the American " drummer," which is really remarkable in accuracy as well as fascinating in its humour and heartiness, and to those who remember him in " The Colonel " it was a real treat to renew their acquaintance with so accom- plished an actor. Unlike many stars, Mr Collette does not rely upon his own individual attractions, great as they are, but as heretofore, brings a strong company of clever artistea to support him. There are no weak spots in the ensemble, and Mr Collette must have been most gratified at the warmth of his reception. We are look- ing forward with pleasure to a longer visit, when he promises us a revival of some of his earlier successes, notably " My awful Dad," in which his fame in London has been fully recognised by the provinces. Mr. Lilford Arthur is still Mr Collette's manager.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. _ Bold-street Wesley Guild. In connection with the above the opening social was held on Monday night, when about 90 members and friends met in the schoolroom. The programme consisted of songs, recitations Sze., after which re- freshments were served, and the company indulged in games. Altogether the proceedings were a de- cided success, and the social was a very good be- ginning of what promises to be a very prosperous session.</p>
        <p>NEW AUTUMN JACKETS, _ Mantles and _ Dresses.</p>
        <p>Latest shapes in WE.ternroof Coat., and Capes, Stormproof Coats a specialit&amp;—W T WILLIAMS, Sankey Street and Legb Street. Warrington. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOE UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. P.S A. at the Y.M.C.A. The usual P S A service was held in the Lecture Ball on Sunday afternoon, when there was a fair attendance- A hymn having been sung and a Scripture lesson read by Mr %V Brown, an address was delivered by the Chairman (Mr Henry Taylor). Other hymns and solos were sung. after which the Rev C Finch spoke upon "The Prodigal Son," his address being listened to with deep interest. The service, which lasted exactly an hour,was concluded by the pronouncing of the Benediction.</p>
        <p>_ BEST BOOTS FOR ALL—UNDER THE CLOCK.—Ad LAWNMOWERS GROUND AND REPAIRED. Green mowers Llways iii stock•—.T. S. GARLAND. Ironmonger Bridge-street, Warrington —Advt. Local Oddfellowship. On Saturday, the Loyal Garibaldi Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows celebrated its fortieth anniversary. The first part of the pro- gramme was a drive to the Ring-o'-Bells,Dare.bury, thence to Stratton, where the members indulged in a game of bowls for half-an-hour, arriving at head- quarters, Blue Lion, Market-place, at 5 30, where a capital knife and folk tea was partaken of. After half-an-hour's interval, the members, friends, and wives met for a social. Bro Thomas Wright, NG, 'occupied the chair, and he was supported by T Dickinson (secretary), PP GM James Smith, Bros W H Powney, V G, Roberts, P G, and J W Johnson, D 0 31, ot the district,. The toast of "The King, Queen, and Royal Family," was submitted by PG 31 Hands. "The Loyal Garibaldi," was pro- posed by Bro Smith, and the Secretary responded. Songs were rendered by Bros J Richardson, T Wright, J Smith, C Tasker, J W Perry and G Roberts, after which a vote of thanks was passed to Hc*t and Hostess Lake, for their excellent catering, and more especially to Bro A Bates for acting as accompanist.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC IS THE KING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, YV arrington.—(Advt•) ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL ATERS.—Advt</p>
        <p>The Conservative Club Bowling Main. A meeting was held at the CAaservative Club on Monday avening for the purpose of presenting the prizes gained at he recent bowling main. Mr R Pierpoint M.P., presented the prizes. He remarked that be was not very experienced in the game •of bowls, and he remembered the re-turfing of the bowling green, when be had the honour of bowling the first ball; and although he bowled it very badly they bad the goodness to applaud his effort. The game he was more accustomed to was the political game of bowls in the House of Commons. He fer- vently hoped he would have for many years the honour of representing their interests. (Applause.) Mr Pierpoint then distributed the prizes as fol- lows: 1. bronzes, J Holland; 2' cutlery, W Davies; 3, jam dish, j Pinnington ; 4, cheese, W Millington; 5, cruet, R Dutton; 6, kettle, W Strettle ; 7, thim- ble, L Aukland ; 8, cruet, S Woods; 9, cheese dish, S Grant; 10, half-a-coz-n tumblers. T Sutton; 11, water jug, Mr Antrobas; 12, celery cies,' Mr Barnes. Consolation Nfain.--I, clock, A Sid&amp; ; 2. cup, P Bradburn ; 3, knives, W Dooley; 4, cask of, ' beer, S Warburton; 5, bottle of whisky, T Cooper; 6, pipe and 2lb tea, J Genders; 7, sovereign purse J Harrison ; 8, pipe. J Gittens ; 9, wine, M Gurnell. The usual vote of thanks were accorded. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. PUBLIC BRNEPIT UNDER THE CLOCKYoR BESP BOOTS ui LANCA Shlßß.—Aclrt</p>
        <p>County Revision Courts. Mr Clement E Lloyd, barrister, attended in the, Large Room, Grappenhall, on Tue,day, at noon, for the purpose of revising the lists of voters for the townships of Latchford, Latchford Without, and Grappenhe.ll. Mr Joseph Smith (Knutsford) repre- sented the Liberals, and Mr J H Dean (also of Knutsford), the Conservatives, the assistant over- seers present were Messrs WII Woodcock War- rington), J Smith (Latchford Without), and S J Woodcock (Grappenball). Thera were 12 claims and objections on the ownership register and none, on the occupiers list--The Court for the Revision of the Voters' Lists for the parish of Stockton Heath was held on Tuesday afternoon at the club Room, London Bridge Inn, Stockton Heath the Revising Barrister being Mr Clement E Lloyd. There were also prssent Mr Edwin Bradshaw (assistant overseer)and Mr H Westbrook who (with Mr Dean, of Knutsford) repre,ented the Conserva- tives and Unionists, and Mr John Smith, of Knuts- ford, who represented the Liberals. No case of public interest occurred, btr. Mr Smith was over- ruled on an objection he made to the dates of the claim forms of certain lodgers, and their claims were allowed. The Revising Barrister, at the close, paid Mr Bradshaw a well-deserved compliment, by saying that his iist did him credit—there being no claims and no objections to the occupiers' list.— The Court for vevision of voters for Appleton and Stretton parishes was also held on Tuesday after- noon. Mr John Ellison, assistant overseer, was present, and Mr Westbrook and Mr Dean represen- ted the Conservatives, and Mr Smith, of Knutstord, the Liberals.</p>
