        <p>From Dr. B. J. BotTLTON AND CO., Horneastie. We have made pretty extensive use of Chlorodyue in our practice lately and look upon it as an excellent direct Seda- tive and Anti-spasmodic. It seems to allay pain and irrita- tion in whatever organ, and from wh stever cause. It induces a feeling cf comfort and quietude r ot obtainable by any other remedy, and it Seems to posses . this great advantage over all other sedatives ; that it leaves no unpleasant atter effects. .• Earl Russell communicated to the College of Physlciana that he received a despatch from her Majesty's clonsul at Manilla to the effect that Cholera had been raging fearfully, and that the ONLY remedy of any service was CHLOSO- DYNE."—See Lancet. lit December. 1864. Cl'o OF PIRACY AND IMITA- TIONS. CaUTlols.—Vice-Ohancellor Slr W. PAGE WOOD Stated that Dr. J. COLLIS BRoWSE was, undoubtedly, the inventor cf "ONLORODYNE " aim that the story of the defendant FREEMAN, was deliberately untrue, which he regretted to say bad been sworn to.—See Zintes, 13th July. 1864. • Sold in bottles at Is. 4,1.. Ea. 9d., and 45. 6d. None la genuine without the words "OR. J. COLLIS BROWNE'S CHLORODYBR "on the Government Stamp. Overwhelm, leg Medical testimony accompanies each bottle</p>
