      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>S7SDAY AFTERNOON SCENE NEAR BANK QUAY STATION.</p>
        <p>-BOOKING CLERK ASAIILTED.</p>
        <p>—„-- „ William Keily,wini resides at 19, King-streer,appeared Irfore Mr F Monks and other magistrates at the Warrington Police Court on Monday, in answer to a summons charging him with having been drunk and disorderly in Park, r-street on Sunday, September 22nd, and assaulting William Barrows, assistant booki.ig c!ere at Bank Quay Station.—Mr Steel represented the defendant. —P.C. Clough said he was on du'y near Bank Quay Station at 4 20 on the afternoon in question, and saw the defendant and another young man named Collins, who appeared before the Court a week ago, coming from Bank Quay Station, followed by the stationmaster,and William Burro ws,a booking clerk. When they got into Parker-street, defendant, without any provocation, kicked Burrows severely on the leg and gave him several blows in the chest. Witness interfered and separated the men, but when they got into Thynne- street they again became disorderly and made 1192 01 very bad language. Be was obliged to lock Collies up. Both the men were drunk.—William Burrows gave similar evidence and added that his leg was kicked so severely as to cause it to bleed.—William Henry Aspinall, sta'ionmaster, said he was on duty at the station on Sunday, September 22nd, and at about twenty minutes past four the young lady from the re- freshment room came to him and asked him to request defendant and Lis companion to leave the refreshment room, as they ere very disordelly. The men then came out and created a die- turbance. As they would not go away be called upon Burrows and Two porters to help him. When they got tkiem outside, defendant rushed at Burrows, and kicked and struck him several times. Eventually the officer came up and separated them.—For the defence, Mr Steel said that defendant and his companion had been to the station to see off a baggage man connected with a theatrical company. They went into the refreshment room a.nd ' defendant had four bottles of beer. He was not aeonstnmed to having drink and the beer seemed to have overcome him. They were ejected from the refreshment r-,om, and when they got outside. Kelly, according to his s-aternent, was tanned" by Burrows. He lost his temper and assaulted Burrows, and he now regretted his conduct.—The magi,trates tined del:el:lda:it 5i and costs for the assault (E 1 15s in all).</p>
        <p>IMPOSING UPON A Ptremoas. At the Warrineton County Petty Sessions eri Wo.3l3pc_ day, before Captain Withington (in the chair) and Mr John White, a labourer named James Nield living in Batteraby-lane, was charged with having on Sunday 22nd Seotember, by falsely representing himself to be a traveller, obtained a pint of beer from the Longford Hotel, Longford, during prohibited hours namely 9.55 a.m. Torre was a similar charge against a man named John Millatt, cf Havdock-stree, but he failed to put in an appearance..—Joseph Batton, landlord of the hotel, stated that on the morning in question the defendant Nield came into the hoe!e and represented himself as a traveller from Widnes. and he was served with a pint of ale. The police visited the house at 955 and examined the pre- mises and saw people present from Ashton, Haydock, and Earlestown.—P C Metcalfe said he visited the house in comoany with P C Metcalfe and found a dr.z,ua men with beer before them. The defendant said his name was Richard Jones and that he had come from Widnes. Inquiries were made and it was found that he lived in Battersb7-lane, Warring:on, and hie name war Niel&amp; His house was only one mile and 307 yards away from the hotel.—? C Metcalfe corroborated.—Defendant said be had actually walked from Widnes on the morning in question, and he thought tin was entitled to a drink.— The Chairman said landlords must be protected against such men, who falsely represent themselves to be bona- fide travellers. It was impossible always to tell whether they were or not. D=fendant would be fined 103 and costs—The Deputy Clerk asked the landlord if he wished to proceed against Millett.—Mr Hatton said he left the matter in the hands of the Bence, but he had been informed that the second defendant Was a reservist and had gone to Seuthatrinton. It was decided to pro- ceed with the case in his absence,and he was eventually fined 10a and costs. Evidence being givea to the effect that defendant obtained a drink by saying he had come from Penketta whereas he had only come from Haydock- street.</p>
        <p>SINGULAR A FFAIR AT A RPLEY.---WARRINGION PAINTER</p>
        <p>_ VIOLENTLY EtSSkULTED. At the County Borough Police Court on Wednesday before Mr. Geddes and other magistrates, Thomas o'!Aalley was chsrged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street at 10 30 the previous night and Assault- ing James Brooks, a painter who resides at 27, Orford- e Latchford.—Brooks said that at the tirro in question he was :ring towards the iron bridge at Arplby and saw the prisoner quarrelling with a woman. Be stood there a short time and prisoner shouted some disgusting language to him. Witness then passed on and on getting to thP bridge beard the woman shouting " Murder." Be turned back and saw the woman was on the pronnd. He went to PC. Phillips and made a oomplaint of prisoner's con- duct, and prisoner seeing him talking to the officer came up and made a rush at him (witness). Witness fell down and prisoner tried to " gouge " his eye out with his thumb. Witness's knee Wa9 also twisted, and in the struggle he lost his can and tie. The constable took the man into custody.—P.C. Phillips said the last wit- ness was talking to him when prisoner rushed at him and krrcked him to the ground. He struck Brooks several time's in the face, and witness had difficulty in get'ing him away. It appeared that the woman was prisoner's wife, and she was complaining about his conduct and wanted witness to lock him uti.—Prioner stated that Brooks interfered between him and his wife,and "that was a personal way he had of chastising anyone who did so."—Prisoner was fined 5i and costs for drunkenness and 20s and costs for the assault. The Bench allowed Brooks 101 as a reward for informing the police officer of prisoner's Conduct, and same recom- pense for the treatment he received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>MINOR OASES. – Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. - Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Before Mr Monks (in the r hair), Alderman Heaton and Messrs. Hayward. Wright and Thornton. A LODGER'S EXEItANGE.—Henry Pilkington was eharged with stealing from the lodging-house of James Hermode. Hose and. Crown-street. at six o'clock that morning, a pair of boots valued at 3s. 6d. the property of James Parker. He pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable said the two men were fellow-lodgers, and on Sunday night Parker put his bcot3 by the bedside when he went to bed. When he awoke at slx o'clock on Monday morning his boots were missing, and so was Pilklngton. who also was the only person who ha I left the house. Parker got up and gave information lc Police-constnbie Whittaker. Prisoner was arrested soon afterwards weal ing the boots. He said he bad taken the 'toots b,cause they would wear easier than his own. He bad left a pair of boots of absolutely no value behind him. Prisoner was fined 5s and costs or seven days' hard labour. CsurELTT TO A Hocan —Arthur Boden 10, Frederick-street and Peter Mitchell 12. Edgeworth-street. were summoned, the former for working a horse and the latter for causing , It to be worked while in an unfit condition. on September 16. —lnspector Print, of the Royal Society for the Prevention Cruelty to Animals, ad Police-constables Monks and</p>
        <p>Houghton gave evidence to the effect that the first defendant was in charge of a bay gelding attached to a vehicle laden with chairs. It wa. suffering from an old wound about the size of a 4s. piece. There was a large quantity of blood and matter on the hair round the viol-Ind.-The defendants denied that the wound was so large as described, and said it had developed since coming out of the stable.-A flue of 10s. and Costs was imposed. • IMPRISaNEo Foe STONE TITRowiNn.-James Tranter, 2, Warburton-street, and George Pearce, 15, Longford-street, were summoned for throwing stones to the danger of passengers in Warburton-street on September 17th.-Joseplo Pozetty. property repairer, stated that on the I.7th of Sep- tember he was engweci in repairing Aimneys ,n arburton- street, when he saw the two defendants throwing stones. He was struck several time; with the missiles, oae nearly hitting I him on the bend. Damage was done ta the slates. PC. Lewis gave evidence.-The Chief Constable stated that Tranter was a very bad boy and !..ad been three times pre- viously cmavicted. once for stealing bottled stout.-The magistrat-s sentenced defendants to three days' imprison- ment Fact.. UNINHABITABLZ PRoPERTY.-James Grace, Potteries, Sutton. St Hel-n., wa. summoned ti show cause why an order should net be made upon him to abate a nuisance existing on property in Winwick-road which was in an uninhsb,table condition -Mr Stanley Wilson, deputy town clerk, said the property was situated on land on which was formerly a poi tary. The building in question was formerly used as an offize.but since the works were closed, it had been occupied as a dwelling house by an old man and his wife, who acted as caretake-. The condition of the premises, as described bp the Medical Officer of Health. was foul, offensive and uninhabitable. The Building Inspector was of the opinion that It was almost impossible to make the building inhabitable.-Inspector Flood said there was no proper ventilation to the pace, and the premises were in a most insanitary conditiou - he magistrates made an order for the nuisance to be abated in seven days, defendant to Day the costs cf the case.</p>
        <p>TUESDLT.—Before Alderman _Sutton (In the chalr), Shaw</p>
        <p>Green. and Hutchinson and Dr. Fox.</p>
        <p>A CHEAP Dog."—Thoyme genderson and William Rodaers, of no fixed abo le, were charged with sleeping in an unoccupied soop in Friars' Green at 12 43 the previous night.—P C Corbett said he was oa duty u ear the place iu question and on examining the premises found prisoners sleeping therein. Thev hal ganel airwttance through a broken window, an i on being asked what they were doing there, replied that they had gone for a " doss."—Botti priaor ers were committed for scren days. WEDNESDAY—Before Mr tleVes (in the chair), Aldermen</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Heaton and &amp;fonks.</head>
        <p>Heaton and &amp;fonks.</p>
        <p>IMPUDENT SHOP DOOR TEEFr.TbOrflfts Rovrlinson was charged with stealing from outside the shop of Isaac Houghton, pawnbroker. Mersey-street. at 6.10 the previous evening a blue pilot jacket value 153 6d,--The Chief Con- s'able said that two girls named Edith O'Neil and Ann Hastray were in the neighbourhood of the shop and saw the prisoner in company with two other men. His companions went on in the direction of Bridge Foot but prisoner stayed behind, and mounted a itep ladder which was in the doorway of the shop. He went up several rungs anl unhooked the jtaket which was hanging up, and took it away. He secreted it in an entry ne.r Mr Bolton's brewery. Infor- mation was given t ) the police and prisoner was arrested by Suoerinrendent Monaghm. - He was committed to gaol for two months with hard laoour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Runcorn Petty bessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty bessions.</p>
        <p>Inm:us—Before Messrs R Reynolds (in the elvtir). and F.</p>
        <p>J. Norman.</p>
        <p>A Anaemia TR ADEQ:u.s.N FlNED.—William Edgar Lea. iron- monger, High-street,ltuncrn, was charged with riding his bicycle without a light on the 14th September.--The defen- dant said that owing to an accident he could not ride at his usual speed and was out much longer than he expected to be.—Detenlant Was fined is and Is 64 costs. A PAIR op PitoFganoNAL VAGRANri —JOIda Carroll and his wife Teresa Carroll were charged with allowing their three. children. Thomas. Michael; and Nellie, to be in a public thoroughfare for the purpose of inducing people to give them alms on the 21st September.—The male defendant pleaded .guilty and the female not guilty.—Mr Browne on behalf of the N S.P.C.C. said that t e defendants went about from town to town sponging upon people under the pre- tence of selling a few laces for which he had A pedlars' licen ce. On Thursday they came tO Ruhcorn and went to Mr Cook's, the relieeing ofiloer, and represented themselves as beingdestitute. He gave them passes to lodgings in the t ,wn yet on the same night they spent over 4s in drink at different public houses. At ten o'clock defendant was found at Halton drunk. The woman was afterwards lock d up for begging. One of the boys, aged 17. had been to a reformatory. He had afterwards obtained a situation, bit kis father had taken him away to tramp the roads.—Alfred Carroll. aged 17, said he was tha sou of the defendant. He had ben t ken from a situation by his father.—By Mr. Browne He would go to Canada if he eonid g-t a passage out. It was his father's and not his mother's fault.—Teresa Carroll elected to be sworn and said her husband b longed to Warriagton. She always kept her children clean. It they would deal leniently with her she would go to the Work- house and take the children with her.—The female defendant Was hound over for six months. and her husband was sent to gaol for a month with bard labour. USING OBSCENg LANGUsGE.—George Warr 11, Warrington, Thomas Jones Benjamin Hughes and James Bostock were fined lOs and costs, or in default 7 days for-using indecent language. DRUNK AND Elfaoniacktr.—James Bostock, was fined 51 and costs or in default 7 days for being druua and disorderly. P.C. Nicholls prove i the case. A .Mlscuntyaus Boy—WilllAM Bradshaw, a boy of four- teen was summoned for throwing stones.—P.C. Bowyer said that on the 18th of September he saw the boy throwing stones. He had received a complaint about a window being broken. an the defead 'Tit had been Identified as the boy who had broken it. The damage was 7s.—After further evidence hod been given by the boy's brother, Hiram Brad- shaw. the defendant was fined. 2. 6,1 and costs or 14 class. IMPUDENT THEFT AY A :MARRIED WoMAN.—Sarah Barrett. a young marriei woman was charged with stealing a shawl, the property of Mary Ellen Booth. The evidence showed that on the 230 September prosecarix left her house for a few minutes sad missed the shawl on her return. On being arrested prisoner denied;ltnowlOge orate thett,but afterwards aimitted pledging the shawl at Widnes.—Prisoner, who , pleaded that she did not know wPat she was doing. was I fitted Vs and costs or 14 days. _ .</p>
        <p>.... _ A DiatpusEif WOMAN.—A yortng woman name d Maria livins was fined 5s and costs or seven days for being drunk. Defendant, on hearing the sentence, said she would rathsr go to gaol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>"SHARPSHOOTERS."</head>
        <p>"SHARPSHOOTERS."</p>
        <p>A Tale from Real Life.</p>
        <p>the typical Lincolnshire village of Little Hale, two mites from lisckington, lives Miss Mary Charlotte Porter. Sue is a dressmaker ; and having for some time been cksely connected with the Primitive Methodist body is well known locally as an enthusiastic worker in the good cause. A'• Boston Indspendent " reporter with an eye to good " copy " called upon her recantly to ask her if she woula recount the real facts of an experience through which she has recently pa;sed ; and she willingly acceded to his req sect. Thereupon she. related to him a wonderful account of how Chas. Ford' a Bile Beans for Biliousness had cured her of neuralgia and rheumatism with other complications from which she had suffered for years. Said she :- 1 " For twenty years I was troubled with rheumatism and neuralgia. I suppose I bad it in troy system, and • being ou.: dressmaking and sitting in draughty houses , would have something t de with it. I thought I should never overcome it. Then I had infle&amp;zs more thaa once' and that, of courae, made me worse. I have been in bed and could hardly move, and even in bed I had to wrap carefully up. The pain was chiefly in my head and round my ne&amp;r. Dace I had a vto eat attack, and it drew my bead quite down to my right shoulder. I thought it was a stroke. We called in a doctor, and I was very ill foe about a mouth. I suffered also a deal with my face, and seine of my toeth loosened and came out. I telt the pain in my limns too, and at times could scarcely move. When I did move I had sharp I atlantic g pains—sharpahooters I used to call them— which made me fairly hold my breath. At the same time I was sorely Oistressed by bad bilious attacks accompanied oy sick headache. With this combination or ailments you may imagine the miserable plight I was in. Doctors were unable to b iag ime real relief. So when I read in the paper &amp;DOUG the good done by Cnas. Forde's Bile Beans I thought I would try them, and got some. In a very short time I experienced good from them. They seemed to clear toy mad ; and the pain which had always been present to a greater or I, s3er extent, disappeared completely from my back and head."</p>
        <p>"What was till result of the continued course ? asked the reporter " 1 found I could eat better," was the reply, "and 1 bad no more. biliousness. frost has now quite gone. I feel brighter and stronger, and can wcrk well and locg without the o:1.1 feeling oi distress Th rheihnatism and neuralgia have also been driven from my system. My appetite has coutinued to improve, and is now better than it has titles for years. 1 am strong and health e; in every way, and my cure is due solely to Chas: Forde's Ede Beaus. I have never met their equal in all my experience, and I have used many medicines. If th,st can do me so much good, they can do others good too,and I strongly advise sufferers to give them a trial." _ .</p>
        <p>_ Ches. Forde's Bile Beans for Biliousness are without doubt the world's specific for neuralgia, rheumatism, gout, nervous and general debility, colds, bilious attacks, sick headache, indigestion, pains in the chest, side, and back. palpiration, areemia, liver chill, female ailments, constipation, piles, pimples, face sores, all blood impurities, and liver and kidney disorders. All chemists sells Bile Beans, or they may be had, post free, from the Die B2an Manufacturing Co., 119, London Wall London.E.C., for one and three balf-peuce or two and nine.</p>
        <p>On Monday Field Marshal Earl Roberts, V C, corc- mander•in-chiettentered upon his seventieth year,having been born on the 30th of Septernner, 1832. His army service ben on the 12.6 cf December, 1851, when he was appointed a second lieutenant in the Bengal Artil- lery of the East India Company's Service. He was appointed Commander-in-Chief on the 3rd of January of the present year, hut preliminary Intimation of the appointment was made a year ago on Monday. ACCIDENTAL ELECTEOCUTION.—A shocking accident occurred at Chatham on Monday, resulting in the death of an electrician Darned James Gray, in the employ of the Chatham and Rochester Electric Lighting C3mPlm9 Toe unfortunate plan was attending to the wires at one of the stations in High-street, when he accidentally touched the main, and 'receiving the full force of the current, was killed on the spot, There were many people near at the time, and Gray's death caused a painful sensation.</p>
      </div>
