      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>After Many Years. Warrington Beat Runcorn.</head>
        <p>After Many Years. Warrington Beat Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Immense interest was taken in Saturdav's fixture between Warrington and Ru icorn at Wilderspool, seeing that the teams were on practically level terms in the leagur, and that local rivalry was as keen as ever. It was said to b 3 13 years sinze Warringt:in triumphed over Rancirn, and hopes were high in the vicinity of the Wilderspool grJund that "paid" would be put to the account this time. Toe anticipation was realized too, for at ttin end of a fiercely fought game Warrington were leading by 10 points to 3 punts. Splendid weather favoured the fixture and there was a cr rwd of abut 7,000, Rucicarn bringiaz a good number with them. 13,41 a teams were as advertised, and under the charge of Mr. J. H. Smith (Widnes', turned out in thi following order. Warrington : Hallam, full back ; Isherwood, Fish. Dickenson, acid Harris, tbreequarters ; Allen and Burgess, hiiloes ; Boardman, Morrison, Ethic, FAI, Edmondson, W Swift, F Swift, arid Cunningham, forwards.</p>
        <p>Runcarn : Houghton, full back ; Jo es, Richardson, Butterworth, and Ing'esfr:ll,, thresquarters ; Jolley and Faulkner, halves ; S Walker, Lightfoot, Farmer, Woods, Dixon, Houg,htan, Langley, and Williams forwards. Warrington won the toss, and toak advantage of the wind and sun in the opening stages. Farmer Com- menced operations, and after a few exchanges the Run- corn vanguard carried play to the home quarter, where Hallam checked tee rush. 'slier wood was upset under the posts, and a scrummage forming here Runcorn passed out, but the home backs were too quick for their opoonents. Warrington got back to centra'again, but F Swift rather foolishly got offside and lost his side 50 yards. Runeorn attacksd with determination, and Jolley whipping the ball out dropped at goal, but failed to score. Dickenann and Harris Ichanged the venue promptly Richardson having to kick in touch to save his aide from disaster. War- rin.gton were now in an excellent position and the ball coming smartly from Alien, Dickenson and Isherwood passed rapidly across, Fish taking the ball and running in cleverly at the corner aini 1 deafening cbeers. Hallam kicked from touch and landed a grand goal, the applause being renewed. Richardson restarted and followed up speedily, Dickeason being grassed in his owd half. Jolley got away and passed to BaLterworth, who was safely held. Runcorn attacked hotly again, Isherwood saving right ontbe line. Jolley again secured and passed to Inglestield, but Fish cleverly intercepted on the line, and conning to centre punted past Eloogb— top. A fine race for the ball followed, Harris coming up splendidly, but Dickenson managed to save in the nick of time. Ft6h cenered from touch and the home forwards rushed in a body to the line where tight scrimmages were fought out, Warrington had a penalty opposite the posts but Hallam was unsuccessful with the kick. Then Fish dropped from long range and was very near success. A break- away by the Runcorn forwards drove Warrington back, Hallam being caught with the ball. Long kicking by Runcorn kept Warrington at bay. Play was of a desperate character and before the interval Runcorn got to the home line again. Ta.ir stay was only tempora:y however, for Allen dodged through and punted clear to centre. The same player carried the ball.to the other end where Houghton gat injured and Ruacorn had a narrow escape. Half time came with Warrington leading by 5 points to nil. J Swift set the hall in motion again. Jolley returning. Isherwood responded with a fine run but, was pui:hei in touch. Then Was initiated a monotonous s:yie of play by Runcorn. Houghton marked, and almost every trine afterwards. Richardson follawea suit whether he was pressed or had yards to spare. These tactics not only spoiled the game—for Warrington had to kick back generally—hut they lessened Runcorn's chanc-s of getting on even terms, End to end play was witnessed, Fell making a fine run late the visitors half. Warrington were soon sent back again, Jolley and Butterworth getting clear away. The latter pmsed to Faulkner who bad a fine chance, : hut muffed it, possibly through his knee giving way. Runcorn attacked and after bard play Jolley got hold and cleverly bluffing the home backs goofed near the corner. Inglesfield took the goal kick, but made a poor attempt to put his side on even terms with Warrington. Runcurn's score quickened the earns up and several minor injuries were rec.:ivel owing to the desperate character of the play. Jolley took a penalty shot opposite the home goal, but again failed. Allen passed to Harris who ran strongly into the Runcorn half. Quickly the hall travelled back again, and Allen kicking out weakly a fine chance of marking opposite the posts was afforded Runcorn but Houghton and Jolley both went for the ball at tbe same time. This Was Runcorn's last chance for iVarringtan forced the game aaain, and Burgess breaking away cleverly passed to Dickenson who took the ball grandly and dodged in under the posts. Fiati added a goal and the match was as good as over. A few minu'es of even play followed and then the whistle sounded leaving Warrington winners by ten points to three points. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Salford v. Huddersfield.</head>
        <p>Salford v. Huddersfield.</p>
        <p>The first match in this League between these eubs took place at Fartown, Huddersfield, before 4.030 sp.,c• tators. Safford won the toss and Su cliff s kicked off for:Huddersfield. The home team quickly attacked,and Deere was jui.t bmught down by Smith in time to avert a score. Bode relieved with it capital run to midfield, where he passed the ball to LIIII3R, who dodged several opponents and scored an unimproved try for Salford! At ball time Salford led by three points. Early in the second half Smith had his kick charged down, and Deere scored an unimproved try for Eutidersfield. With the score level, Salforo made a determined attack on the Huddersfield line, and Price scored a try,which Griffiths failed to improve. Lomas also scored a try and kicked a goal. Firal score :—Salford, one goal and three tries (11. points) ; Huddersfield, oce try (three points).</p>
        <p>Brighouse Rangers v. Leigh. lei'')lgrhwagreidttng their passina- At Brighouse, before 2,000 spectators. BrighouFe game. and at the opt,' atye eetd a thisb utlpi ore% ernutsehdi n machinery, but sutsequehtly the visitors improved con- siderably, and in the second half had practically all the game. Leigh's goal-lticking proclivities were again noticeable, Molyneux, Dunbavin and Johnson dropping one each. Leigh won by three goals and a try (nine points), to a goal and a try (five points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Bradford v. Oldham.</head>
        <p>Bradford v. Oldham.</p>
        <p>At Bradford, bef6re 15,000 spectators. At the inter- val there was no score. Oldham in the second half had the advantage of the wind, which was blowing slightly, and A Lees kicked high. There were some scrummages in Bradiora's quarters. Lawton got away, and the bill went from Civil to Spottiswoode,and a try was scored by the latter. Tiezzsy tried at goal, but failed. The game was once more carried near the Bradford line. Lees commenced passing, and Eluzzev scored, a try and placed a goal. Final : Oldham, 8 points ; Bradford nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
