      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Earlestown v Darwen.</head>
        <p>Earlestown v Darwen.</p>
        <p>Darwen emphasizel their posi:ion at the head of the table by beating Earlestowa on the Dar wea 2round by four goals to none, a victory which would have been more marked had the Earlestown backs and goalkeeper not shown great cleverness. A lead of two goals was ' obtained inside six minutes, and at the interval D trwen had the ;advantage by yet another point. Although, they pressed very severely throughout the second half Darvren added only one goal ; and near the finish Earles- town had a chance of soaring from a penalty kick, but Dawson pllc3d wide of the posts. Almon saved well in the home goal and Duckworth played a dashing game at back, though Crompton was tee more finished of the pair. The half-back trio were not so good as usual,and Ef alligun was slow in centre, and missed several chances. Dawsen. Quinn, and Cooper were the pick of the front line. Chew was brilliant in the Etrlestown goal and could not he blamed for the goals made against him. Hayes and Leigh played splendidly, and in the half-back line Whitehouie was very prominent. The forwards gave a disappointing display, and shot very badly.</p>
        <p>Chorley v Haydock.</p>
        <p>This return match was playrA at Cherley. lisydock we without 11i,-,xon and Gleave, Lltham and Bindle bsing substituted. Chorlev were first to score, Minks s„inz twice, Lanvton doing the trick after three minutes plav• Havdock then had hard lines, the bad only just being scraps from the posts. Yates forced a corner, which was wsll rea,....,d and the coore was ised. Einvaarth Er.oreci thrl clad for aodok, ad then Monks was fouled twice before the interval. Final, Bsydock 3, Chorley 1.</p>
