        <p>NTERESTING LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>MiUer.— Rath bone.</p>
        <p>A large congregation assembled in St, Paul's Church on Thursday last week, to witness the solemnization of the marriage of Mr Harold .liller, chemist, Bringe street, eldest son of Mr J H Miller, solicitor, of Grantham, Lincolnshire,and Miss Loutie Rathbone, daughter of Mrs Rathbone, of Warring- ton. The wedeing aroused considerable interest, the contracting parties being well known in the town The service was fully choral, and commenced with the singing of the hymn " The voice that breathed o'er Eden." The officiating clergymen were the Rev E R Tarbuck (vicar), and the Rev L W Tnomas (curate). The bride, who was given away by her mother, was charmingly attired in a dress of ivory brocade, trimmed with chiffon. She wore a veil with wreath of orange blossom, and a brooch set with pearls, the gift of the bridegroom, and carried a lovely shower bouquet, also the gift of the bride- groom. The bridesmaids were Miss Wallington, (cousin of the bride), and Miss Miller (sister of the bridegroom). They wore dresses of white silk, with real Spanish lace, and black picture hats. They also had brooches set with pearls andcarrie I shower bouquets, the gifts of the bridegroom. The bride was also attended by two pages, Master Eric Dickin, who wore a suit of white satin, and Miss Bessie Picton, who was dressed in white silk. The bride's mother wore a dress of black brocaded silk, and a bonnet trimmed with lavender and str4sl whilst the bridegro )m's mother's dress was of black silk. The duties of best man were discharged by rtlr Percy Miller. At the conclusion of the service Mr E Wilkes, the organist, gave au efficient render- ing of Mendelssob n's Wedding March, Toe wedding party proceeded to the late re4idence of Mr Andrew Rathbone where they were photographed by Mr Birtles, and afterwards adjourned to the Winmar- Leigh Cafe for the wedding breakfast. Over fifty guests were present. Later in the day, amid the hearty congratulations of numerous well-wishers, , the newly-married couple left for Derhyshire,where the honeymoon will be spent. The bride's travell- ing dress Was a crus.hed strawberry coloured cost- ume, with a large b!ack hat. The following is, the list of presents : Bridegroom to bride, gold bracelet Bride to bridegroom. gold cuff links and studs and spade roe guinea Bride's mother, silver tray, silver candlesticks, household linea, travelling rug and chiNyte Mr Miller, black skin rug, silver cake dish and knife Mrs Miller, ehegote Mr Percy Miller, si'ver smoker's companion Mr Pried Miller, silver egg cruet Mr Sid Miller silver dower vases Miss Miller, silver toast rock Miss Bessie Miller. plate basket Misses S and A Miller, velvet cushion Mr W 0 Miller, hall glass and brushes Mr and Mrs Kirupton, Leicester, butter dish Mr and Mrs A liornby Bathbone, silver centre piece ant rose bowl Mr and Mrs Andrew Rathbone, bra's curb and fire brasses Mr Percy ltathbone and Miss Ada Burrows, brass and mirror fire screen Mr Andrew Rathbone, jan., spring mattress Mr and Mrs Fre i flathbone, silver salts Mr Samuel Dickin, Mickleton Hall, (Salop). cheque Mr and Mrs William Dickin, (Liverpool). silver salts Mr and Mrs Walter Dickin, Bel:41104n, nand painted pans Miss Dora Dickin bread fork Mrs Bstnla (Whltchurch), copper kettle Miss Dickin and Mr Johnson (Shawsbury), cruet Mr and Mrs bans Pic.on, sugar bowl Bride's Godmother, silver spoons and cheque Mr and Mrs Walter Goodall (London) silver toast rack Mr and Mrs George Wallington,te4le cutlery Misses M and A Wallington, brass stool Mr and Mrs Mamie (Lea Hall), trinket set Mr and Mrs Calvert (Southport) silser flower and fruit vase Miss Theckstone (lressington), satin tea cosy Mi 93039 Cooper, silver spoons and tongs Mr C Roff. (Spain) soanlsh lace Mrs and Mies Pickton, rose bowl Miss L Skelland sugar bowl</p>
        <p>Mr and :Mrs A H Burgess, silver photo frame Messrs Fennelly. Walker. and Brawn, smokers' cabinet Mrs and Miss Massey, copper cake stand Mr Stanley Rail (Hebington). silver spirit kettle Mr Reg Platt (Bebington), biscuit barrel Mrs Ho!ford flower pot Mrs Mills, set of jugs Mr and Mrs Frank Warburton, table lamp Master Reggie Warburton. toasting fork ' Mrs Lord dining room table cloth Mr and Mrs Robson. (Morley), silver muffineers and mustard pot Mr and Mrs G C Thomson, silver serviette rings Miss A Robinson, silk table centre Miss L Dayeaport (Heyward), worked table cover Mrs Lightfoot, (Widnes), sugar and cream cruet Misses Campbell, cheese dish Mr and Mrs Winstanley. copper crumb brush and tray Mrs Hollingirake (Baalford) silver jelly dish Mrs Ashly Healey (Christchurch). sauce ladles Mrs Fitchew and Mrs Taylor, jam dish Mr V L Wahltuch, cruet Mr and Mrs Chappell, silver and oak butter dish Mr A Goodwin, silver cigarette case Rev 11 R and 'sirs Tarbnek, wicker chair MISS Westwell, sugar bowl end cream jug Mr and Mrs CI Brown (Widnes), silver kgg cruet Mr and Mrs H F Greenough, butt r dish Mrs S Albinson. cake dials and d'oyleys Mr Grime, satin eider down MrAlfred Clive and Miss Roserts,drawing room ornaments Mr J Massey, silver pin tray Mrs Lindley, Worcester vases Miss L Dickin, pin cushion Mr Fernley. silver sugar tongs Mr Dickin (Stourton), carvers Miss II Dickin, satin tea cosy Where Is Security in</p>
