        <p>THE MAYORALTY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parr Accepts the Invitation.</p>
        <p>Mr J C Parr has communicated his decision with respect to the Mayoralty. On Thursday last week. hisiWorship the Nlayor (Aidermanßoberts)received the following letter : Grappenhall Hayes, Warrington, September 25th, 1901. The Mayor of Warrington.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr Mayor, In reference to the requisition I have had the pleasure of receiving from your hands, headed by your signature, and signed by nearly aft the mem- bers of the Town Counoil, raquesting me to be Mayor of Warrington for the ensuing year, I feel I can make but one reply and that is to accept the honour, which I do, relying upon the kind and en- couraging assurance contained in the request that I may count upon the "cordial support and assist- ance of all in the discharge of the duties of the office." .</p>
        <p>h lace re ly thanking you and all concerned for this proof of your confidence. I have the, honour to be, Yours faithfully, J. CHARLTON PARR.</p>
        <p>The requisition referred to by Mr Parr, and te which the Mayor attached his signature, was signed at a meeting of the General Purposes Committee on Monday ,evening, after , a statement by the Mayor.</p>
        <p>The -MAYOR Said :—" I desire to make a state- meat in connection with the important question of the Mayoralty for the purpose of placing before the Council my views and the attitude I hese deci ded to adopt. bathe first place, I wish to correct mis-Aatement which appeared in the " Guar- dian " of baturday last to the effect that I was aware before I left home that the date of the Caucus meeting of the Council was fixed, and that therefore I wee a party to the meeting being held on the date upon which it occurred: This statement has no foun- dation in fact. The caucus meeting was duly held. I prefer to express no opinion as to the atti- tude adopted at this meeting by a few members of the Council hitherto professedly members of the same political party as myself towards me, but it does not appear that the Conservative party have seriously attempted to use their numerical super- iority for the purpose of doing me a manifest injus- tice, and I therefore do not believe that the intrigue which has been going on while my back was turned, and I was ill, has emanated from my political opponents. I believe that the majority of the Conservative members of this Council had they been left alone would have joined with my Liberal friends in paying me the compliment, which I am vain enough to think my work during the past two arduous years has at least entitled me to. At the Caucus meetinz a re- solution was adopted on a second vote by sixteen out of twenty nine to invite to preside over this Council during next year a gentleman whose char- acter and reputation are such as would at once justify the utmost confidence and respect Fol- lowing up the resolution referred to, a certain in- dividual who has suddenly sprung into unenviabte notoriety busied himself in obtaining signatures to a memorial to the gentleman I have already referred to. In passing let me say that any such memorial is, in my judgment, en unwarrantable interference with mutt is the sole prerogative of this Council, namely, the right. to the selection of the Mayor, fcr the time being. This remarkable document contains the signatures of some gentle- men who have, for the first time, within my know- ledge, shown ithe ;slightest interest in tee affairs of the town. Many others have written to me expressing their sincere regret at having signed the petition under the impression tnat 1 had definitely refused to accept the Mayoralty for another year Other gentle- men have gone a little further and said that they were induced to sign by representations made to them which they have since discovered to be un- true. The motives which have prompted those who are responsible for this petition have heen solely motives of personal animosity, and I leave them to the pleasant reflections whicti always 101- 1)w the discovery of such contemptible conduct. Now, I approach the main question, and whatever I may think of the action ot individuals, I desire to say in the most emphatic manner possible, that the gentleman who has been asked to accept the Mayoralty is in no sense or degree a party to anything which may appear to the minds of my friends and myself as in any way unfair or unjust to me. I have had two interviews with Mr Parr, at the first of which I put my case fully and frankly before him. and as I felt sure he would, he said at once that while in the face of the resolution of the Caucus meeting of the Council, and the great amount of pressure which had been brought to bear upon him, and his high sense of duty to the town in which he takes, and always has taken, so deep an interest, he should find it difficult to refuse the Mayoralty, still he should hesitate to accept it if I was of opinion that I ought to press my claims. to the position. I left Mr Parr after this i.nerview with, if posEible, my respect for him, which dates back many years, intensified by the courtesy which I received from him, but also with my mind in a condition of great indecision. I have since carefully considered the whole matter again in con- junction with my wife, who in this, as in many other circumstances during wy Mayoralty,bas been a true helpmeet to me, and we both feel strongly that my first duty is to my native town. The mere questien of what may or may not be due to we per-</p>
        <p>sonally is a small matter compared with intense desire to see Warrington and all varied interests and undertakings thorol and successfully :governed. To ensure especially in the face of the enor expenditure which at present confronts U. desirable, and in fact necessary, thiv eaab every member of this Corporation should indii ally and collectively use all possible energy 10 direction, undistracted by personal feeling Party spirit. For three or four years past', happy condition of things has, to a great 05' prevailed in this Council, with the result better and more work has been accomplished hag been the:case in any previous period of ell duration. It is because of my earnest wish to to perpetuate this condition of things that I come to my present decision. At My ' interview with Mr Parr, I therefore him that I would unreservedly withdraw claims, that I would promote a memorial from_2 Council, first bearing my signature. asking Ow accept the Mayoralty for the next, . year ni by every member of the Cou earnestly trust that this memorial will be sW laopypaelatlytotommy eLibbeyral friends to show their ' accepts, what I hopesiwginliinbge nioctlitianaudn:L: tation, as I feel sure be will, I will do a" power to assist him in his work. te whenever possible, and to make his year "` one which will always accord him the plea„ recollections and will reflect the greatest r</p>
        <p>credit upon the town. A short discussion ensued: We understani all the members present at the meeting elguf memorial with three exceptions. It was site/ forwarded to Mr Parr and is acknowledged letter accepting the invitation.</p>
