        <p>independent Methodists in ..,onference. The annual convention of sunnily school worker; in connection with the lndepc,ndent Methodists of Lancashire and r istrict was held on Saturday after- noon at Tetlow-street Church, Walton, The Rev R Voice, presided over a large attendance. Amongst the delegates and visitors were Mr J Crumble- hulme, president of the Connexion, of Bolton; Mr and Mrs Crumblehulme, sen. (Southport),Councillor Worthington (Wigan),Messrs R Berry (Swinton), Wilkinson. F.G.S. (Bolton), R Le and W Price (Wigan), 3 Inca (Lowton),Howarth (Ashton-under- Lyne). J W Berry (Sointon, Mounfilld (Warring- ton, Wilson (Nelson), J Maulers (Bootle), I Kendrick (St Helens), E J Crumblehulme (Bolton), Rickhou•e (ilorwich). A Davies (Sutton), T Back- house, E G Stafford, J Bailey, tc. The chairman propounded a scheme, passed on Friday night by the Liverpool Free Church Council, for another mission to be held next year about Easter, in the interests of young people, and to satisfactorily carry out the work they would require at least £350. Messrs Mounfleld (Warrington), Berry, Wilson, Maclure, end Crumblehulme spoke on "The great nafioral misQion to Sunday scholars and young people." After tea a public meeting was held, and conducted by the president of the Connexion. Several speakers denounced football as "not being helpfni to the Christian life."</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ACCOMMODA- TION CRISIS AT WARRINGTON. Letters from the Board of Education.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ADJOURNED LICENSING SESSIONS ihe Re-building of the "Cattle Market" Inn.</p>
        <p>The adjourned licensing sessions for the County Borough of Warrington were held at the Police Court, yesterday morning, the magistrates on the Bench being :—Mr F Monks (in the chair), Al der men James Fairclough, Francomb, and Hutchinson Councillors William Pierpoint, and John Roberts, Dr Fox and Messrs g T Fairclough, Hayward, Geddes and Toornton.</p>
        <p>THE REBTJILDING OF THE 'CATTLE MART" INN</p>
        <p>.-AN UNDESIRABLE STATE OF THING S.</p>
        <p>Mr BO.Wrie-ilt-li--ea-agi-s-tiliTes-v;oulci-remember that the application of Messrs &amp;reenill,Whitley and Co., to rebuilt the `• Cattle Market" Inn, was ad- journed from the ordinary licensing sessions one reason being that there was a grid in Market-street which it was thought desirable by the magistrates should be done away witn. Arrangements bad been made for the entrance into the neer cellar to be from the back of the premises in Golden &amp;rove. It was, however, found that the cellar could not be lighted without a plate glass wind)w was left, and the propozal was to fix a glass, which would not open at all, but would be simply to light the cellar.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : Are we to understand that that was the only object:on raised ? Mr Browne: Ico not know of any other. The Chairman : TOL is another maiter. Mr Browne: The Chairman, of course, did not hear the application. Toe" Cattle Market "Inn is an old house recently purchased tram the trustees of Mr kticharclon, tripe dealer, by Messrs Greenail The proposal Is to re-buiid is An make i a uo.lera house to suit modern requirements. Toe Chairman, Will there us any increase in the area?</p>
        <p>Mr Browns: About 19 square yards. Continuing he said there was au o:d elaughter-house at the back of the premises, which it was proposed to do away with. The slaughter house was licensed before .a certain date and could'nfot be done away with without compensation. Messrs Greenall's were quite willing .to do away with it and thus do away what some people coasidered an evil and a nuis- ance. There was also a butcher's shop adjoiniag the beerhouse, and strange to say there was a door leading from the shop into the beerhouse. There was a discussion on toe last occasion as to what was included in toe licensed premises. The shop was included in the premises at the present time. The Chief Constable : Uufortunately it is. 1 Called tne attention of the boleti to it last time.</p>
        <p>_ Mr Browne said the shop was in a yard on the licensed premises, formerly a coal house. It was converted into a abop by air Cirlisle on his own responsibility. _</p>
        <p>The Chair Man : To nit) a homely expression I should say it ha l beea " cribbed " at 93Dad time. Mr Browne said the hods must have a yard to it.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: A yard is a yard. Mr Browne: It is on the licensed premises. There is an entrancs by the side gates. The Chairman : Do you mean to say Grsenall's or any other brewers would increase roe size of a house by taking in a yard and covering it? Mr Browne: I did not say the whole of it, the shop is only a portion of the yard. The Chief Constable said the shop had been recognized for years as part of the licensed premises, and he himself bad seen persons go in it for drink. He had caled the attention of tha Bench to the matter on more thac one occasion as it was a condition of things which ought not to exist.</p>
        <p>Mr Browne read a letter written by Mr Carlisle in 1895, stating tbat the shop was built by him- self eight years ago,on what was formerly the coal place. The Chairman : That is not the question before us The question is whether the shop forms part of the licensed premises. Mr Browne: I think there can be no doubt that there is a door leading from one to the et her. The Caairman : It will be a very large increase in the area.</p>
        <p>_ 3Lr Browne said there would only be an increase of 19 square yards. All new houses were better for Sanitary purposes and police supervision than the old-fashioned houses. At Bedford Leigh every house hwas being re-built, and it would oe a vast improvement to the town. At the present time customers drank in the kitchen at the " Cattle , Market " Inn, which was most undesirable. When the house was re-built there would be proper sani- tary arrangements and accommodation for tbe ten- ant and his family. After consultation the magistrates granted the application. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
