      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Appeal by Church School Managers.</head>
        <p>Appeal by Church School Managers.</p>
        <p>A crisis has been reached inthe school accommo- dation difficulty at Warrington. In May last we pointed out that the accommodation in the elemen- tary schools was being sorely taxed and that unless some steps were taken to provide additional accom- modation, the Education Department would step in and demand adequate accommodation. The num- ber .of children for whom accommodation is pro- vided in the elementary schools of the Borough is 13,464 and at the present moment there are over 13,000 children on the school registers. With the exception of the schools in Town Hall Ward,which are in the centre of the town, all the schools are filled to overflowing, and scholars are constantly being refused admis,ion. The matter was put very succinctly by His Majesty's Inspector, Mr PhelpF in the following letter addressed to the Rev Morley Stevenson : NvEsr KIRBY, 12th Deaember, 1900 Dear Mr Steven- son, The additional accommodation needed in Warrington comes to rather a large order, end is, as far as I can judge, somewhat as follows: 1. Enlargement of St. Anne's National (foe which plans were, I believe, prepared nearly two years ago). 2. Enlargement of St Barnabas' National Infante' Deptrtment. 3. Enlargement of Latchford St. James' C B, Infants', or better still, a new Infants' School and the present room thrown into the Mixed School. 4. A new school somewhere in the district at present insuffi- ciently supolied by the Fairfield and Hamilton Street Schools. Of course I can only form my opinion from the crowding and refusal to admit which I find in the existing schools. I know nothing of the children—though I have , no doubt that there are many—who are not attending school at all. A really.saiisfactory estimate could only be obtained by a census of the children taken by the lotal authorities. _ _</p>
        <p>This cornmunication has been followed by one from the Board of Education which is very explicit. It reads as follows : Board of Education, 2od August, 19M, Reverend Sir,— lam directed to inform you that the Board of Education have under consideraticn the question of the sieficiency of public school accommodation in Warrington. They under- stand that a committee of which you are secretary has been formed with a view t f receiving sufficient funds to sunply this deficiency. I tan to inquire whether yourcemrnittee are prepared to submit definite proposals for supplying the necessary accommodation and to carry out such proposal without delay. The accommodation required is as folio vvs: —ln Marsh House Lane near Conies Corner. boys 250, girls 253. Infants 300 ; Bevrsey Road near Lovely Lane, infants 15') ; near St Bar nabas national school, infants 100 ; Wilderepool Causeway, near St James' Church, Latchford, infants 150. I am to state that if the Board of Rdecation receive no reply within one month they will proceed forthwith to issue the first notice for the supply of the deficiency. I hare the honour to be, your obedient servant, C. M. COWIE.—Rev. Morley stevenson. A reporter of the " Examiner" waited upon the Rev Morley Stevenson with the object of ascertain bag what steps bad been taken to meet the per- emptory demand of the Education Department, " The communication came," he said "as you will see from the date, at a time when everybody was away on holidays.l:wrote to the Board of Education acknowledging the receipt of it and informed them that a committee had been formed to endeav- our to raise a fund to supply the deficiency but at the present moment they were unable to prosecute their inquiries because almost all the people whom they wished to see were away on their holidays and therefore I begged them to let the matter stand over for a short time."</p>
        <p>"Since then," continued the Principal of the Training College, "the Committee has met and has drawn up a scheme which if carried into effect will provide sufficient school accommodation for ten years, The scheme will involve an expendi- ture of £l4 000 and several handsome donations have already been promised in response to an appeal which has %een issued. If the committee succeed in their endeavour to build these additional church schools, there will be no necessity for the formation of a school board for at least ten yeay. The committee are at present con- sidering whether the measure of support which has been already Promised is sufficient to justify them in prosecuting their scheme."</p>
        <p>In answer to further inquiries, Mr Stevenson said it was purely a Church of England scheme, but the schools would, of course, be open to children of other denominations. Suhloined is a copy of the appeal which has been issued by the committee :</p>
        <p>CHURM SCHOOL ACC lIMoDATIIN IN THE BOROUGH OF wARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>DEAR SIR OR MADAM.</p>
        <p>_ The question of providing further accommodation for the education of chi!dren in the Borough o Warrington which has been for some time pending has lately become acute As the result of a meeting, to which representatives of all the Church Schools in the Borough were invited, held on December 3rd. 1903. to consider this question, a small Committee was appointed to collect information and to report as to the best course to be taken.—The Committee met several times and gave much thought and attention to the matter. They considered not only the needs of the present moment, but endeavoured to forecast what amount of extra accommodation would be required to supply the nee is of the Borough for ten years. They unanimously agreed Diet the following scheme of work should if pos. sible.be carried out in the order specified :- 1. Enlargement of S. Ann's School, Winwick-roat 2, New School in 9 Peter's for Boys end Girls 3, Class-rooms addel to All Saints' Misoion Room to form New School., Bewsey. 4, A New Infants' School at S. James'. Latchford. 5. Further accommodation in g. Barnabas'. 6. Farther accommodation in the Fairfield district, Manchester-road. 7, A Small School in Christ Church, Latchford. 8. Further enlargement of S. Ann's School. They are of opinion thrt: this work could be carried out for £14.003. and that if It were accomplished no morel school accommodation would have to be provided for ten i years. They' felt strongly that If all interested in education In the Borough would unite to raise the sum, the task I would be easier than if left to each parish separately. It was also thought probab'e that the donors would prefer to give to one general fund rather then to be subject to frequent demands from different parishes. The scheme would not contemplate any interference with the workin each parish, which would be Carried out solely by the managers of the school, or by wJaomsoever they might appoint, but would aim only at handkng over to each body of managers such a sum as might be considered reasonably sufficient for the purpose in view. This report was considered and adopted at a meeting el school amnadnag ear s ittiodresoe vheursdcaoyilm Februarylttee appo2ilnstte. d 193t10 rise the funds and carry out the scheme. , nntat The Committee now ask your k Ind and earnest Considers'. tion of this scheme.and commend It to your liberal sup. port. Contrlbutirns may, If desired, be spread over a period of years. and maybe sent to the Hon Treasurer or Hon Secretary. A letter is appended from HAL Inspector, Mr B L Phelps, stating his views on the subject. The following schools have already received intimations from the Board of Education that they must provide furtaer accommodation s—S. Ann's, S. Peter's, S. Barnabas. S. James, . . '</p>
        <p>The scheme propounded in the above appeal, differs id some respects from the demands of the Board of Education. It will be seen that items I. 2, and 6, are substituted for the suggested new school in Marsh House-lane. Following upon the letter to the Rev Morley Stevenson, the Town Clerk received the following letter from the Education Department on the 12th inst. • Board of Education. Whitehall, London. S.W., 11th September. 1901. Sir,—l am directed to inform you that the Board of Education have under consideration the ques- tion of the provision of wiecoste pohlic elementary school accommodation in Warringtm. Th.v propnse to issue a yetice under Section 9 of the Elementary Education Act, 1870. calling for the provision of additional accommodation for 250 boys. 250 girls,and 303 infants in the Marsh House Lane district,and for 150 infants in Rewsev Road district. and for 100 and 150 inf ,nta near St Mynahs,' School and St James (Lalchford)Ohurch. respectively. Before issuing the notice, they will be glad to consider any observations your committee may wish to offer on the matter. I have the honour to he. Sir. Yonr obedient servant, JOHN WHITE.</p>
        <p>L Whittle. Eno., Clerk to the Borough Schwa Attend- ance Cdmmittce, Warrlnaton. The matter was considered at a meeting of the General Parpoges Committee when it waq resolved "That the Chairman of the School Attendance Committee be regiyist.d to call a meeting of the influential ratepayers of Warrington to discuss the above matter."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>. _ THE MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>. _ THE MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Interest is already being taken in the forth- coming Municipal election. The Councillors who go out of office on the Ist November next are as follows : Town flail Ward, Councillor W J Forshaw (C). Whitecross Ward, Councillor W Brough (L). Bowsev Ward, Councillor W Rogers (I). Orford. Ward, Councillor J Wilkinson (L). St John's Ward, Councillor J Bsird (C). Fairfield Ward, Councillor D S Duncan (L). Howiev Ward, Conan'nor S Davies (C). St Austin's \Carl. Councillor T Burton (L), Latchford Ward, C)unlillor L Ellison (L). It will he seen that of the retiring Councillors, 5 are Liberals, 3 Conservatives, and 1 an Independ- ent. Councillor W J Forshaw has been selected as the Conservative candidate in Town Hall Ward and at Liberal meetings in V 7 hitecross. Orford, Fairfield and Latch Ford wards held during the past week the retiring Councillors have in each case been selected as the Liberal candidates i for the forth-coming elections.</p>
      </div>
