      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>I.ttters to the Orator.</p>
        <p>The Mayoralty.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the Warrington papers that have been for- warded tome here I notice on or two letters sating that some of the memorialists signed under the impres- sion that Alderman Roberts did not wish to seek re-election as Mayor. So far as I am concerned. I wish to state that I never made any remark that could convey such an impression. Indeed to anyone who knows Alderman Roberts. it would be obviously absurd to mske such a statement. All that I did say was that the Couccil had decided to ask Mr Parr to allow himself to be nominated as Mayor, and that a memorial would be presented asking him to comply, with the Council's wishes, and I invited signa- tures to this memorial.-- Yours faithfully. EDWIN WOOD 3.</p>
        <p>Grand Central 11nt.1, 13.1 Fast, September 21tb, 1901..</p>
        <p>---- Sir,—As it le impossible for me to reply separately to all who have written to ask me to accept the position of Mayor of Warrington hr the ensuing year, I be through your columns to express my sincere anpreci.- tion of their goodwill and especially that of the 180 working men from whom I have received post cards, I am deeply PAn4ible of the kind encouragement thus afforded. Yours faithfully, J. CHARLTON PARR. Grappenhall eyes, OctclPr 2id, 1901.</p>
        <p>The Study of a Mad World. Sir,—l am one of those individuals who never have an opinion of their own upon any subject whatso- ever. I am always influenced by the majority. I believe in the " vox pomuli, vox dei "theory and plentr ot it. lam always living and learning—two very diffi- cult matters now-a-days—especially the former ! If I form a theory and the " madding crowd " thinks otherwise I instantly become " madding" and think otherwise. As the world (especially during a war) is full of " madding crowds," each mad with a theory of its own, It follows as a natural consequence that I am continually in a state of insanity. For the time being my mind becomes vanant and then I laugh the loud laugh of Goldsmith. This is a airy, for I believe that nature intended me for a thinker, and by continual mental revolution I have become a combination of weathercock and jelly-fish I begin to think there is nothing whatever in human opinion. I begin to think that all the world's a fool and all the men and women merely footers. But I was not ever thus. About tare years ago I had opinions—great opinions—opini,ne Imola Politics, B,cialism, art, religion, the duty of man and things. I believed in civilization, progression, thought, the brotherhood of man, the amelieratina of manners, the Mission of the churches, the Hague Conference. I be- lieved in England, in Englishman, in rnag.nanimitc,free- dom and fair-play. Tcen came the war ! Then came the "madding crowds"!—esoecialy on Welting night ! I beheld how like the gambols of an elephant is England's capering when she rejoices. Toe spectacle staggered me. I made an effort to retain my equili- brium and failed. The last state of the man was worse than the other one ! Bot I will be more explicit. Two years ago my mental condition WV; somewhat as fol- lows. I believed that every question has two or more Sides, and I endeavoured as a reasonable man to put myself in the position of Boer and Briton, with sore's little knowledge of their previnne history. I believed that Capitalists are self-interested (ilst a trifls) both nationally and internationally. And are always. in some degree, responsible for war nationally and internationally. Study of history teaches this. I believe that peace is better than war, love than discord, light than darkness. I believed in the common fatherhood of God and the common brotherhood of mar. I believed that England was the home of free soeech. Above all I believed that God is the author of all good and the devil of all evil. And that the angelic song of 'peace and good will to men' is the keynote of the gospel of Christ. I believed that Chriltianitv teaches unwavering non- resistance, weakness, gentleness and humility. And that in the words of Flail Caine. " Though Christ Himself was a Jew, and His nation was under the heel of the conqueror RP was not a patriot. He denounced violence and rather than have the sword used in His own defence. He stiff ,red degradation and death " Further, I believed that to go to the old Testa- ment for a justifiration of war, is as absurd as to seek therein for a justification of polygamy. If the one is not condemned neither is the other. Such was my mental condition two years ago. Then came the war Then came the jingo fever ! Then came the British defeats—called reverses, the British surrenders,—called disasters ; the reliefs of Ladysmith and Mafekinv. the march to Pietoria, the general election, the C.I.V 'S, the 'madding crowds.' and the Echo wren ! This proved too much for a humble individual like myself. If I am poor, I am dishonest as Billy Richardson would have said. Nevertheless, always ready to learn, I open-el my mouth aro drank in the inspired sentiments of Vox t'opuli. Vox lives in big hous79, presides at schoo•-boards, preanhes in pulpits or becomes a magistrate. Popuii runs about the streets ; smokes a dirty clay and lets Vox do it. Vox and Pop however were unanimous upon the subject of the war and they taught me things. These are some of the un- compromising thinge they taught me and I defy anyone to refute • them. That God is the author of war. That free speech is unpatriotic and not to be permitted under penalty cf rotten eggs, a broken head and a sacked homestead. That non-resistance and Christianity are impracticable folly. That because war always has been it always will be Moe argument that) that War is the noblest most elevating, the pleasantest, and most glorious diversion, and the 'Proper study of mankind. That Roberts as an entity is greater than Telsto•,land by deduction that Manna- lomis is greater than PAWL That this war,unfilte every other has no capitalistic interests, no question of diamond-fields ad vold-mines. no connection with the Stock Evehancra. That Rhodes, Reit and Co., Kynoch and the rest of 'em are disinterested patriots whose only fault is that they think in Empires. That the capitalist who in spite of an enlightened and newspapsr public, oppresses the freeborn labourer at Grimsby and Penrhyn, will in the wilds of Africa. where there Is no check of public opinion, treat the savage Kaffir like a father.and protect a Basuto like a grandmother. Such, Mr Editor are some of the sentiments of Vox Populi which havereduced me tolvacancy. Vox and Pp have been too inneh for MB. So much so, that I almost say to them, Vox you are a fraud and poc_vnu. lie ! But I have no opinions whatever. I merely hear their sentiments—compare them with cny own—stagger —and laugh ! Vtitti whiCh sir, : beg to remain yours eincerely,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>.L.EIOIIMt Qt7l MT. The National Trust and the Shore of Derwentwater.</head>
        <p>.L.EIOIIMt Qt7l MT. The National Trust and the Shore of Derwentwater.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your readers will rejoice with the Council of I the National Trust, to hear that the purchase price of the Brandelhow Estate on the Western shore of Der- wentsvater has been raised, and that, after paying the necessary expenses entailed in carrying out the scheme there will remain some moneys in hand to co to a per- manent maintenance fund. It is hoped that this latter fund will grow. There will he a small yearly income from the meadow land and from the necessary thinning of the wood from time to time. It is not pronnsed to oulesrise the beautiful pnasersion by cutting walks or laving out grounds, as some have suggested, but there must of course be cer- tain expenses in upkeep. Woods require exnort atten- tion, and during the tourist season some ore must be in charge to see that the Estate takes no harm at the hands of the public, who will, we hope, ecj-,37 this lakeside paradise. The task of collecting the necessary fords has ham lightened by the hearty co-operation of the local sem.e taries and committees in Manchester, Liverpool, Birmingham Leeds, and Keswick, and by an enthusias- tic goodwill to the ornipct as evidenced by the letters of the donors. A noticeable feature has been the concur- rent testimony of thrsil who a-o foro.d to dwell " With sorrow barrioadosd evermore Within the walls of cities." _ .</p>
        <p>to the inestimable boon that even a brief holiday and a single visit to our beautiful lake country has been to them. In all classes—from the master manufacturer to I the man who could noir afford his shillieg—from the we'll-known journalist, iitterateur, or artist, to the humblest working man—there was the same scitness borne to the ineffaceable memory of a day on D"rweear. water. Ile-fore the minds of all, at times, to solace and to cheer them, had ariFen the blue-tzrey hills and the gleaming water-lino I. To " that inward eye which is the bliss of solitude " the vision of Catbels, and the Brandylhow woods and meadows, has been a joy for ever. It is this that fills one with hope for the future. If the workers of the lard have so learned the worth of natural beautc.there is likelihooo that the sacred shrines of her pre-eminent grace will be more jealously guarded in each generation, and the ration will be the gainer both from its inspiration and its rest. The Council of the National Tru et are encouraged to believe that not. only may some who have sub- scribed to the purchase be willing to remember that there is now at 1, Great Colley-street. Westminster, a maintenance fund opened, but may also with to be- come annual subscribers to a Society whose work they now resiise as its'eful to the nation. A minimum subscription of 109 will not only give the subscriber nowt possession of all the properties of the National Trust, but will also ensure the annual report of its doings. Finally. the Council wish to thank the Press For their Sympathy in this matter. it is the Press who have really won Brandelhow for the nation. H D RAWNSLEY,</p>
        <p>Crosthwaite Vinaraae, KPswicit. The GLEN-SPEY DISTILLERY. SituatP in the finest Whiekv-producing District of Scotland, is the property of &amp;A. Gilhev. This Whisky is made entirely from homegrown Barley and is kept absolutely unblended in his Majesty's Bonded Warehouses to mature, and sold by W. &amp; A. Glibey's agents in every town at 3s. 6d per bottle,</p>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>The America Cup.</p>
        <p>Victory of Columbia.</p>
        <p>At 1-41., Columbia was half a mile ahead, and must win, bar accidents. THE RESULT. Columbia crossed the line first, and won the race.</p>
        <p>The course was as follows :—The first leg east by half-south, the second south-west by balf-south,and the third course north north-west. The first leg is a bead reach, the second leg a close reach, and the third _leg, a dead beat to windwad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>Another Severe Repulse for</p>
        <p>the Boers,</p>
        <p>Heavy British Losses,</p>
        <p>From Lord Kitchener to the Secretary of State for War. RESIDENCY, PRETORIA, WEDNESDAY,</p>
        <p>The night attack by Delarey on Kekewich's camp at lioedwil,reported in my telegram noon 30th September was pressed by entmv, 1 000 strong, with great vigour.</p>
        <p>After close fighting for over two hours enemy were driven off with considerable loss. Our casualties were, 1 regret to say, severe, namely, one officer killed and one dangerously wounded (since dead) ; also ..leven officers severely wounded and three slightly wounded; uon-commissioned officers and men killed 31, dangerously wounded six, severely wounded 42, slightly 26. All these have been brought into Rus- tenburg and are being well cared for.</p>
        <p>In addition to the above about forty more wounded, of whom I have no detai!s, atilt remain to COCIII3 into Rustenburg.</p>
        <p>All the canoes are being reported separately.</p>
        <p>Colonel Kekewich, who was himself slightly wounded in two places, reports that all ranks behaved extremely well.</p>
        <p>Heavy legg of enemy at Itala and Prospect confirmed, and it is reported that 250 Boers were killed and 300 wounded.</p>
        <p>RESIDENCY, PRETORII., WEDNESDAY, 1 p.m.</p>
        <p>The two guns o U Battery Royal Elorse Artillery captured at Vlakfontein, as reported tc my telegram of 20th September, have been recovered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Local War Honours. Distinguished Service Orders for South Lancaehires.</head>
        <p>Local War Honours. Distinguished Service Orders for South Lancaehires.</p>
        <p>Local regiments figure prominently in the list of war honours publiihed on Saturday morning. The King has givenlorders for numerous appointments to the Order of the, Bath (additional). the Order of St. Michael and St. Gecrge. (additional), and the D.atinguished Service Order, for promotions in the army, and for the gra t of the nardal for di3riaguished conduct in the fielU to officers and 931dia,ni in recognition of th,ir sr vices ur- ine, the war in Stith Africa. The following are the local awards and decorations. To be Companions of the Distinguished Service Order Lieutenant-Colonel and Honorary Colonel William Cans- field, Lord Gerard, Lancashire Husiars ; Lieutenant Edward P.2ndarves Dorrien-Smith, the King's (Shrop- shire Light Infantry). 1 LINE BATTALIONS.</p>
        <p>PRINCE OF -WALES'S VOLUNTEERS (SOUTEI</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE REGIMEN r,)</p>
        <p>To be Companions of the DiFtineuished Service Order. —L'eutenant-Colonel R H Hall, Lieutenant Arthur Harold Bailey. To be brevet lieutenant-colonels.—Maj:irs T Lamb and F di Adam. To be brevet major.—Captain E T J tines. To have the Distinguished Conduct Medal.—Bergeant- Maj)r G Dsvlin, Cotour-S,rgeants E 0 Brie ,1 and T Simon, Private P Coulter, Drummer G Handley. THE CHESHIRE REGIMENT. _ . . . .</p>
        <p>To be Cmdpanion of the Distinguished Service Order : Major William Candler Neville. To be brevet major : Captain W V Mout. To have the bonorary rank of captain : Qaarter- master and Honorary-Lieutenant A Gregory. To have the distinguished conduct medal : Sergeant- Major M Foley, Colour•Sergeant H G Cowan, Colour— Sergeant Et Grayston,Sergeant A H Hope and Sergeant J Latimer, •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>MILITIA.</head>
        <p>MILITIA.</p>
        <p>3an BATTALION SOU EH—LA—N-61SHIRE REGIMEN T. To be companions of the Distinguished Service Order: Major Montague H,ath Hall and Major Alexander Francis Tarbet, C.M.G. To have the distinguished conduct medal : Sergeant- Major J A Altman and Quartermaster-Sergeant S T Boast. 4113 BATTALION THE CHESHIRE REGIMENT. To be a Companion of tne Order of the Batt) : Lieutenant-Colonel and Honorary Colonel Charles Harrop Beck. To be a Conwanion of the Distinguished Service Order : Second Lieutenant Evelyn Branscombe Flanagan (nom 2nd Battalion Cheshire Regiment). To have the distinguished conduct medal : Sewn.; Major J G Wills and Sergeant-Instructor E Davies.</p>
