      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1901</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1901</p>
        <p>the rainy season comes along it will all be spoiled. I see the statement made that the farmers can get and drm 20,000 men to remove their produce. I can- not say how this will turn out. The banks are evi- dently deeply interested in the movement, and will, I think, insist upon some one making a move this week. The leader of the whole movement, who I may say is a Norwegian, and has been my only intimate acquaintance since coming here, dined at my house to-day,and tells me it will be a fight to the finish. I think it will be over before this week is out.</p>
        <p>lam writing this on the hof September. The day is a State holiday to celebrate its admission into the family of the United States. Hai the in- formation brought to us through our morning papers been of the kind we were dreading, instead of holiday gaiety, we should have had sorrow and gloom as a people's expression of a great loss through the action of a mad coward. During the afternoon of Fliday the 6th, President 3113inley was shot, it was feared, the result would be fatal Thelbulletin boards as3ure us he will recover. Long ere these lines are in print the world will ,know h'ow far this assurance is justified. All our hopes go with those now watching at his bedside. WHITHAM.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EVERLASTING LABOUR TROUBLES;</head>
        <p>THE EVERLASTING LABOUR TROUBLES;</p>
        <p>[By Mr. H. Whitham.] Ta those familiar with the labour troubles of England, the strikes, lock-outs and what not of the United States, will have considerable interest, as, in a number of respects they are very similar. Novel readers will remember with what graphic effect Charles Reads in "Put yourself in his place" lays before the public the troubles of workers in Sheffield sixty years ago. Disraeli in " Sybil,or the two nations," depicts in forcible his- toric language the plug riots that played such havoc with trade throughout Lancashire and Yorkshire some fifty years ago. George Eliot in "Felix Holt,"and Charles Kingsley in" Alton Locke," and " Cheap clothes and nasty" also, after the manner of lingland's best novelists, draws public attention to the existence of two nations within a nation, or, in other words, to the truth of the old saying," that one half the world does not know how the ether half lives." One wonders what would have been the state of the lowly workers of to-day, if the same class of yesterday, or of thousands of years ago,had not been combine I for self:protection Since the days when the Israelites were ordered to make bricks without straw, right on through the ages of the servile wars when thousands upon thou- sands were crucified for the heinous crime of asserting their manhood, and demanding their full ' share of leisure and pleasure, and an equal oppor- tunity to gain their part of the riches ot the earth, until the present day the battle has raged. In San Francisco we have on at this time of writ- ing the greatest struggle between labour and capital the Pacific Slope has ever seen. Indeed, throughout the United States a fierce contest be- tween the two opposing forces is being carried on for what, to the superficial onlooker, would seem a matter of small importance,'or. a something once understood might soon be settled. To the worker himself the cause of all the trouble is simply the denial on the part of the employer of the right of the worker to combine This is putting the case in rather bald fashion, and the employer would no doulor, call it false. Well then the employer grants the workman the right to 'combine, but refuses to recognise the union You had a similar state of things in Warrington a short time ago. The reader may ask.—" What has led up to this state of things ?" For a number of years the question of how many hours shall constitute a day has been discussed by organized bodies. Dividing the. 24 into three equal parts has always seemed the readiest way of solving the problem, and there are few men of any intelligence but will allow that eipht hours a day is enough for any man to work. As a matter of fact you have, in a sense, educated your masters and the struggle is now on in real earnest, and if the worker now is willing to accept nine hours as a working day, he had growing within him a stern determination to have much less than even an eight hours day, and a much greater share of all that Dame Nature has to give to her children. So it came about that in some of the eastern states shorter hours with no reduction in pay was de- manded. Is it possible that rich men, and women, with their sons, and daughters think that the worker and the worker's wife cannot see, and are unable to read of the wealth of the idlers and of their doings? Surely it must be known that the Sunday papers of the U.S, and the society papers of Europe in their anxiety to lay before the lower class the daily doings of the upper class are but driving home the argument of the man in the street. In a number of cases the demands of the men were granted, but the real fight came when the ' unions approached the big trusts. How do work- men look upon these trusts ? Simply as powerful organizations using brains and capital for cheapen- ing production. So far the men nave no kick, but trusts have no use for trade unions, and wherever possib a And where friction could be avoided non-union labour was oreferred,and so it came about that lockouts became freqaent, the men being told . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>--0-- HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>--0-- HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>Holy Trinity Church, Runcorn,</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services were continued on Sunday. In the morning the preacher was the vicar (Rev C R Killick). In the afternoon a children's service was held, when there was a fair attendance. The Rev W H &amp;bole gave an appropriate address, and a number of special hymns were sung. At the evening service the church was crowded. The prayers were read by the vicar, and the lessons were road by the Rev N J Poole, curate, while the preacher was the Rev J Ducker, oi Sr. Ambrose, Liverpo 1. The preacher founded a most impressive sermon on the text " 'roe harvest is I cast, the summers ended, and ye are not saved." The principal items at the evening service were a solo from Elijah, by Mr J Findlow, Goss's anthem, "Fear not, 0 Laud," with solo by Mr EL Waterworth. Mr A Savage rendered the organ accompaniments with his usual</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>St. John's, Earlestown.</head>
        <p>St. John's, Earlestown.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services were commenced at St John's Church, E arlestown, on Sunday. The in- terior of the church was profusely decorated with fruit, flowers and v-getables,the gifts of the congregatioo and much labour had been expended chiefly upon the chancel and choir stalls and pulpit. The lady members of the congregation were well to the fore as decorators, and the result was eminently satisfactory. The preacher on Sunday morning was the Rev R Wolkenburg, 8.A., and in tha evening the Rev E Avent, 8.A., occupied the pulpit, the discourses on each occasion being appro- priate to the season. In the afternoon the Viaduct Prize Band was preaent, and played a selection of sacred items. Ou Monday evening the services were continued when the Rev W Bracecamp preached to a sparse con- gregation. Oa Wednesday evening there was again a moderate congregation, when the pulpit was occupied by the Rev H A Pomeroy. Ihe collections throughout were in aid of the church funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Earlestown Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Earlestown Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>Thanksgiving services for the ingathering of the har- vest were held in the Wesleyan Church, Earlestewn, on Sunday. Considerable care had been expended in the dee 7,ration of the church which presented a really pleas- ing appsarance. There was a profusion of fruit, flowers, vevetanles and corn, the rostrum being a perfect bower of flowers and fruit. To add to the attraction the Earlestown Orchestral Society's string band ha I been engaged, and the effect produced by the baud and organ in the accompaniment of the hymns was charming. The preacher for the day was the new circuit Superinten- dent. the Rev. G Parker, of St. Helens, whose discourses were appropriate to the occasion. At the evening service sacred solos were sung by Miss J Adams "The lost chord," and Miss L Barr s' 0 come Lit us worship. In the afternoon a musical service was given bAore a crowded attendare • the following being the programme :—March, " Silver trumpets," band ; solo and chorus, " Arm, arm, ye brave," Mr A Robinson and choir ; duet and chorus, " flail, Judea," Misses Barr and Adams and choir ; solo and chorus, " Sound an alarm," Mr F P Clare and choir; chorus," Sing unto God," choir ; inteanezzo, band ; solo, " The promise of life," Miss Holliday ; solo, "Lead. kindly light," Mr J Webster ; sanctus, band ; solo, " Entreat me not to leave thee," Miss Minnie Biro. ; Benedictus, the band. Mr T Clare conducted and Miss Greenall presided at the organ: The services were con- tinued on Monday evening, when the Rev R Passinore of Morecambe, and late of Earlestown, officiated. On Tuesday evening a musical and social evening was given in the Lecture Hall,when the fruit and vegetables with which the church was decorated were sold, the proceeds being devoted to the poor fund. There was ver7 lair attendance and a pleasant evening was passed</p>
        <p>that either their jobs or tneir unions must be given up. When men read of a million dollars being paid to the President of a trust as a year's salary they find in it much food for thought. The machinists and ironworkers struck because they felt the octopus of capital was winding its powerful arms around them and was determined to crush out the lite of trade organizations. in a number of places the trusts are now pulling down their mills and removing them to new districts. It remains to he seen what benefit will follow this strange proceed- ing. In San Francisco We have also at least 1,000 machinists on strike. These men are made up of the various trades:connected with the ship-building and are known as the Allied Iron Trades, but our greatest trouble is entirely local in its origin When people talk of strikes they say "it is in the air." They speak of the thing as if it were catching. Perhaps it is. We have all seen how men and women are affected almost to m idness during a religious revival. In California the formation and growth of unions may have been a madness, but at least it tried to have method in its action. The politician and the press were never Ired of talking about the booming trade. Millionaires were growing up all around like mushrooms. America was not only producing more than any other country, but was able to produce so much cheaper and better in every line of mechanics. As'a consequence of her artisans superior skill, and harder working habits she was fast, becoming master of the worlds markets. Again we have a logical result, men began to see the necessity for organization so that they might be in a position to demand an equal:share of the good things resulting from their labour. The workers were not the first in the flail to begin organizing. The merchants took the lead:and indeed became the dictators to all other employers. The merchants hand was shown' first when the cooks and waiters at the restaurants got mixed up with the retail butchers, and by striking upset the feeding business of the city. The whole- sale meat men refused to sell meat to retail butchers who employed union men, or served meat to eat- houses that employed union cooks or waiters. Wholesale dealers refused supplies to manufactur- ers in all directions. In the meantime the boss draymen had agreed with their teamsters that no hauling should be done for employers outside the Employers Association. Now comes along the Epworth League Convention with delegates in thousands from all parts of the world. The firm of draymen whose special work it was to move all the baggage of the visitors had more on its hands than it could manage and not being in the employers union w,-,8 in a fix. However, something had to be done, the bazgae was piling up at the depot, so the " scab " boss employer applied for help to the organized boss employers,and here came the chance of the merchants association.and one they had long been looking for. The merchants said the boss teamsters must make their men haul the Epworth League baggage, or forfeit their business. The teamsters, numbering between one and two thou- sands, voted not to haul it. and charged their em- ployers with wilfully breaking a written agreement It was of no use the boss teamsters pleading their helplessness in the matter, the teamsters all came out on strike. Scab drivers were drawn from the country, and, as every other line of industry is organized this brought about curious complications that at once resulted in sympathetir strikes. A large proportion of the organised trades are members of the Federated Trades, and this body at once decided that no merchandise of any kind must be handled by any member of any trade union. Sailors, stevedores and firemen came out, and then commenced an experience for San Francisco such as she, and, I think very few other cities ever had before. For six weeks at least 25,000 men have been walking the streets,while thousands of dollars worth of fruit and other perishable goods have - been left to rot. In their :efforts to run their teams the employers have had to call in the aid of the Child's Body Found in a Parcel. police. The scab teamster receives from 20s to 23e A gruesome discovery was made in Knntsford- a day, and rides a horse for the hire of which the road on Friday evening last week. While P.C. city pays 14s a day. With these two goes, as a Sennett was on duty there at 9-30 p.m his atten- rule, a clerk from the merchants office at 12s a day* Lion was drawn to a brown payer parcel which I have often seen four men guarding one team on was lying in the entry of a house near to the Primi- its way to the freight yard. The teamsters,sailors,and tive Methodist Chapel. He cat the string and stevedores of San Francisco are not of a very lamb. found that the parcel contained the body of a fully like character,and as a consequence it takes about a developed male child. It was taken to the Police column in tile paper each day to record casualties Station, where the inquest was held at noon on in the shape of broken heads. Pistol work, and Saturday, before Mr Brighouse, coroner. The in- rough handling make scab teaming hazardous, and quiry was of a purely formal character and occu- the hospitals have been kept busy. It is no easy job pied only a few minute; Elizabeth Caldwell, wife in a city like. San Francisco to drive four horseg.with of William Caldwell, 173, Knutsford-road, deposed seven or eight tons behind, and when the wheels to giving information to the police about the dis- get into the habit of ruining off the axle, and covery of the parcel.—Dr Smith deposed that he tumbling the wagon over just in front of a Street was unable to say whether the child was born alive car, it affords great fun to a crowd of strikers who, or whether it had had a separate existence apart just by chance, happen to be looking on. from the mother, and the jury, therefore, acting on Fortunately for me I have managed to keep out the advice ?f the Coroner, returned a verdict of of the whole business, although working right in "still born.' its midst. Should the strike last another three CADBURY'S ConOL Is a pure refined beverage, nutritious, weeks it will certainly bring about an immense s eotstimulating,tkm anrdda rddigoefsliiibglieje.stTphueri.t. Lrejorn't'irs,ar tp refpre- loss. Thousands of tons of grain are now lying in admixtures, such as ki)la, malt, hops, etc. ee Insist field , field and at wharf, and should it remain there until upon havidg CADBURY'S, as other Cocoas are sometimes substituted for extra profit. In Packets and Tins only. (4).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT]. FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Earlestown were again operating on foreign terri- tory on Saturday, and as they had as opponents the formidable Darwan eleven, a defeat of four goals to nil occasioned little or no surprise, Of ,course a draw would have ben far more palatable to their supporters, but a licking was almost universally expected. The disp:ay of the re-arranged forward line was vety disappointing, although perhaps the excuse might he urged that as it was the first time the men had played together, they did not know each other's play, but this does not account for the poor shooting they displayed. Dawson, of whom so much was expected, 1739 the most noticeable delinquent of the quintette. The latter player had .a splendid opportunity of opening the scor m - g against Dar Wen in League football when he was deputed to take a penalty kick in the closing stages of the game, but he failed to put the ball into the net, and tnus missed the best chance of the match. Thg play of the Eirlestown defence was the outstanding feature of the game, Chew and his backs behaving splendidly through- out the game. Chew saved shots innumerable in all sorts of positions and it becomes more and'm ore evident that Earlestown are alright an the last line of defence however lacking the rest of the eleven may be. To-morrow they meet St Helens town at St Helens in the English Cup Competition and it will be interesting to see how they fare again,t the Town Club with theirj ambled up team.</p>
        <p>Newton hai nothing more important on hand than a friendly at Nursery Park where they enter- tained their old rivals Warrington. Neither team was at full strength and the game was dull aiird uninteresting throughout. Newton won by thres goals to one and this about represented the dispar- ity between the teams. Warrington tried a new forward named Lipshaw, who is brother to the expectations by a long chalk. He start3d well by Sheffield United player, but he did not come up to opening the scoring but he finished at that. He does not pass to his witiZs at all but seems to have a tendency towards kicking the ball as far down the field as he can and then careering after it. Nine times out of ten before he got anywhere near ths leather, one of the opposing backs had sent it flying back to the other end of the fiAd.</p>
        <p>Although greatly handicapped by injuries ta two of their players very early on in the game, the Rovers managed to defeat Eluyton Recs. on Satur- day, by one god. to nil. Neither of the two injured men took any further part in the game, and the Rovers were forced to play thrise forwards, rather than resort to the one-back game. The result re- flects great credit on the play of the Rovers, as they had to work like trojsne to keep the Rees at buy, whilst very little combination wss possible between the broken-UP forward line.</p>
        <p>, •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>NOTES ON (AMES AND IPRESEN fATIONS TO THE ATHLETICS. I WARRINGTON CURRUGBY FOOTBALL, PORATION. The Volunteer Service Company's Memento.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON (AMES AND IPRESEN fATIONS TO THE ATHLETICS. I WARRINGTON CUR- RUGBY FOOTBALL, PORATION. The Volunteer Service Company's Memento.</p>
        <p>Two presentations have been made to the War- rington Corporation this week and both gifts were duly acknowledged by the Mayor at the meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening. One present consists of a loving cup, subscribed for by the Warrington members of the Volunteer Service Company who have recently returned from South Africa, as a memento of the event and as a token of the gratitude they feel towards the people of Warrington for what they have done for them and their relstives during their absence at the front. The cup is made from the case of a naval 12-pounder shell, fired at Gras Kop, in the Trans- vaal. Round the base of the cup is an imitation in silver of a stone sangar, while a girdle of Lee- Metford and elauser bullets on a projecting platform completes the base of the cup. The handles are formed of Lee-Metford and Mauser rifles crossed, in solid silver. The top of the cup is designed in solid silver, with a miniature of the South African medal and ribbon enamelled in correct colours, and two silver coins of the Transvaal. The lid of the cup is designed to represent a kopje and is sur- mounted by a reproduction in silver of the servant of Captain Aopletonen full marching order(Private Max Sixdorfe) who accompanied him throughout the campaign. The arms of the borough are engraved on a silver shield on the body of the cup, while the inscription is engraved on another silver shield, introduced into the general design. The work has been carried out by Messrs Eustance and Co., who arranged the scheme of design from the materials supplied by Captain Appleton. The inscriptions on the cup are as follows: "Presented to the Mae or and Corpora- tion of the county borough of Warrington, by the members of the Volunteer Service Cempany, Ist South Lancashire Regiment, on their return nome June Bth, 1901, after 16 months' active service in South Africa during the 13, er war, in grateful appreciation of the kinaness of the people of Wars rington to them and theirs while at the front." "This loving cup is made from the case of a naval 12-oounder shell fired at Gras Kop,Transvaal Colony, at 91. m, August 14th, 1900, where the Company formed part of a detachment of the regirnent hold- ing the Ladysmith-Pretoria Railway under Major F A Adam (now colonel), second in command let South Lancashire Regiment •, and Adjutant of the let Volunteer Battalion in Warrington, 1888-1892" The members of the Active Service Company (Ist and 2nd contingents) met at the Drill ball on Mon- day evening preparatory to attending in the Coun- cil Chamber at the Town Hall for the purpose of presenting the cup to the Mayor, on behalf of the town. Tnere was a ,very representative gathering of the man, who, accompanied by Captain Appleton subsequently proceeded to the Council Chamber, where they were met by the Mayor and the Town Clerk, Aldermen Heaton and Hutchinson, and Conncillors Smethurst and Rogers. Captain APPLEToN, who was received with ap- plause, said: Mr Mayor, Aldermen, Councillore,and gentlemen. —The officers,non-commissioned officers, and men from Warrington, ia the Active Service Company of the South Lancashire Regiment, have had the great honour of representing this ancient and loyal borough and their Volun- teer Battalion, and serving for a time in the let Battalion—the old Fighting Fortieth. We have been naturally very proud of that honour,and have not forgotten the grand example shown us by past generations of Warrington citizens who have invariably come forward and offered their services in times of national emergency. The occasion of our going out to South Africa has been of some historical interest, as it has been the first time that our citizen soldiers have taken their place side by side with their line battalion at the front. As a permanent record of this event, and as a tangible proof of the intense gratitude we feel towards the people of Warrington for all they have done for us both collectively and individually, we wish to prei sent, through you, to the Corporation of Warring- ton, a "Loving Cup," subscribed for by the War rington members of the Company; and should like to add that the pleasure of doing so is increased by being able to present it to you,who have person- alcy shown us (and here must also include your Town Clerk, Mr Whittle) so much kindness, and to both of whom we wish to express our gratitude for all you have done for us. I will now call upon Colour Sergeant Moore, senior sergeant in the er- vice Company, to present the cute. Colour-Sergeant MOORE did so,in a few appropri- ate words. The Mayor, in accepting the loving cup, said,—lt affords me very great and sincere pleasure to ac- cept, on behalf of the Corporation and the.people of Warrington, this very handsome present from Captain Appleton, the officers, non-commissioned officers and men of the Volunteer Service Company. I can assure Captain Appleton and those connected with him that no presentation of this kind wa&amp; necessary to further cement the good feeling which has existed during the past two years between what I may call the civic: and the military side of the life of the tuvvn. I feel certain that the action of the battalion in volunteering for active -service when the war broke out was a circumstance of which all Warringtonians were extremely proud, and one which will never be forgotten ; and turther, when it was not considered neces- sary to accept the service of the whole battalion, but a smaller number, the ready response made by the officers and men, which was tar in excess of the requirements of the War Office, and the equally ready response on two subsequent occasions, showed that when our Empire was on its trial we had in our reserve forces—hitherto largely unappreciated—a thoroughly reliable force. (Applause). Warrington has alwae s been noted for its loyalty to the Throne and its interest in every- thing which makes for the solidarity of the Empire, and that reputation has been more than maintained by the action of the Volunteers to which I have referred. I feel quite confident that the goed feel- ing now eeisoing between the civil and the military authorities will increase as years go on. (Applause). This loving cup, a beautiful work of art—and which reflects credit not only upon the silversmith, but upon someone who msde the suggestions which the silversmith embodied—will be highly treasured by the Corporation, and always be a feature on occasious of civic hospitality, while it will in itself bear ,silent witness to the devoted spirit of patriotism which was characteristic of our soldier citezens• The cup was displayed in the Council Chamber on Tuesday evening and was greatly admired by all present. The Mayor said he had to announce, and he did so with very great pleasure, the receipt from Captain Appleton and the non-commissioned officers and men of the Service Company of this very beautiful memento (a silver loving cup which stood on the Mayor's table) of the war in South Africa. He need not go into any detail as to all the circumstances which led toithese men,of whom they , were so proud volunteering and serving the country , for so long a period as they did. They all knew, and he thought all sympathised with the very good feeling which existed in this town at any rate be- tween the civil and the military element, and while anything of this kind was unnecessary to further increase or rather help to cement that feeling, still he felt sure the members of the Council, along with him, would be equally proud to have this perma- nent testimony of the events to which be had re ferred to, add of the fact that these good fellows had thought it desirable to place on record their own estimation of the way in which they were treated by their relatives and friends at home dur- ing the time they bad been serviug the interests of the Empire abroad. To himself it was a matter of considerable pride to receive on their behalf and of the town tots very beautiful present. (Hear, h ea r). • The second present is of a not less interesting character. It consists of a framed composite pho- tographic group of the members of the Council and the chief officials during this the first year's ex ist- eNce of Warrington as a county borough. It is the gift of Mr Thomas Birtles, who on three previous occasions has made a presentation to the Council of a like character. The MAYOR real a letter at the Connell meeting from Mr T llirtles asking his acceptance of a com- posite group of the members of the Council repre- senting the first year of the county borough. He would, he added, be glad if a place could be found for it in the Council room. Mr Birtlea, observed the Mayor, had on three previous occasions—this being the fourth—been good enough to present to the Corporation a photographic group of the mem- bers of the Council for the time being, and he thought it was at least due to him to acknowledge with sincere thanks this evidence of his interest of them, and also the fact that permanent facial</p>
        <p>records of those who were with them now and thoP who bad served tce town in days gone by were of very great interest to them and everyone connected wi,,h the municipal government of the town. tie had pleasure in moving that a sincere vote of thanks be accorded to Mr Birtles for this interesting gift. St.TTON, in seconding, said he agreed with all the Mayor had said in reference to the kindness of Mr Birtles in presenting them with this memento. He was quite sure it would be of very great interest not only to those living now but to thoie of many generations to come. The vote was heartily carried.</p>
        <p>HAYDOCK COLLIERY FATALIIY. Youth Caught Between wheels.</p>
        <p>The inquest on the body of Thomas Williams. 18 years of ago. of 3, Penny•lane, Haydock. woo was killed in the Wood Pit, belonging to Messrs. Evans and Co., was held by Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, on Saturday morning. Mr. E Pearse and Mr. J. Robinson repres:nted the pit owners. On Wednesday morning Williams was engaged in his work as a haulage hand with two other men, named Patrick Dowd and John Brown. At the junction of the main road and a branch road there were cwo pulleys to take the haulage ropes. which were going at the rate of a mile and a half an hour. It was Davies's duty to unlash boxes coming from the main road, so that Brown on the other side of the pully could lash them on the branch haulage rope. Somehow he got his fingers caught and he was dragged botween the ytilleys. big right arm being almost torn off. Re received other injuries and died in a faw minute, Just when he was caught he shouted " Knock hold and the otters sig- nalled, the motion being at once stoppe. It was stated that had Davies been unable to unlash a tub before getting to the pulley an electric signal was very • handy for him to stop tbe motion. There were signals for the other two hands. Dowd and Brown, however, said that they had had no boxes to deal with for several minutes. They did not see how Davies was caught. The Caroner said it had been clearly proved that deceased's duty must of necessity be at some little distance from the wheel, and as he was not unlashing boxes he must have gone to the pulley for some purpose unknown. The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>New Railway Development in Cheshire. A Wilmslow correspondent states that the new Cheshire line of the Midland Railway has jtht been completed as far as Cbeadle Heath, and will be opened at once for passenger traffic. The line runs fram Heaton Mersey t 3 New Mills, where it joins the main to London. The Midland Company will now be able to run trains the whole way from Manchester on their own railway. A great saving in distance and time in connection with the expresi traffio between the two cities will be effected.</p>
        <p>OUR NEW SERIAL.</p>
        <p>We have pleasure in announcing that we have secured for publication In our columns a Serial entitled— "A QUEEN'S SOLDIER," HT WALTER WOOD, AUTHOR OF " BARRACK' AND BATTLEFIELD," " FAMOUS BRITISH WARS HIPS." " THROUGH BATTLE To pßonionoN," " A CAPTAIN IN THE FUSILIERS," 'A SOLDIER OF THE LEGION." Etc.</p>
        <p>EONETEING ABOITT THZ AUTHOR.</p>
        <p>ME WALTER WOOD first became well-known as a writer of stories dealing with military life through the medium of the London magazines,whose editors were not slow to recognise the unusually close acquaintance with barrack and campaign life and the dry humour which his work consistently re— vealed. lie possesses the fundamental quality noses- sary to success as a novelist—the ability to tell a story ; and the reader's interest never flags.</p>
        <p>Of his first published volume of military stories the London "Daily Telegraph " said, "There are few stronger or more captivating stories in the English language." A companion volume of naval articles won the rare honour, for a young writer, of a special—and generous—article in " The Saturday Review," from that distinguished naval critic, the late Admiral Colomb.</p>
        <p>SOMETHING ABOUT THE STORY. "A QUEEN'S SOLDIER " is a story of Yorkshire and of the South African battlefields. Its hero is it trooper in the Imperial Yeomanry, who leave 3 his father's farm aad his sweetheart for the dusty veldt and the interminable hardships of the- campaign. The author's experience in war subjects enables him, without apparent effort, to bring before the reader a brilliant series of pictures of life in the- field. While he offers plenty of excitement in the way of skirmishing and of more serious battles, he never loses sight of the obligation as a novelist to give a human interest to his work, and the events et- the war are shown, not as matters of dull history. but as thew appeared to Mark Bonn, trooper in the Imperial Yeornanry,while performing his duty as a soldier of the Queen. Henn is wounded in a skirmish, and is taken as a prisoner to Pretoria. Events enable him at length to escape, but only to undergo terrible sufferings. Perhaps this portion of the tale will be found to possess the most thrilling interest for readers. Benn's experiences in his wild dash for liberty are partly founded on fact and they are graphically described. There are afterwards some glimpses of life in hospital, and the tale ends as happily as everyone who makes the acquaintance of Mark Bann and hi; sweetheart would desire it to do. "A Queen's Soldier," as the work of one who knows how to write intelligently about war, with- out overloading the picture, will, we confidently believe. be found to rank among the best stories which have been written about the South African straggle.</p>
        <p>The operdng chapters of this Story will appear in our columns on FRIDAY, OCTOBER I I T. 2</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>By " Ovalist."</head>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>At last ! After many years Warrington have succeed- ed in beating Runcorn. The day was bound to come, and there were not a few good judges of form who were confident that Saturday would prove Warrington s superiority, and it did. They won the match by 10 points to three points, and they were the better team, though neither side was seen at its best. Exactly how many years it is since Warrington defeated the "Linnets "I can't say. Most of the papers on Satur- day and Monday seemed to know, but none agreed, and striking an average between them I should put the period down at about 14 years. Even this may not be exactly right, but it is not far out. Perhaps some kind statistical genius will oblige me with duly accredited evidence if I am wrong.</p>
        <p>How true it is that " nothing succeeds like success " was evidenced by the crowd at Wilderspool on Satur- day. Warrington folk were well-pleased at the form shown by the team at Broughton and they rolled tip in large numbers to see the historic battle between War- rington and Runcorn. who are, by the way, the only local teams in the Northern League. A few hundred enthusiasts came from Runcorn even though every- body there is in a state of chronic hard-up. According to a Chronicle" noter the query at Runcorn " How's trade ? " elicits only the laconic reply " There is no trade." Doubtless this is a slightly exaggerated version of the state of things, or so many Runcornians would not have been able to come to Warrington on Saturday. There must have been well on for 3,000 people present and the gate' receipts reached the hand- some total of £175. This speaks well for the prosperity of the Warrington Club, and to maintain it the team has only to go on winning.</p>
        <p>Both teams were strongly represented and few harder games have been played at Wilderspool than the one on Saturday. Runcorn were just as determined to keep up their sequence of successes as Warrington were resolved to break it. And when such is the case the natural result is that the stronger side comes out on top. The visitors had a band with them but the martial strains did them no good apparently. Let them leave the band at home , next time if they have any faith in Omens. ,</p>
        <p>Runcorn were the first to attack and were not far from scoring before five minutes of the eighty bad elapsed. The invasion was repulsed, however, .and Warrington retaliated in kind. Once on the Runcorn line and they showed a little of the quality which has already carried them to the third place in the League. A series of quick transfers was too much for the visitors defence, Fish rounding the backs and scoring a fine try at the corner. Hallam repeated his Broughton success by kicking a grand goal from the touch-line. The home crowd were now on good terms with themselves, and right up to the interval Warrington had rather the best of a keenly fought game, though the " Linnets " made a number of fierce attacks which took a lot of check-mating.</p>
        <p>The best football was undoubtedly shown in the first quarter of an hour. After that Runcorn initiated a style of play which does not please the spectators and will not win them matches either. They went in strongly for making marks and punting. Perhaps they recognized that Warrington could beat them at passing, but that is a poor excuse for the methods they adopted. Unfor- tunately, too, Warrington followed suit to a great ex- tent and did a lot more kicking than was good for them. Warrington are not a team of punters and never were. No doubt it is difficult to play the game your own way when the other side persists in long kicking, and this is what Warrington found. Play deteriorated in interest though not in excitement, especially as Jolley got witinnltwo points of Warrington's score by a'fine indivi- dual effort. Near the close Warrington got their com• bination at work again and Dickenson scored under the posts for Fish to place a goal, thus leaving Runcorn 7 points in arrear.</p>
        <p>The futility of Runcorn's play was strikingly appar• , ent. Here they were behind their opponents and must ' needs go in for making marks. I have seen many a game won by it, but never by the team whose score was in a minority. It is a safe policy when you have the lead. Richardson was a terrible sinner in this respect. He took nearly all the catches and he marked almost every time wherever it was. He was cheered ironically very time he ran instead. Undoubtedly he was the] best catcher on the field, and on the day's play the worst footballer. In my opinion he did more to lose the game for Runcorn than anything else you could think of. Time was when Runcorn delighted in the art of passing and running, and now when it is more necesary than ever they appear to have forsaken it for the old-fashioned kick and rush game. A poor season is in prospect for them if they keep to Saturday's methods, and moreover, Warrington will beat them at Runcorn unless they vastly improve.</p>
        <p>The Warrington threequarter backs were well-marked by-Runcorn and rather ill-served by their halves. Burgess passed badly and but for the opening he made for Dickenson—an opening remarkably well taken—he would have to be voted a failure. Allen did much the more valuable work, and improves each week. The three• quarters must be kept moving; however, if Warrington are to keep on winning. It is not their sole business to kick and follow-up. They were all good, though Isherwood was not at his best in fielding the ball. Fish, though almost neglected in the second half, played well—as he always does against Runcorn. His try was a beauty, and needed a bit of getting. Forward and full back the teams were exceedingly well matched. Houghton got his side out of many a difficulty and Hallam was certainly the saviour of Warrington on many occasions. Jolley also did fine work, but he was too selfish for my liking. The Runporn threequarters seemed to me only an average lot,and with the excep- tion of Butterworth much below the class of the home quartette. In tackling and general defensive work they had nothing to learn, hotvever.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire teams were successful in every instance where they opposed Yorkshire foemen on Saturday, Oldham, Salford and Leigh displaying excellent form away from home, while Swinton just got the better of Hunslet at Swinton. The tutelage of the Tykes is an expensive matter for them this season.</p>
        <p>Warrington Club members and supporters have been feeling very savage and very sore since the Northern League meeting on Tuesday The worst fears were realised for the League Committee had no option, but to deduct two points from Warrington's score in the Northern League owing to a breach of professional law two by the signing on of J Fairhurst after he had signed for Fletcher Russell's. Swinton were similarly penalised re the Barnett case, and Warrington therefore have Salford and Swinton as companions in misfortune. That does not 'afford much conso!ation, however, nothing could well have been more annoying for not only does it pay Warrington back when they are doing well, but the points arc lost on account of a player Who the Club could very easily have made a present of to the Engi- neers. It is particularly hard on the players who have worked hard to keep the club in the forefront. The only thing left is to make sure and whack Leigh to•day at Leigh. We have had the best of matters the last two :seasons, and if Warrington go determined to get those two points back and play their usual game they will win at Mather—lane, even though Salford did come a crcpper there. There must be none of Saturday's kicking, though. The backs will have to whine game, , and they can only do it by combination. Quick passing and quicker running are called for. Let us have them!</p>
        <p>The case in which Burgess and Wattie Davies are concerned should have been dealt with on Tuesday,but owing to the absence of the referee it was postponed until Friday evening.</p>
        <p>am sorry to hear that the injury sustained by j Faulkner, one of the Runcorn half backs in the Northern League match at Warrington on Saturday, has proved more serious than was anticipated. It appears that the cartilage of the knee has given way, and on Tuesday Mr Royle Garnett, a member of the committee, took Faulkner to Allison's celebrated hospital at Manchester. where he is now an inmate. Faulkner's absence will cause a complete rearrangement of the team.</p>
        <p>A very smart catalogue of his well known sports requisities has been issued by Mr. F. H. Sugg, 32, Lord- street, Liverpool, a speciality of which is the printing in colours of the illustrated designs of football shirts. Mr. Frank Sugg is well known as a cricketer who has played for three counties as well as in the players, and also in all England matches, and as a footballer who has also played for Sheffield United, Burnley, Derby County and Everton League Clubs. His partner Mr. Walter Sugg is an old Sheffield United football player, and is still a member of the Derbyshire Cricket Team. Both have exceptional knewledge of the business in which they are engaged and anything the footballer gets from Frank Sugg's can be relied upon as being the best. Goods are manufactured-on the premises, which is quite a feature in the athletic outfitting. I can recommend Frank Suggs catalogue as being worth the perusal of all sportsmen.</p>
      </div>
